(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch06.json"]={"n": 6, "title": "The Empire of Trebizond (1204–1461)", "events": [{"id": "H4-001", "date": "1185", "date_text": "12 September 1185 and after", "precision": "year", "title": "The fall of Andronikos I; his grandsons taken to Georgia", "summary": "The emperor Andronikos I Komnenos was overthrown and killed in Constantinople in September 1185; his son Manuel was blinded and soon died. Manuel's small sons Alexios (born 1182) and David were taken, by routes the sources do not describe, to Georgia, where they grew up at the court of Queen Tamar. This Georgian upbringing is the root of the Empire of Trebizond.", "people": ["Andronikos I Komnenos", "Manuel Komnenos (son of Andronikos I)", "Alexios Komnenos", "David Komnenos", "Tamar of Georgia"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia (ed. van Dieten 1975), on the fall of Andronikos I", "Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, first entry (ed. Lampsides 1958; trans. Kennedy 2019)"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936, Speculum 11, pp. 3-37", "Toumanoff 1940, Speculum 15, pp. 299-312", "Eastmond 2017, Tamta's World, ch. on Trebizond", "Samouilidis, pp. 18-19 and n. 23; Asp-Talwar, in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 188; Bryer 1980, V p. 148 n. 140", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 18 (the brothers brought up at the court of Tamar)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 48 (supported by Tamar)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "How the boys reached Georgia, and how exactly they were related to Tamar, are unknown. Panaretos calls Tamar Alexios' 'paternal aunt', which Bryer (1980) counts among Panaretos' loose terms of kinship, 'for what is actually first cousin once removed on the father's side'. Fallmerayer, followed by Ioannidis (1870), wrongly made Tamar a daughter of Andronikos I; Toumanoff (1940) argued that the boys' mother was the Georgian princess Rusudan, which would make Tamar their aunt on the mother's side.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "where Andronikos I was overthrown"}, {"name": "Tbilisi (court of the Georgian kings)", "lat": 41.693, "lon": 44.802, "role": "where the princes grew up (approximate: the court moved)"}]}, {"id": "C2b-02", "date": "1204", "date_end": "1461", "date_text": "13th-15th centuries (inherited from the theme of Chaldia; in working order until 1461)", "precision": "century", "title": "The seven banda of the Pontos", "summary": "The empire's heartland was organised in seven valley districts, the banda or 'military parishes' inherited from the theme of Chaldia. From west to east they were (probably) the Philabonites (Harsit valley), Trikomia (round Platana, Akçaabat), Trebizond itself, Matzouka-Palaiomatzouka (Maçka), Gemora (Yomra), Sourmaina (Sürmene) and Rhizaion (Rize). Only the Philabonites was lost entirely to the Turkmen; the other six kept their military structure until 1461 and later became, in turn, Ottoman kazas and modern districts. Their village communities, smallholders and local defence, rather than the trade of the capital, were the strength of the empire: Bryer puts the capital at about 4,000 people after the Black Death, in a 'pocket empire' of 200,000-250,000.", "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle; Acts of Vazelon (13th-15th c.); Ottoman registers of the 1520s (all via Bryer)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 117 (the seven banda)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 121-122 (population; the Matzouka figures: c. 1520, 57 villages, 12,080 Christians and 1,665 Muslims, 88% Christian)", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 182 (Matzouka and Palaiomatzouka as banda in the Gabras records)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 251 (Palaiomatzouka detached from Matzouka as a separate bandon between 1384 and 1408; Matzouka the only bandon without a maritime capital and the only inland area ruled directly); p. 319 (Gemora first appears as a bandon in the Soumela bull of 1364 and was joined with Sourmaina as \"Gemorosyrmia\" before 1432)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer gives the list as 'probably' right; the boundaries are those of the later Ottoman districts, which he argues geography made identical. The population figures are Bryer's estimates (the capital's 4,000 after Tafur), not a medieval count.", "places": [{"name": "Philabonites (Harsit) valley: mouth at Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "bandon 1 (lost to the Chepni)"}, {"name": "Philabonites (Harsit) valley: Ardasa (Torul)", "lat": 40.556, "lon": 39.295, "role": "bandon 1, upper valley"}, {"name": "Trikomia: Platana (Akçaabat)", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 39.571, "role": "bandon 2"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "bandon 3"}, {"name": "Matzouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.613, "role": "bandon 4 (with Palaiomatzouka, Hamsiköy)"}, {"name": "Gemora (Yomra)", "lat": 40.953, "lon": 39.857, "role": "bandon 5"}, {"name": "Sourmaina (Surmene)", "lat": 40.91, "lon": 40.12, "role": "bandon 6"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "bandon 7"}]}, {"id": "H4-002", "date": "1204-04", "date_text": "April 1204", "precision": "month", "title": "Alexios and David Komnenos take Trebizond", "summary": "With Georgian backing from Queen Tamar, their kinswoman (Panaretos: Alexios 'set out from Iberia with the zeal and labour of his paternal aunt Tamar'), the brothers Alexios and David Komnenos came along the coast from Georgia and took Trebizond in April 1204 (indiction 7, AM 6712); Alexios, aged 22, was proclaimed emperor. This happened in the same weeks as the crusaders' capture of Constantinople (12-13 April 1204), and probably before news of it reached the Pontus. How large the Georgian share was, and what Tamar intended, are debated. The state they founded lasted until 1461 and was ruled by their descendants, the Grand Komnenoi, throughout.", "people": ["Alexios I Megas Komnenos", "David Komnenos", "Tamar of Georgia"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, ch. 1 (ed. Lampsides 1958, p. 61): 'εξελθών μεν εκ της ευδαίμονος Κωνσταντινουπόλεως, εκστρατεύσας δε εξ Ιβηρίας σπουδή και μόχθω της προς πατρός θείας αυτού Θάμαρ, και παρέλαβε την Τραπεζούντα μηνί Απριλίω, ινδικτιώνος ζ΄, έτους ςψιβ΄, ετών ων κβ΄' (English in Asp-Talwar, in Eastmond ed. 2016, p. 188)", "Georgian royal chronicle (Kartlis Tskhovreba), Life of Tamar: Tamar 'offered' Trebizond and the Pontos to her kinsman Alexios; the expedition answered the emperor's seizure of her gifts to the Black Mountain and Athos monasteries (KTs II 142, trans. Vivian 86-87, as summarised by Eastmond 2004, pp. 18-21, nn. 7-8, 16)", "Niketas Choniates, Historia: David 'hired a company of the hungry Iberians of the Phasis' ('μοῖραν μισθωσάμενος Ἰβήρων τῶν πεινώντων τοῦ Φάσιδος') together with Paphlagonians and men of Pontic Herakleia", "Michael Panaretos, Chronicle 1", "Georgian Chronicle (Kartlis Tskhovreba)"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936, Speculum 11, pp. 3-37 (p. 19 on the timing)", "Toumanoff 1940", "Kursanskis 1977, REB 35, pp. 237-256", "Karpov 2007, Istoriya Trapezundskoi imperii", "Karpov 2012, Gamer 1, pp. 75f (lead seal; St George's day, 23 April)", "Miller 1926, pp. 14-15", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, pp. 18-21 and nn. 7-8, 15-17", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 48", "Sheard 2021, Byzantium and the Black Sea, c. 1000-1204 (PhD, Royal Holloway), p. 151 and n. 475 (after R. Metreveli, The Golden Age, 154-5)", "Samouilidis (Greek popular history of the Empire of Trebizond), pp. 18-20, 99-100", "Bryer 1980, study V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975), p. 148 n. 140", "Ioannidis 1870, pp. 51-52", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 56", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 18 (the brothers set out before Constantinople fell; the Life of Tamar makes the raid a reprisal against Alexios IV Angelos)", "Vasiliev 1936, Speculum 11, pp. 3–37", "Bryer 1980, Variorum", "Eastmond ed. 2016, Byzantium’s Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 48", "Sheard 2021 (PhD thesis, Royal Holloway), p. 151 (Tamar made the new state a Georgian tributary, after Metreveli)", "Agtzidis 2005, Έλληνες του Πόντου, p. 75 (Tamar sheltered the brothers, who reached Georgia probably in 1203, and 'helped them dominate the Pontus'; stresses the Greek population) [Greek]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Georgian help as such is attested by the Georgian royal chronicle, by Panaretos (Tamar's 'zeal and labour') and indirectly by Choniates (hired Iberians); no source known to us denies it. Its scale and purpose are disputed: a Georgian conquest handed to a client, the new state a Georgian tributary or buffer (the Georgian chronicle; Vasiliev 1936; Sheard 2021, after the Georgian historian Metreveli); a Georgian reprisal raid that became an empire only because Constantinople fell (Eastmond 2004, 2016); Tamar backing a bid for the throne of Constantinople itself (Kuršanskis 1977); or help with mercenaries or money only (Lampsides, as cited by Samouilidis). Chrysanthos (1933) does not mention Tamar and credits the refugee scholarioi and the native military families of the Doranites and Tzanichites, who are attested only from the 14th century; that is his emphasis, not evidence. The date is also disputed: Vasiliev has the brothers move before the fall of Constantinople was known; Karpov (2012), from a seal, suggests about 23 April, St George's day.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "captured and made capital"}, {"name": "Georgia (Tamar's kingdom; army's point of departure, approximate)", "lat": 41.693, "lon": 44.802, "role": "waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Coast of Lazia (Rhizaion, Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "waypoint 2 (probable approach along the coast)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "falls to the Fourth Crusade, 12-13 April 1204"}, {"name": "Trapezous", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "taken by the Komnenoi"}]}, {"id": "H4-003", "date": "1204-05", "date_end": "1205", "date_text": "1204-1205", "precision": "year", "title": "David Komnenos' campaign west to Sinope, Paphlagonia and Herakleia Pontike", "summary": "While Alexios held Trebizond, his brother David led an army west along the coast and took Sinope, Amastris and Paphlagonia as far as Herakleia Pontike. This brought the new state face to face with Theodore Laskaris, who was building his own successor state at Nicaea. For a few years the Grand Komnenoi held almost the whole Black Sea coast of Anatolia.", "people": ["David Komnenos", "Alexios I Megas Komnenos", "Theodore I Laskaris"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia (ed. van Dieten 1975), pp. 626, 639-640", "George Akropolites, History 7 and 11 (trans. Macrides 2007)"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936", "Miller 1926, ch. 1", "Karpov 2007", "Savvides 2009", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 19 (David led an army as far as Pontic Herakleia and made himself ruler of Paphlagonia)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 48", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 71 (\"In 1204/5 the city [Sinope] passed into the hands of Alexios and David Komnenos of Trebizond\")"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The exact order and dates of David's conquests are not recorded; most reconstructions (Vasiliev 1936) place them in 1204-1205. Whether Amisos (Samsun) was held is unclear: a Seljuk Samsun and a Greek Amisos may have stood side by side.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 1 (start)"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "waypoint 2 (probable)"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Unye)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.29, "role": "waypoint 3 (probable)"}, {"name": "Amisos (Samsun)", "lat": 41.29, "lon": 36.33, "role": "waypoint 4 (probable; control uncertain)"}, {"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "waypoint 5 (taken)"}, {"name": "Amastris (Amasra)", "lat": 41.746, "lon": 32.386, "role": "waypoint 6 (taken)"}, {"name": "Herakleia Pontike (Karadeniz Eregli)", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.417, "role": "waypoint 7 (western limit)"}]}, {"id": "H4-004", "date": "1205", "date_end": "1208", "date_text": "C. 1205-1208", "precision": "year", "title": "Clash with Nicaea: Laskaris defeats David's army; David allies with the Latins", "summary": "David pushed into Bithynia, but his general Synadenos was defeated by Theodore Laskaris near Nikomedeia. David then turned to the Latin emperor Henry of Flanders, whose intervention saved Herakleia when Laskaris besieged it. The struggle between the two Greek successor states for the western Pontic coast is the first big theme of the empire's history.", "people": ["David Komnenos", "Theodore I Laskaris", "Synadenos", "Henry of Flanders"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates, Historia (ed. van Dieten 1975), pp. 639-640", "George Akropolites, History 7"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936", "Karpov 2007", "Macrides 2007 (commentary on Akropolites)", "Miller 1926, pp. 16-17", "Bryer 1988-89, Archeion Pontou 42, p. 183", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 19 (Theodore Laskaris whittled away at David's lands)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The sequence and dates (1205, 1206, 1208) are reconstructed from Choniates and Akropolites, who wrote from the Nicaean side and are hostile to David; scholars differ in detail.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Nikomedeia (Izmit)", "lat": 40.765, "lon": 29.94, "role": "Synadenos' defeat (approximate)"}, {"name": "Nicaea (Iznik)", "lat": 40.43, "lon": 29.72, "role": "capital of Theodore Laskaris"}, {"name": "Herakleia Pontike (Karadeniz Eregli)", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.417, "role": "besieged by Laskaris"}]}, {"id": "H4-005", "date": "1205", "date_text": "1204-1206 (greatest extent)", "precision": "year", "title": "The Empire of Trebizond at its greatest extent, 1204–1206", "summary": "A coastal strip from Herakleia Pontike to Makriali in Lazia, about 30-60 km deep, with Chaldia south of the Pontic Alps. Inland limits are approximate: the state was a coastal one and the interior was Seljuk or Danishmendid.", "people": ["Alexios I Megas Komnenos", "David Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Niketas Choniates; George Akropolites (as for the campaign)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985, Introduction and Fig. 1-2", "Vasiliev 1936"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The inland limits are drawn by convention, not surveyed borders; whether Chaldia and Amisos were held in 1204–1206 is uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Herakleia Pontike (Karadeniz Eregli)", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.417, "role": "boundary vertex 1 (coast)"}, {"name": "Amastris (Amasra)", "lat": 41.746, "lon": 32.386, "role": "boundary vertex 2 (coast)"}, {"name": "Cape Karambis (Kerempe Burnu)", "lat": 42.02, "lon": 33.34, "role": "boundary vertex 3 (coast)"}, {"name": "Abonouteichos (Inebolu)", "lat": 41.98, "lon": 33.76, "role": "boundary vertex 4 (coast)"}, {"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "boundary vertex 5 (coast)"}, {"name": "Halys (Kizilirmak) mouth", "lat": 41.74, "lon": 35.96, "role": "boundary vertex 6 (coast)"}, {"name": "Amisos (Samsun)", "lat": 41.29, "lon": 36.33, "role": "boundary vertex 7 (coast; control uncertain)"}, {"name": "Iris (Yesilirmak) mouth", "lat": 41.37, "lon": 36.65, "role": "boundary vertex 8 (coast)"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Unye)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.29, "role": "boundary vertex 9 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.98, "lon": 37.88, "role": "boundary vertex 10 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "boundary vertex 11 (coast)"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "boundary vertex 12 (coast)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "boundary vertex 13 (coast)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "boundary vertex 14 (coast)"}, {"name": "Archabis (Arhavi)", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 41.3, "role": "boundary vertex 15 (coast)"}, {"name": "Makriali (Kemalpasa)", "lat": 41.485, "lon": 41.52, "role": "boundary vertex 16 (coast, eastern limit; approximate)"}, {"name": "Lazian ridge", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 41.55, "role": "boundary vertex 17 (inland)"}, {"name": "Kackar ridge", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 41.1, "role": "boundary vertex 18 (inland)"}, {"name": "Pontic watershed south of Rize", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 40.55, "role": "boundary vertex 19 (inland)"}, {"name": "East of Chaldia, short of Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.4, "lon": 40.05, "role": "boundary vertex 20 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Keltzine (Kelkit)", "lat": 40.05, "lon": 39.45, "role": "boundary vertex 21 (inland)"}, {"name": "West of Cheriana (Siran)", "lat": 40.15, "lon": 38.95, "role": "boundary vertex 22 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Kerasous", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 38.4, "role": "boundary vertex 23 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills behind Kotyora", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 37.85, "role": "boundary vertex 24 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills behind Oinaion", "lat": 40.9, "lon": 37.2, "role": "boundary vertex 25 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of the Iris delta", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 36.6, "role": "boundary vertex 26 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Amisos", "lat": 41.1, "lon": 36.25, "role": "boundary vertex 27 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Sinope (towards Boyabat)", "lat": 41.55, "lon": 35.0, "role": "boundary vertex 28 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Abonouteichos", "lat": 41.75, "lon": 33.7, "role": "boundary vertex 29 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Amastris (Bartin)", "lat": 41.6, "lon": 32.35, "role": "boundary vertex 30 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Herakleia", "lat": 41.15, "lon": 31.5, "role": "boundary vertex 31 (inland)"}]}, {"id": "H4-006", "date": "1212-12-13", "date_text": "13 December 1212", "precision": "day", "title": "David Komnenos dies as a monk on Mount Athos", "summary": "After the western lands were lost or under threat, David Komnenos retired to the monastery of Vatopedi on Mount Athos, where he died as the monk Daniel. Alexios I ruled alone thereafter.", "people": ["David Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["A marginal note giving 13 December 1212 (cited by Bryer, Archeion Pontou 42, 1988-89, p. 184)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1988-89, Archeion Pontou 42, p. 184", "Vasiliev 1936", "Miller 1926", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 19 and n. 11 (marginal note in the Vatopedi psalter, MS Vatopedi 760, fol. 294a, ed. Oikonomides, REB 25, 1967, p. 141 n. 67; facsimile in Chrysanthos 1933, p. 360)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 48"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The day comes from a marginal note (Bryer 1988–89); Eastmond (2004) also takes 1212, while Bryer & Winfield (1985), after Chrysanthos, give 13 December 1213, and Fallmerayer 1214.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Vatopedi monastery, Mount Athos", "lat": 40.313, "lon": 24.211, "role": "where David died"}]}, {"id": "H4-007", "date": "1214", "date_text": "1214", "precision": "year", "title": "Laskaris takes Herakleia and Amastris: the western lands are lost", "summary": "By 1214 Theodore Laskaris had taken Herakleia Pontike and Amastris, and Paphlagonia west of Sinope passed to Nicaea; the Treaty of Nymphaion (1214) with the Latins left him free to do so. Trebizond never again held land west of Sinope.", "people": ["Theodore I Laskaris", "Alexios I Megas Komnenos", "Henry of Flanders"], "sources_primary": ["George Akropolites, History 11 and 15"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936", "Karpov 2007"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Herakleia Pontike (Karadeniz Eregli)", "lat": 41.283, "lon": 31.417, "role": "taken by Nicaea"}, {"name": "Amastris (Amasra)", "lat": 41.746, "lon": 32.386, "role": "taken by Nicaea"}, {"name": "Nymphaion (Kemalpasa near Izmir)", "lat": 38.43, "lon": 27.42, "role": "treaty of 1214 between Nicaea and the Latins"}]}, {"id": "H4-009", "date": "1214-11", "date_text": "After November 1214", "precision": "month", "title": "The empire after 1214", "summary": "After the loss of Sinope and Paphlagonia the empire was a coastal state from the Iris delta (Limnia) to Makriali in Lazia, with Chaldia beyond the Pontic Alps. The Crimean Perateia (Cherson, Gothia, and Sougdaia until the 1220s) still paid tribute.", "people": ["Alexios I Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Ibn Bibi; John Lazaropoulos, Synopsis (for the Cherson tribute)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985", "Vasiliev 1936", "Karpov 2007", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 172 (part of the Crimea a dependent of Trebizond from 1204 until after 1223)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The western limit (Iris or Thermodon) and the depth of control in Chaldia are uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Iris (Yesilirmak) mouth", "lat": 41.37, "lon": 36.65, "role": "boundary vertex 1 (coast, western limit)"}, {"name": "Thermodon (Terme) mouth", "lat": 41.21, "lon": 36.97, "role": "boundary vertex 2 (coast)"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Unye)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.29, "role": "boundary vertex 3 (coast)"}, {"name": "Polemonion (Fatsa/Bolaman)", "lat": 41.03, "lon": 37.5, "role": "boundary vertex 4 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.98, "lon": 37.88, "role": "boundary vertex 5 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "boundary vertex 6 (coast)"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "boundary vertex 7 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kordyle (Akcakale)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 39.48, "role": "boundary vertex 8 (coast)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "boundary vertex 9 (coast)"}, {"name": "Sourmaina (Surmene)", "lat": 40.91, "lon": 40.12, "role": "boundary vertex 10 (coast)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "boundary vertex 11 (coast)"}, {"name": "Athina (Pazar)", "lat": 41.18, "lon": 40.88, "role": "boundary vertex 12 (coast)"}, {"name": "Archabis (Arhavi)", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 41.3, "role": "boundary vertex 13 (coast)"}, {"name": "Makriali (Kemalpasa)", "lat": 41.485, "lon": 41.52, "role": "boundary vertex 14 (coast, eastern limit; approximate)"}, {"name": "Lazian ridge", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 41.55, "role": "boundary vertex 15 (inland)"}, {"name": "Kackar ridge", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 41.1, "role": "boundary vertex 16 (inland)"}, {"name": "Pontic watershed south of Rize", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 40.55, "role": "boundary vertex 17 (inland)"}, {"name": "East of Chaldia, short of Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.4, "lon": 40.05, "role": "boundary vertex 18 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Keltzine (Kelkit)", "lat": 40.05, "lon": 39.45, "role": "boundary vertex 19 (inland)"}, {"name": "West of Cheriana (Siran)", "lat": 40.15, "lon": 38.95, "role": "boundary vertex 20 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Kerasous", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 38.4, "role": "boundary vertex 21 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills behind Kotyora", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 37.85, "role": "boundary vertex 22 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills behind Oinaion", "lat": 40.9, "lon": 37.2, "role": "boundary vertex 23 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of the Iris delta (Limnia)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 36.6, "role": "boundary vertex 24 (inland)"}, {"name": "Cherson (Chersonesos, Sevastopol)", "lat": 44.611, "lon": 33.493, "role": "Perateia: tributary town (separate marker)"}, {"name": "Gothia: Doros/Theodoro (Mangup)", "lat": 44.59, "lon": 33.81, "role": "Perateia: Gothia (separate marker)"}, {"name": "Sougdaia (Sudak)", "lat": 44.84, "lon": 34.97, "role": "Perateia: until the Seljuk expedition of the 1220s (separate marker)"}]}, {"id": "H4-008", "date": "1214-11-01", "date_text": "1 November 1214", "precision": "day", "title": "Sultan Kaykaus I takes Sinope; Alexios I captured", "summary": "The Seljuk sultan Izz al-Din Kaykaus I besieged Sinope. Alexios I, caught outside the city, was taken prisoner and shown, after torture, to the defenders, who surrendered on terms on 1 November 1214. Alexios was released as a tributary vassal of the sultan, and the Seljuks gained their first port on the Black Sea; their building inscriptions of 1215 still stand on the walls of Sinop. The price of release was tribute and vassalage; the yearly tribute is given as 10,000 dinars, 500 horses, 200 cows, 10,000 sheep and 50 loads of gifts (Ibn Bibi, as given by Eastmond) or as 12,000 gold pieces, 500 horses, 2,000 cattle, 10,000 sheep and 50 bales (Ibn Bibi, as given by Bryer & Winfield). A bilingual Arabic and Greek inscription on the walls records the Seljuk rebuilding of 1215.", "people": ["Kaykaus I", "Alexios I Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Ibn Bibi, al-Awamir al-Ala'iyya (trans. Duda 1959), on the Sinope campaign", "Seljuk building inscriptions on the citadel walls of Sinop, 1215"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936", "Cahen 1968, Pre-Ottoman Turkey", "Shukurov 2001", "Karpov 2007", "Vasiliev 1936, pp. 27f", "Kuršanskis 1988, p. 113", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 123 (1 November 1214; bilingual inscription; architect 'Sebastos', governor Hetum)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 19-21 and nn. 13, 19-20 (tribute; Ibn al-Athir has Alexios paying tribute to Kaykhusraw I as early as 1205/6)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 49", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 71-72 (Alexios and the Sultan rode into Sinope on Sunday 1 November 1214, a date given by an inscription on the walls and by Ibn Bibi; tribute of 12,000 gold pieces, 500 horses, 2,000 cattle, 10,000 sheep and 50 bales; the bilingual inscription on tower 38 records Bedreddin son of Abu Bakir completing the walls in April-September 1215, with Sebastos, \"presumably a Greek\", as architect; the Armenian Rais Hetum a later governor)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The day is Ibn Bibi's. Some scholars think Sinope was held by a local lord allied to Alexios rather than by Trebizond itself. The inscription names Bedreddin son of Abu Bakir and the architect Sebastos (Bryer; Bryer & Winfield 1985); Kuruoğlu, cited by Bryer, gives the builder as Abu Ali ibn Abi'r-Rakka of Aleppo.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "besieged and surrendered"}]}, {"id": "H4-011", "date": "1222", "date_end": "1227", "date_text": "1220s: 1221 (Togan), 1225 (Kayhan 2019, after Ibn Bibi) or 1227 (Turan)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Seljuk expedition to Sougdaia in the Crimea", "summary": "Using Sinope as a naval base, the Seljuks sent an expedition across the Black Sea and took Sougdaia (Sudak) in the Crimea for a time. It cut into the Perateia, the 'land across the sea' from which Trebizond drew tribute.", "people": ["Kayqubad I", "Husam al-Din Choban"], "sources_primary": ["Ibn Bibi, el-Evāmirü'l-'Alā'iyye (Turkish tr. M. Öztürk 1996), pp. 315–345, as cited by Kayhan 2019"], "sources_modern": ["Cahen 1968", "Shukurov 2001", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 173 (Sinope 'an excellent base for raiding the Crimea and its shipping', as the Seljuks found in the 13th century)", "Kayhan, H. 2019, 'Selçuklular devrinde Kırım'ın sosyal, kültürel, ekonomik ve dinî gelişimine bir bakış', Karadeniz Araştırmaları Enstitüsü Dergisi 5 (7): 85–98 (Sudak taken in 1225; notes Togan 1981, p. 203: 1221, and Turan 1984, p. 357: 1227) [Turkish]", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 72 (Sinope was the port of Alaeddin Keykubad's short-lived attacks on the Crimea in 1219 and 1225)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The expedition is dated 1221 (Togan), 1225 (Kayhan 2019, after Ibn Bibi) or 1227 (Turan), all as reported by Kayhan; Bryer & Winfield (1985) give two Seljuk attacks on the Crimea from Sinope, in 1219 and 1225.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "naval base"}, {"name": "Sougdaia (Sudak)", "lat": 44.84, "lon": 34.97, "role": "taken by the Seljuks"}]}, {"id": "H4-010", "date": "1222-09", "date_end": "1223-08", "date_text": "AM 6731 (1 September 1222 - 31 August 1223), 'in the second year' of Andronikos Gidos (Panaretos; Lazaropoulos); written 1222/23 or 1223", "precision": "month", "title": "Seljuk siege of Trebizond and the 'miracle of St Eugenios'", "summary": "Andronikos I Gidos succeeded his father-in-law Alexios I, who died on 20 February 1222, the Sunday of Orthodoxy (Panaretos). In AM 6731 (September 1222 - August 1223), the second year of his reign, a treaty with the 'sultan Melik' was broken when the Melik's vassal Etoumes (Het'um), rais of Sinope, seized a ship that had run ashore there with the yearly revenue of Cherson and Gothia; after a Trapezuntine raid on the Sinope coast the Melik marched by Erzurum, Keltzene and Paipert and besieged Trebizond. The siege failed, the Melik was captured and released, and the city credited its patron saint, the martyr Eugenios. John Zanzes, who fell in a sortie, was buried in a monastery to which his widow gave an estate at Ammodion: St Eugenios in Lazaropoulos's text (as Rosenqvist reads it), the Chrysokephalos in Chrysanthos (p. 393). Chrysanthos adds that the empire then reached only from Soteriopolis (Sukhumi) to Oinaion.", "people": ["Andronikos I Gidos", "Melik (Seljuk prince; identity debated)", "St Eugenios"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle (ed. Lampsides 1958, 61.8-10): 'Ἐν δὲ τῷ ϛψλα΄ ἔτει, τῷ δευτέρῳ χρόνῳ τῆς τοῦ Γίδωνος βασιλείας, ἦλθεν ὁ Μελὶκ σουλτὰν κατὰ τῆς Τραπεζοῦντος' (trans. Asp-Talwar, in Eastmond ed. 2016, p. 188); Alexios I's death: 'εκοιμήθη Φεβρουαρίου κ΄ [20], ημέρα α΄ της Ορθοδοξίας, έτους ςψλ΄' (as quoted, in monotonic, by Samouilidis, n. 15)", "Joseph Lazaropoulos, Synopsis of the miracles of St Eugenios, Miracle 23, lines 1152-1154 ('in the second year of the reign of... Andronikos Gidos, in the year 6731 from the creation of the world') and 1310-1313 (Zanzes), ed. and trans. Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 310-311, 318-319"], "sources_modern": ["Rosenqvist 1996", "Shukurov 2001", "Bryer 1980 (Variorum)", "Miller 1926, pp. 19-21", "Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, pp. 50-58 and p. 434 (commentary on line 1154: identity of the Melik)", "Kunik 1854 ('the Trapezuntine-Seljuk war of 1223', via Rosenqvist 1996, p. 103 n. 37)", "Eastmond 2004, p. 20 ('ended in 1223'); Eastmond 2016, p. 53 ('in 1223')", "Samouilidis (20 February 1222, 'according to others 1 February'); 1223 elsewhere in his book", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 57; p. 393", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 123 (Mughith al-Din Tughrilshah of Erzurum may afterwards have become a vassal of Gidos and endowed a monastery in Trebizond)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 21 (Seljuk vassalship ended in 1223)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 72 (in 1222 Alexios Paktiares, archon of the Perateia, was driven into Sinope with the tribute ship; Hetum seized it; Andronikos sent a fleet to Karousa)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 219 (the Melik's third attack on Trebizond in 1223, launched from St Barbara in the west to St Constantine in the east; fig. 41)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 223 (the Melik camped close to St Eugenios for his second assault in 1223; John Zanxis, a hero of the siege, was buried in the monastery of St Eugenios and his widow gave it a stasis in the chorion of Ammodion)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Both medieval sources give AM 6731, September 1222 to August 1223; 1223 is the more likely calendar year if Gidos' regnal years are counted from 20 February 1222, but that cannot be proved. Chrysanthos, Kunik, Samouilidis and Eastmond (2016) write 1223; Rosenqvist and Eastmond (2004) 1222/23. The 'Melik' is a title, not a name, and has been identified as Kayqubad I (Fallmerayer; Savvides; Chrysanthos), as Mughith al-Din Tughrilshah of Erzurum (Cahen; Bryer) or as a son of Kayqubad (Janssens). Cahen and Kuršanskis tied the ship from Cherson to refugees from the Mongol raid on the Crimea of 1223. Lazaropoulos wrote more than a century later, as hagiography; only the date has an independent witness (Panaretos). Bryer & Winfield (1985) read Lazaropoulos as Rosenqvist does, with Zanzes buried at St Eugenios, and tentatively place Ammodion at Bulancak.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "besieged"}, {"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "where the tribute ship was seized"}, {"name": "Cherson (Chersonesos, Sevastopol)", "lat": 44.611, "lon": 33.493, "role": "source of the tribute"}]}, {"id": "H4-012", "date": "1230-08", "date_text": "August 1230", "precision": "month", "title": "Battle of Yassicemen: Trebizond on the losing side", "summary": "Andronikos I Gidos' troops fought alongside the Khwarazmshah Jalal al-Din against the Seljuk sultan Kayqubad I near Erzincan. The Khwarazmian defeat left Trebizond once again a Seljuk tributary.", "people": ["Andronikos I Gidos", "Jalal al-Din Mangburni", "Kayqubad I"], "sources_primary": ["Ibn Bibi (trans. Duda 1959)"], "sources_modern": ["Cahen 1968", "Shukurov 2001", "Karpov 2007", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 21 and n. 23 (1230/31; the routed troops fled to Trebizond, Bar Hebraeus p. 395; tribute afterwards of 200 lances, Simon of Saint-Quentin via Vincent of Beauvais)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 61-62 (the Yassı Çimen was the scene of the three-day battle of Ramadan 1230 between Kaykubad and Celal al-Din; Bar Hebraeus: many fled to Trebizond; Bryer & Winfield place the Yassı Çimen on the north side of the Çimen Dağı along the Balahu Dere, just west of Balahor, beside Satala)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The size and part of the Trapezuntine contingent are unclear. Eastmond (2004) dates the battle 1230/31 and places the Khwarazmian rout at Ahlat rather than near Erzincan; he infers the alliance from the flight of the routed troops to Trebizond, and has Andronikos pay a new tribute of 200 lances.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Yassı Çimen (Bryer & Winfield: north side of the Çimen Dağı along the Balahu Dere, just west of Balahor and Sipanazat; mapped at Aşağı Özlüce, old Sipanazat, Kelkit)", "lat": 39.9948, "lon": 39.5438, "role": "battlefield of 1230 as placed by Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 62 (their proposal); approximate, a few km east of the site"}]}, {"id": "H4-013", "date": "1238", "date_end": "1263", "date_text": "1238-1263 (reign of Manuel I)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Hagia Sophia of Trebizond built under Manuel I", "summary": "Manuel I, 'the most fortunate', built the monastery church of Hagia Sophia on a headland west of the walled city. Its sculpted Genesis frieze, single-headed Komnenian eagle and star-shaped (muqarnas) niches mix Constantinopolitan, Georgian, Armenian and Seljuk art, and its frescoes, probably painted by one workshop in under three years, are among the finest of the 13th century. Building and decoration were probably finished in the mid-1250s. Manuel's donor portrait, still seen in the 19th century, called him 'faithful emperor and autocrat of the Romans'. It was partly a mosque by 1701, was restored as a mosque in the 1880s (when the paintings were plastered over), was conserved by David Winfield's team for the Russell Trust in 1957-1962 and then opened as a museum, and became a mosque again in 2013. Chrysanthos (1933, p. 426 ff.) cites the founder's inscription copied by Finlay in 1850 ('κτήτωρ τῆς ἁγίας μονῆς ταύτης Μανουὴλ ὁ Κομνηνός'), calls the church the monastery of Christ (feast 6 August), and dates its conversion to a mosque between 1578 and 1609.", "people": ["Manuel I Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["The building, its reliefs, frescoes and graffiti (earliest dated graffiti 1291 and 1293)"], "sources_modern": ["Talbot Rice (ed.) 1968, The Church of Haghia Sophia at Trebizond", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium: Hagia Sophia and the Empire of Trebizond", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 231-233 (Finlay's 1850 facsimile of the founder's inscription naming Manuel Komnenos; a graffito of 1291 shows he was Manuel I (1238-63); the earlier paintings \"consistent with the years 1250-70\"; graffiti record restorations of the church and dome in August 1486 and October 1547; its patronal feast the Transfiguration)", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 426 ff.; p. 221", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 15 (completed probably in the mid-1250s)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 18 (donor portrait and title)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 82 (Tamara Talbot Rice dated it to the 1250s, after Kose Dag)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 126 (one workshop, under three years, probably early 1250s, after Winfield)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 153-154 (conclusion)", "Eastmond 2016, 'Hagia Sophia at Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 92 ('probably in the 1250s, but possibly up to twenty years earlier')", "Eastmond 2016, 'Hagia Sophia at Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 59 (Tournefort 1701; Fallmerayer 1840; restored as a mosque in the 1880s)", "Eastmond 2016, 'Introduction', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 25 (conservation 1957-1962, then a museum)", "Eastmond 2016, 'Introduction', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 24 (church, mosque, ruin, stable, storehouse, cholera hospital, museum, mosque again)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 51 (reopened as Ayasofya Camii in 2013)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 124 n. 31"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The building was probably finished in the mid-1250s (Eastmond 2004), the paintings in the early 1250s (Winfield); Eastmond (2016) allows 'possibly up to twenty years earlier', and rejects a longer painting campaign of over a decade (Talbot Rice, Restle). The conversion to a mosque is put soon after 1461, in 1584, or, by Chrysanthos, between 1578 and 1609; graffiti on the dome recording restorations of the church in August 1486 and October 1547 (Bryer & Winfield 1985) tell against a conversion soon after 1461, and Tournefort found it partly a mosque in 1701. On what the mixed decoration means: Tamara Talbot Rice (1963, British) saw Seljuk-style panels made by Seljuk refugee craftsmen after Köse Dağ, an image of Komnenian triumph over the Turks; Bryer (1975, British) found the refugee thesis 'hardly convincing' and the interchange Armenian-Seljuk rather than Greek; Eastmond (2004, British) reads it as a Roman imperial church that adapted Constantinople to local audiences, in which 'Anatolian motifs only decorated a Byzantine body' (see also H4-041).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Hagia Sophia, Trebizond", "lat": 41.003, "lon": 39.697, "role": "church"}]}, {"id": "H4-014", "date": "1238", "date_end": "1242", "date_text": "1238-1242", "precision": "year", "title": "The Mongols overrun the Crimea: the Perateia lost in practice", "summary": "The Mongol campaigns in the western steppe (1238-1242) brought the Crimea under the Golden Horde. Trebizond's revenue from the Perateia did not survive this for long, though the claim stayed in the imperial title until 1461 and the Crimean principality of Theodoro (Gothia) kept close ties with Trebizond.", "people": ["Batu"], "sources_primary": ["William of Rubruck, Itinerarium 1 (1253): at Soldaia 'the captains had gone with the tribute to Baatu'; the Comans fled into the Crimea 'upon the coming of the Tartars' (Kerr, General History and Collection of Voyages, vol. 1, ch. 9, sect. 1, 1811 English version)"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936b, The Goths in the Crimea", "Karpov 2007", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 172 (Crimean dependency until after 1223; the tribute ship seized and the first Tatar attack in 1223; Genoese colonies from 1274; the Greek principality of Theodoro from the mid-14th century)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "When tribute from Cherson and Gothia actually stopped is unknown.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Cherson (Chersonesos, Sevastopol)", "lat": 44.611, "lon": 33.493, "role": "Perateia"}, {"name": "Gothia: Doros/Theodoro (Mangup)", "lat": 44.59, "lon": 33.81, "role": "Perateia"}, {"name": "Sougdaia (Sudak)", "lat": 44.84, "lon": 34.97, "role": "Perateia"}]}, {"id": "H4-015", "date": "1243-06-26", "date_text": "26 June 1243", "precision": "day", "title": "Kose Dag: Trebizond passes under Mongol overlordship", "summary": "The Mongols under Baiju crushed the Seljuk army at Kose Dag. Trebizond, a Seljuk tributary, then accepted the overlordship of the Mongols (from 1256 the Ilkhans of Persia), which lasted until the Ilkhanate broke up in 1335. William of Rubruck, crossing the Black Sea in 1253, noted that Trebizond 'has its own prince, named Guido, who, although of the imperial race of Constantinople, is under the Tartar dominion' (Kerr's English version).", "people": ["Baiju", "Kaykhusraw II", "Manuel I Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Ibn Bibi (trans. Duda 1959)", "Bar Hebraeus, Chronography", "William of Rubruck, Itinerarium 1 (Kerr, General History and Collection of Voyages, vol. 1, ch. 9, sect. 1, 1811)"], "sources_modern": ["Cahen 1968", "Shukurov 2001", "Karpov 2007", "Bryer 1980", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 22 (after Kose Dag Manuel's territory was left untouched in return for tribute)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Whether a Trapezuntine contingent fought at Kose Dag on the Seljuk side is reported in some sources and doubted by others.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kose Dag battlefield, between Sivas and Erzincan (approximate)", "lat": 40.07, "lon": 38.2, "role": "battlefield (approximate)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "becomes a Mongol tributary"}]}, {"id": "C2b-01", "date": "1246", "date_text": "1246 (kuriltai of Güyük, summer 1246)", "precision": "year", "title": "Manuel I at the Mongol kuriltai at Karakorum", "summary": "After Kose Dag the Mongols left Manuel I's land untouched in return for tribute. In 1246 he travelled to the kuriltai that enthroned the Great Khan Güyük at Karakorum, as the equal of the sultan of Rum, the two kings of Georgia, the sultan of Erzurum and the emir of Aleppo, and received a yarligh (decree) confirming his rule. The new Mongol order also moved the caravan trade north, away from Baghdad and Syria, towards Tabriz and Trebizond.", "people": ["Manuel I Megas Komnenos", "Güyük Khan"], "sources_primary": ["'Ala al-Din Ata-Malik Juvaini, History of the World-Conqueror, trans. Boyle 1958, I, 257 (as cited by Eastmond 2004, n. 29)"], "sources_modern": ["Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 22 and n. 29", "Bryer 1994, 'The Grand Komnenos and the Great Khan at Karakorum in 1246', in Itinéraires d'Orient (Hommages à Claude Cahen), pp. 257-261 (cited by Eastmond)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Juvaini's list of rulers at the kuriltai is read as including the ruler of Trebizond by Bryer (1994), followed by Eastmond (2004, p. 22); whether Manuel went in person or sent an envoy depends on that reading.", "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Manuel's capital"}, {"name": "Karakorum", "lat": 47.197, "lon": 102.824, "role": "seat of the kuriltai of 1246"}]}, {"id": "H4-016", "date": "1253", "date_end": "1265", "date_text": "C. 1253-1265 (recapture dated 1253 to the late 1250s; lost 1265)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Manuel I recovers Sinope; lost again in 1265", "summary": "Exploiting Seljuk weakness under the Mongols, Manuel I took Sinope back in the 1250s and installed a governor of the Gabras family; the Seljuk regent, the Pervane Mu'in al-Din Suleyman, retook it in 1265, this time for good. Manuel's reign also saw the first Trapezuntine silver aspers, coins with the emperor and St Eugenios that were copied in Georgia as the standard currency (the 'kyrmanueli').", "people": ["Manuel I Megas Komnenos", "Mu'in al-Din Suleyman Pervane"], "sources_primary": ["Ibn Bibi (trans. Duda 1959)", "Coins: silver aspers of Manuel I"], "sources_modern": ["Shukurov 2001", "Karpov 2007", "Bendall (on the aspers)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 22 and n. 35 (1253; Ibn Bibi p. 286; Nystazopoulou, REB 22, 1964, pp. 241-249)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 22 and n. 33 (the kyrmanueli)", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 173 (Gabras archon of Sinope 'during its brief recapture by... Manuel I in the late 1250s and early 1260s')", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 123 (a Gabras installed as governor when the Trapezuntines 'retook Sinope for the last time in the 1260's')", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 72 (Manuel I recaptured Sinope \"in 1254 (or 1258, or 1259)\" and installed a Gabras archon, killed when the pervane retook it \"in summer 1265 (or 1267 or 1268)\"; the pervane's medrese inscription reads A.H. 666 = 1267-68; a final unsuccessful attempt about 1280)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The date, and even the fact, of the recovery are questioned: Bryer & Winfield (1985) give 1254, 1258 or 1259, and the loss as summer 1265, 1267 or 1268, reading the Pervane's inscription at Sinope as 1267/8. They also record a last, unsuccessful Trapezuntine attempt on Sinope about 1280.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "recovered c. 1254, lost 1265"}]}, {"id": "H4-017", "date": "1258", "date_text": "1258 onwards", "precision": "decade", "title": "Tabriz becomes the Mongol capital: the caravan route to Trebizond", "summary": "After Hulegu sacked Baghdad (February 1258), Tabriz became the commercial capital of Mongol Iran, and its silk, spices and dyes came to Europe by caravan over Erzurum and the Zigana pass to Trebizond. Trebizond's customs duties made it rich, and Italian merchants settled there from the 1280s. Pegolotti's merchant manual (c. 1340) notes that the weights and measures of Tabriz were the same as Trebizond's; its list of toll stations is for the road from Ayas (Laiazzo) in Cilicia to Tabriz, not from Trebizond.", "people": ["Hulegu"], "sources_primary": ["Francesco Balducci Pegolotti, La pratica della mercatura (c. 1340; ed. Evans 1936), stages Trebizond-Tabriz, pp. 28-29", "Marco Polo, Devisement du monde, on Tauris"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 1981, L'impero di Trebisonda, Venezia, Genova e Roma", "Bryer 1980", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 25 (Pegolotti's route runs from Ayas via Kayseri, Sivas, Erzincan and Erzurum to Tabriz); p. 100 n. 55 (caravans took 32 days over the 954 km between Trebizond and Tabriz)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 118 and n. 12 (the Pontic Gates open from the 1250s to the early 15th century; the route's medieval wealth 'overestimated')", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 370-371 (Central Asian traffic slight compared with local Black Sea trade; imperial revenue 'overwhelmingly from land, rather than commerce'; kommerkion about 6%)", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 339 n. 36 (Pegolotti, ed. Evans, pp. 26-31, 406)", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 22-23 and nn. 30, 37 (trade shift to Tabriz after 1258; Pegolotti p. 29: 'the weights and measures of Tabriz are as one with those of Trebizond')", "Mercier 1994, An Almanac for Trebizond for the Year 1336, p. 23 n. 4 (factors after Karpov: Baghdad 1258, Acre 1291, the papal ban on trade with Egypt)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "On the weight of the Tabriz trade: Heyd (1886, German) and Bratianu (1929, Romanian), from the Italian commercial records, held that the transit trade far outweighed local products, making Trebizond the 'vestibule of Central Asia'; Bryer (1967–1979, British), from Venetian and Genoese notarial records, monastic charters and Ottoman registers, holds that the Central Asian traffic was slight beside the local Black Sea trade, that the emperors lived from land rather than commerce, and that the route's real heyday was 1829–1869; Eastmond (2004, British) and Karpov (1986, Russian) see the Mongol shift of the caravan routes to Tabriz as the source of Trebizond's prosperity, with its silver mines. Pegolotti's list of toll stations is for the road from Ayas to Tabriz, not from Trebizond; Clavijo (1404) travelled the road from Trebizond by Erzurum to Tabriz.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tabriz", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.29, "role": "waypoint 1 (caravan start)"}, {"name": "Khoy", "lat": 38.55, "lon": 44.95, "role": "waypoint 2 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Bayazid (Dogubayazit)", "lat": 39.55, "lon": 44.08, "role": "waypoint 3 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Erzurum (Theodosioupolis)", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.27, "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.227, "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 7 (port)"}, {"name": "Baghdad", "lat": 33.315, "lon": 44.366, "role": "sacked by Hulegu, 1258"}]}, {"id": "C2-021", "date": "1260-01-01", "date_text": "1 January 1260 (AM 6768); Chrysanthos prints 'indiction 6'", "precision": "day", "title": "Patriarchal act: the metropolitan of Trebizond to be elected at home", "summary": "At the request of Manuel I, a synod of 26 bishops under Patriarch Nikephoros II at Kallipolis issued an act allowing the metropolitan of Trebizond to be elected and consecrated in Trebizond in the presence of a patriarchal envoy. The patriarchate agreed, Chrysanthos quotes, looking to 'the gain from it, that is, the union of the Romans' ('τὴν τῶν Ῥωμαίων δηλονότι ἕνωσιν'), i.e. good relations with Nicaea. The church of Trebizond never became independent ('autocephalous').", "people": ["Nikephoros II of Constantinople", "Manuel I Megas Komnenos", "Michael VIII Palaiologos"], "sources_primary": ["Synodal act of 1 January 1260 (eds Triantaphyllides 1870; L. Petit; Siderides)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 175–178"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "1 January 1260 falls in the 3rd indiction; Chrysanthos prints the 6th.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kallipolis (Gelibolu)", "lat": 40.41, "lon": 26.67, "role": "synod"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "metropolis"}]}, {"id": "H4-018", "date": "1277", "date_text": "C. 1277", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Chepni Turkmen appear near Sinope", "summary": "The Chepni, a Turkmen tribe found over much of central Anatolia, were 'causing trouble at Samsun in 1277', in the year the Pervane, the Seljuk regent who had held Sinope, was put to death by the Ilkhan; groups of them had been working east along the summer pastures from Sinope since the early 13th century. In the 14th century they took over the Philabonites (Harsit) valley, the only one of the central Trapezuntine valleys to lose its Greek settlement almost entirely.", "people": ["Chepni (tribe)"], "sources_primary": ["Ibn Bibi, trans. Duda 1959, p. 321 (as cited by Bryer 1980, V 132 n. 69)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975, DOP 29, pp. 113-148", "Shukurov 2016", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 125 and n. 34 (the Chepni active in the Pervane's old lands in Samsun and Sinope in the year of his death, after Sümer, Oğuzlar, p. 327)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 132-133 and nn. 69-70 (Ibn Bibi, trans. Duda, p. 321; the Philabonites valley; a migration of 600 km in 700 years)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 172-173 (Çepni on the Cheriana-Chaldia route in the 14th century and in 1404; still reported in the pastures between Akbulak and Soruyana); p. 130 (the castle of Kerasous garrisoned with Çepni troops after the Ottoman conquest)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Whether the Turkmen of Limnia and Chalybia were Chepni Bryer answers 'perhaps', noting that 'Panaretos does not say so'; he also warns that the Chepni are often confused with the vanished Tzannoi. The first mention is Ibn Bibi's, as cited by Bryer, whose guide to the Turkish sources is Faruk Sümer's Oğuzlar.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "first attestation (approximate)"}, {"name": "Philabonites (Harsit) valley, later the Chepni nahiye (approximate: hills behind Kerasous and Tripolis)", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 38.2, "role": "area of later settlement (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H4-020", "date": "1280", "date_text": "C. 1280", "precision": "decade", "title": "The empire about 1280", "summary": "Sinope is lost for good (1265), and the Crimean Perateia is a claim rather than a possession. The empire holds the coast from Limnia (Iris delta) to Makriali, with Chaldia and Cheriana behind the mountains, under Mongol overlordship.", "people": ["John II Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Panaretos; Ibn Bibi"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985", "Bryer 1975", "Karpov 2007"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The western hinterland was already being settled by Turkmen; the map shows the rule claimed along the coast.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Iris (Yesilirmak) mouth", "lat": 41.37, "lon": 36.65, "role": "boundary vertex 1 (coast, western limit)"}, {"name": "Thermodon (Terme) mouth", "lat": 41.21, "lon": 36.97, "role": "boundary vertex 2 (coast)"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Unye)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.29, "role": "boundary vertex 3 (coast)"}, {"name": "Polemonion (Fatsa/Bolaman)", "lat": 41.03, "lon": 37.5, "role": "boundary vertex 4 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.98, "lon": 37.88, "role": "boundary vertex 5 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "boundary vertex 6 (coast)"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "boundary vertex 7 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kordyle (Akcakale)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 39.48, "role": "boundary vertex 8 (coast)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "boundary vertex 9 (coast)"}, {"name": "Sourmaina (Surmene)", "lat": 40.91, "lon": 40.12, "role": "boundary vertex 10 (coast)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "boundary vertex 11 (coast)"}, {"name": "Athina (Pazar)", "lat": 41.18, "lon": 40.88, "role": "boundary vertex 12 (coast)"}, {"name": "Archabis (Arhavi)", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 41.3, "role": "boundary vertex 13 (coast)"}, {"name": "Makriali (Kemalpasa)", "lat": 41.485, "lon": 41.52, "role": "boundary vertex 14 (coast, eastern limit; approximate)"}, {"name": "Lazian ridge", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 41.55, "role": "boundary vertex 15 (inland)"}, {"name": "Kackar ridge", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 41.1, "role": "boundary vertex 16 (inland)"}, {"name": "Pontic watershed south of Rize", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 40.55, "role": "boundary vertex 17 (inland)"}, {"name": "East of Chaldia, short of Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.4, "lon": 40.05, "role": "boundary vertex 18 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Keltzine (Kelkit)", "lat": 40.05, "lon": 39.45, "role": "boundary vertex 19 (inland)"}, {"name": "West of Cheriana (Siran)", "lat": 40.15, "lon": 38.95, "role": "boundary vertex 20 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Kerasous", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 38.4, "role": "boundary vertex 21 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills behind Kotyora (Chepni settlement beginning)", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 37.85, "role": "boundary vertex 22 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills behind Oinaion", "lat": 40.9, "lon": 37.2, "role": "boundary vertex 23 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of the Iris delta (Limnia)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 36.6, "role": "boundary vertex 24 (inland)"}]}, {"id": "H4-019", "date": "1280-06", "date_text": "June 1280", "precision": "month", "title": "Emperor George betrayed; John II takes the throne", "summary": "George, a son of Manuel I, 'was betrayed through a plot by the archontes at the mountain of Taurezion and captured in June' 1280 (Panaretos); his brother John II then received the insignia. Bryer identifies Taurezion with Tabriz: after the Mongol defeat at Elbistan (1277) the archons escorted George to submit to the Ilkhan Abaga and abandoned him near the Ilkhan's capital. The episode shows how the archons of the Pontus, and the Mongol overlord, could make and unmake emperors.", "people": ["George Megas Komnenos", "John II Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 1280"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Karpov 2007", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 332-350 (pp. 332, 338-339, 349: Taurezion = Tabriz; betrayed to Abaga in June 1280)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 125", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 189 (and n. 74)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Where 'Taurezion' was is debated: the Tauros (Lampsides and older historians), which would imply a much larger empire; a place nearer Trebizond, on an expedition against the Turkmen (Chrysanthos; Ioannidis, a mountain near Soumela); or Tabriz, whose medieval forms fit the name (Bryer 1973, followed by Asp-Talwar 2016). Bryer's reading makes George a victim of the Ilkhan's vengeance after Elbistan, like the Pervane in the same years.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}, {"name": "Tabriz (Bryer's identification of Panaretos' 'mountain of Taurezion'; disputed)", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.29, "role": "where George may have been handed to the Ilkhan"}]}, {"id": "H4-021", "date": "1282", "date_text": "1282", "precision": "year", "title": "John II marries Eudokia Palaiologina; the new imperial title", "summary": "John II went to Constantinople and married Eudokia, daughter of Michael VIII Palaiologos, while Michael was still alive (Panaretos); according to Pachymeres he agreed to take only the lesser title of despot and to exchange the emperor's red shoes for black ones. Manuel I had styled himself 'faithful emperor and autocrat of the Romans' in his donor portrait at Hagia Sophia; from John II the Grand Komnenoi styled themselves 'faithful emperor and autocrat of all the East, the Iberians and the Perateia', a formula seen in the signature of Alexios III's chrysobull for Dionysiou (1374). In April 1283, in John's absence, King David VI Narin of Imereti besieged Trebizond without taking it; John came back with the pregnant Eudokia on 25 April 1283. Chrysanthos (1933, p. 56) likewise has the rulers styled 'emperor and autocrat of the Romans' until the end of the 13th century, and 'of all the East, of the Iberians and of Perateia' from John II; their eagle was single-headed.", "people": ["John II Megas Komnenos", "Eudokia Palaiologina", "Michael VIII Palaiologos", "David VI Narin of Imereti"], "sources_primary": ["George Pachymeres, Relations historiques VI.34 (ed. Failler) (cited as VI.34 by Eastmond 2004, n. 14, and 2016, p. 49 n. 14)", "Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1282", "Chrysobull of Alexios III for Dionysiou, 1374 (Oikonomides, Actes de Dionysiou, no. 4) for the full title"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Karpov 2007", "Bryer 1980", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 56; p. 29", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 18 and n. 14 (Pachymeres VI.34; doubts about his account)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 49 (Pachymeres VI.34: despot's title, black shoes)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 189 (and n. 76: the siege in April 1283)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "When the new title was first used is debated, and so is what 'the Iberians' meant: the Georgian and Laz subjects, or a claim on Georgia. The 'Perateia', the Crimean lands across the sea, was by then mostly a claim. Eastmond (2004) notes 'some doubt about Pachymeres' account', since the 14th-century rulers still called themselves emperor. Panaretos' date for the Georgian siege, 'after the year 6790 (1282), in April', is read as April 1283 (Asp-Talwar).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "marriage of John II"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "attacked by David VI Narin (1282)"}, {"name": "Kutaisi (Imereti)", "lat": 42.27, "lon": 42.7, "role": "base of David VI Narin"}]}, {"id": "H4-022", "date": "1284", "date_end": "1285", "date_text": "Autumn 1284-1285", "precision": "year", "title": "Theodora, daughter of Manuel I, seizes the throne", "summary": "After John II's return in 1283 came 'the attack and occupation by kyr George Komnenos', called 'the wanderer', and with it the usurpation, rule and sudden flight of Theodora, eldest daughter of Manuel I by the Georgian Rusudan (Panaretos). She held Trebizond in 1284-1285 before John II recovered it. She is the first of three women who held the throne.", "people": ["Theodora Megale Komnene", "John II Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 1284-1285", "Coins struck in her name"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Karpov 2007", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 189 (and n. 78: 'George' usually taken to be the deposed George)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 51 ('Theodora who seized the throne from her half-brother John II in 1284-5')"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Georgian backing is inferred, not stated; her mother Rusudan was Georgian, which makes it plausible but unproven. Panaretos links her rule with the return of 'George Komnenos the wanderer', usually taken to be the deposed emperor George, though possibly another member of the family (Asp-Talwar).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}]}, {"id": "C2b-03", "date": "1292-06", "date_end": "1293", "date_text": "June-July 1292 (outward); 1293 (return)", "precision": "month", "title": "Edward I's embassy to the Ilkhan passes through Trebizond", "summary": "An English embassy sent by Edward I to the Ilkhan Arghun bought its supplies at Trebizond in June 1292 and camped on Mount Kampana, on the road south, on 22 July 1292; its accounts list bread, wine, fish, rice, olive oil, fruit, nuts and meat on the coast, against little but meat and bread beyond the mountains. Returning in 1293 it dragged a caged leopard, a present from the Ilkhan, from Tabriz to Dover; a new lock was made for the cage in Trebizond.", "people": ["Edward I of England", "Arghun Khan", "Geoffrey of Langley"], "sources_primary": ["Accounts of the embassy, Public Record Office, Rot. Pat. 19 Edw. I (via Bryer V 120 n. 13)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 119-120", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 370-371 (table: 10,000 aspers spent by the embassy in Trebizond)", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 336 (camp on Kampana, 22 July 1292)", "Bryer 1980, X ('Ludovico da Bologna and the Georgian and Anatolian Embassy', 1965) 179 (Geoffrey of Langley among the 13th-century envoys)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 257 (Kapanion, Mount Karakaban, sold Geoffrey of Langley's party bread, wine and 15 aspers' worth of meat on 22 July 1292; it marked the effective limit of the empire's protection in the treaty with Genoa of 26 October 1314)"], "confidence": "high", "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "supplies bought, June 1292"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "the road south (Kampana lay between Soumela and the Pontic Gates; not located exactly)"}, {"name": "Erzurum (Theodosioupolis)", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.27, "role": "beyond the Pontic Alps"}, {"name": "Tabriz", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.29, "role": "the Ilkhan's capital"}]}, {"id": "H4-023", "date": "1295", "date_text": "1295", "precision": "year", "title": "Marco Polo returns home through Trebizond", "summary": "Returning from China after escorting a Mongol princess to Persia, the Polos travelled from Tabriz to Trebizond and then by sea to Constantinople, Negroponte and Venice. Maffeo Polo's will (1309) records a loss of about 4,000 hyperpera inflicted by 'the Lord Comnenus of Trebizond', who must have been John II. Their route is the Tabriz-Trebizond road at its height.", "people": ["Marco Polo", "Niccolo Polo", "Maffeo Polo"], "sources_primary": ["Marco Polo, Devisement du monde, Prologue ch. 18 (Yule numbering): from Tauris to Trebizond, Constantinople, Negroponte and Venice", "Will of Maffeo Polo, notarised 6 February 1309 (ed. Moule & Pelliot 1938, I, 29), on a loss of about 4,000 hyperpera at Trebizond (quoted by Eastmond 2016, Introduction, p. 22)"], "sources_modern": ["Yule & Cordier 1903", "Karpov 1981", "Eastmond 2016, 'Introduction', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 22 (Maffeo Polo's will, notarised 6 February 1309: 'the aforesaid loss inflicted on us as well by the Lord Comnenus of Trebizond as in his territory was in sum about four thousand hyperpera'; Moule & Pelliot 1938, I, 29)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 51 (John II 'probably responsible for the extortion of Marco Polo's expedition in the 1290s')", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 338 (Polo's 'Tauris')"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The ruler blamed is not named in the will; Eastmond (2016, pp. 22, 51) identifies him as John II.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tabriz", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.29, "role": "waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.27, "role": "waypoint 2 (probable)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Negroponte (Chalkis)", "lat": 38.463, "lon": 23.595, "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Venice", "lat": 45.438, "lon": 12.335, "role": "waypoint 6"}]}, {"id": "H4-024", "date": "1295", "date_end": "1302", "date_text": "C. 1295-1302", "precision": "decade", "title": "Gregory Chioniades brings Persian astronomy to Trebizond", "summary": "Gregory Chioniades, a physician and scholar educated in Constantinople, went by way of Trebizond to Tabriz, studied astronomy there and translated Persian astronomical works into Greek. About 1300 he told his patron Alexios II that he was going to take up the bishopric of Tabriz ('Taurez'); after leaving it he lived the rest of his life as a monk in Trebizond. Through him and his successors at Trebizond (the priest Manuel, George Chrysokokkes) the Persian tables of the Maragha school reached Greek science; by 1336 the Zij-i Ilkhani was in use at Trebizond (see C2b-04).", "people": ["Gregory Chioniades", "Shams al-Din al-Bukhari", "Alexios II Megas Komnenos", "George Chrysokokkes (later, Persian Syntaxis 1347)"], "sources_primary": ["Chioniades' astronomical works and letters (ed. Pingree 1985)", "Vat. gr. 211, fol. 117r (before 1308): a short Greek treatise on Tusi's lunar theory translated by Chioniades, with a diagram of Tusi's device for turning circular into straight-line motion (Library of Congress, 'Rome Reborn: the Vatican Library and Renaissance Culture' online exhibit, item math16)"], "sources_modern": ["Pingree 1964, DOP 18", "Pingree 1985, The Astronomical Works of Gregory Chioniades", "Tihon (articles on Persian astronomy in Greek)", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 336-337 and n. 18 (c. 1300 Chioniades tells Alexios II that he is taking up the see 'of Taurez' and travels there from Trebizond)", "Rosenqvist 1996, The Hagiographic Dossier of St Eugenios of Trebizond, p. 22 and n. 4 (Chioniades' letter: Loukites with Alexios II at Kerasous in 1301), p. 24 n. 16 (after Tabriz, a monk in Trebizond for the rest of his life)", "Mercier 1994, An Almanac for Trebizond for the Year 1336, pp. 14-17 and 92-97 (the Persian tables at Trebizond; Chioniades' activity ended in the first decade of the century; the Zij al-'Ala'i preface examples are for Constantinople and 1295/6, p. 25 n. 19)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 54 (Chioniades 'between 1240-50 - c. 1320'; his Iliad and Thucydides passed to Constantine Loukites)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Pingree and later scholars differ on the dates of his journeys and on his role as a teacher at Trebizond. Mercier (1994) does not know when he died and ends his activity in the first decade of the century; Eastmond (2016) gives about 1320.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tabriz", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.29, "role": "where he studied"}, {"name": "Maragha (observatory)", "lat": 37.39, "lon": 46.24, "role": "source of the astronomical school"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "where he taught and died"}]}, {"id": "H4-025", "date": "1297-08-16", "date_text": "16 August 1297", "precision": "day", "title": "Alexios II becomes emperor", "summary": "Alexios II succeeded his father John II at about 14 and reigned for almost 33 years, the empire's richest period. Around 1300 he married a Georgian princess, Djiadjak, daughter of Beka I Jaqeli of Samtskhe, against the wishes of his uncle the emperor Andronikos II in Constantinople, who failed to annul the match.", "people": ["Alexios II Megas Komnenos", "Djiadjak Jaqeli", "Andronikos II Palaiologos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 1297", "George Pachymeres (on the marriage dispute)"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Karpov 2007", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 189 (John II died at Limnia on Friday 16 August 1297; Alexios II born late 1283, n. 77)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 190 (Alexios II married the daughter of Beka Jaqeli)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 98 (John II died at Limnia, the first mention of the place by that name)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield (1985) date John II's death at Limnia to 17 August 1297, a day later; 16 August 1297 was the Friday that Panaretos names.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}, {"name": "Akhaltsikhe (Samtskhe)", "lat": 41.64, "lon": 42.98, "role": "seat of the Jaqeli atabegs"}]}, {"id": "H4-026", "date": "1301-09", "date_text": "September AM 6810 = September 1301", "precision": "month", "title": "Turkmen defeated at Kerasous", "summary": "Alexios II campaigned against the Turks and at Kerasous overwhelmed a Turkmen leader the chronicle calls Koustouganes (Küçdoğan), killing many Turks; contemporary encomia say he then rebuilt the walls of Kerasous. His minister Constantine Loukites was with him on the campaign. Turkmen raids on the western banda were now regular.", "people": ["Alexios II Megas Komnenos", "Koustoganes"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, ed. Lampsides 63 (September AM 6810)", "Stephen Sgouropoulos, encomia of Alexios II (ed. Papadopoulos-Kerameus 1894; Papatheodorides 1954), on the walls of Kerasous (via Bryer V 133 n. 73)", "Gregory Chioniades, Letter 8 (ed. Papadopoulos 1927), on Loukites at Kerasous (via Rosenqvist 1996, p. 22 n. 4)"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Bryer 1975", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 190 ('September 6810 (1301)')", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 133 and 143 n. 129 ('Janssens is mistaken in following Miller in computing Panaretos' date to 1302'; Alexios rebuilt the walls of Kerasous, after Stephen Sgouropoulos' encomia)", "Rosenqvist 1996, p. 22 (Chioniades' letter places Loukites on Alexios II's campaign at Kerasous in 1301)", "Rosenqvist 1996, p. 81 (Kerasous 1301)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 128-129 (Panaretos' September AM 6810 = 1301, not 1302 as in Miller and Janssens; the keep on the acropolis of Kerasous \"almost certainly\" the fortress built or rebuilt by Alexios II after 1301)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 124 (Bryer withdraws his proposal that Omidie, at Bulancak, gave its name to the Amitiotai/early Akkoyunlu)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Older works (Miller 1926; Janssens 1969) give 1302, a miscalculation of AM 6810, whose September falls in 1301 (Bryer; Asp-Talwar 2016; Rosenqvist 1996). 'Koustouganes' may render 'küçük ağa' or 'küstah' (Bryer).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "battle and new fortress"}]}, {"id": "H4-027", "date": "1304-06", "date_text": "June AM 6812 = June 1304 (Panaretos: the year after the fire of Friday 30 November 6811 = 1302); older works say 1306", "precision": "year", "title": "Quarrel with the Genoese; the suburbs burned", "summary": "After a great fire inside the walls on Friday 30 November 1302, 'the following year' (in June, by Samouilidis' quotation of the manuscript) the Latins burned the exartesis, the shipyard or the still unwalled Lower City, 'when there was a severe conflict' (Panaretos). Pachymeres describes the quarrel in more detail: Alexios II refused the Genoese further privileges and fighting broke out. The emperor wanted the customs revenue; the Genoese, who also held Caffa in the Crimea, wanted exemptions.", "people": ["Alexios II Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Genoese documents (ed. in Karpov 1981 and earlier editions)", "Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, ed. Lampsides 63 (entries for AM 6811-6812)", "George Pachymeres, History (ed. Bekker 1835, pp. 417-419; cited by Asp-Talwar, n. 85)"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 1981", "Miller 1926", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 190 (and nn. 84-85: exartesis = shipyard, Lampe and Lampsides, or the Lower City, Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 183; Pachymeres, ed. Bekker, pp. 417-419)", "Samouilidis, p. 160 n. (quotes Panaretos fol. 290a: the burning of the exartesis 'μηνί Ἰουνίῳ παρὰ τῶν Λατίνων'; his own conversion to 1305/1306 follows Miller)", "Rosenqvist 1996, p. 81 ('two years later' than 1301 'hostilities with the Latins who burnt the Arsenal')", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 195 (\"in 1305 the Genoese burned down an arsenal\", which Bryer & Winfield take to be an imperial arsenal, Panaretos ed. Lampsides 63); p. 183 (fire inside and outside the walls in 1303; Alexios II walled the Lower City after 1302 and had finished by 1324)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Panaretos dates the fire to Friday 30 November AM 6811, which was 1302 (in 1305 that day was a Tuesday), and the Latin burning to the following year, June 1304. The year 1306 in older works (Miller 1926; Samouilidis, with a Genoese embassy of June 1306) comes from reckoning AM 6811 as 1305, which the weekday does not support; Rosenqvist has 1303. What burned is uncertain: the shipyard (Lampsides), the Lower City (Asp-Talwar), or an arsenal, probably the imperial one on the Daphnous shore (Bryer & Winfield 1985, who date the events 1303 and 1305).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "suburbs burned"}, {"name": "Caffa (Feodosia)", "lat": 45.03, "lon": 35.38, "role": "main Genoese base in the Black Sea"}]}, {"id": "C2-022", "date": "1311-05", "date_text": "May 1311 (codex note); in office to 1333", "precision": "month", "title": "Metropolitan Barnabas", "summary": "A manuscript note of May 1311 names 'the metropolitan of Trapezous and hypertimos kyr Barnabas' ('μητροπολίτου Τραπεζοῦντος καὶ ὑπερτίμου κυροῦ Βαρνάβα'). His tomb in the Chrysokephalos ('τάφος τοῦ Βαρνάβα') later received metropolitan Niphon (1364).", "people": ["Barnabas of Trapezous"], "sources_primary": ["Codex note of May 1311"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 230; p. 393"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chrysokephalos church (Ortahisar Fatih Mosque), Trabzon (approx.)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.719, "role": "tomb"}]}, {"id": "H4-028", "date": "1314", "date_end": "1316", "date_text": "1314 and 1316", "precision": "year", "title": "Treaties with Genoa; the Genoese at Leontokastron", "summary": "Peace with Genoa was confirmed by a treaty of 26 October 1314, in which the emperor also promised Genoese traders an escort as far as Mount Kampana on the road south; a further treaty is dated 1316. The Genoese held the Leontokastron, the rocky headland east of the walled city above the Daphnous landing, as their base from the first years of the 14th century until 1316, when they were moved to the arsenal of Daphnous; it was formally ceded to them on 15 June 1349, after the war of 1348–1349 (Panaretos), and they held it until 1461 (Bryer & Winfield 1985). It was a lasting source of friction. A Venetian castle later stood a few hundred metres to the west.", "people": ["Alexios II Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Treaties of 1314 and 1316 between Trebizond and Genoa (Genoese archives; see Karpov 1981)"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 1981", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 197 (Leontokastron, Frenk or Güzel Hisar: the Genoese base \"in the first years of the fourteenth century\"; evicted in 1316 to the arsenal of Daphnous, probably near the Panagia Eleousa; back in 1349 until the end of the empire); p. 222 (St Eleutherios at the Daphnous skala, Latin rite, probably from 1349)", "Miller 1926", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 336 n. 15 (Genoese-Trapezuntine treaty of 26 October 1314)", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 475 (escort to Kampana promised in the treaty of 26 October 1314)", "Bryer 1980, VIII ('Shipping in the Empire of Trebizond', 1966) 10 (Daphnous landing east of the Leontokastron)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 196 (15 June 1349: 'Leontokastron was handed over to them')", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 73 (\"In 1314 and again in 1316 the Genoese forced Alexios II to terms\", including their own base in Trebizond and \"a massive indemnity\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The treaty of 1316 is known from Bryer & Winfield (1985): in 1314 and again in 1316 the Genoese forced terms on Alexios II, including their own base in Trebizond and a large indemnity.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Leontokastron (Genoese castle, later Guzelhisar/Kalepark; approximate)", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 39.741, "role": "Genoese quarter and castle"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "walled city"}]}, {"id": "H4-029", "date": "1319", "date_text": "1319", "precision": "year", "title": "Venetian privileges; Sinopean raiders burn Trebizond", "summary": "Alexios II granted the Venetians a chrysobull and treaty, the first of five Venetian treaties with Trebizond (1319–1396), giving them the same terms as the Genoese; Venice then looked for a better site for its commercial base, in the end on the cape of the Holy Cross (Tzavara 2011). In the same year Turkish raiders from Sinope set fire to the city, and in 1324 Alexios II completed the walls of the Lower City, which more than doubled the walled area (Bryer & Winfield 1985).", "people": ["Alexios II Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Venetian treaty and chrysobull of 1319 (no original survives; new edition proposed by Tzavara 2011)", "Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1319 and 1324"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 1981", "Miller 1926", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 191 ('In 6827 (1319), the Sinopitans caused a conflagration')", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 190 n. 85 (Bryer: the wall round the Lower City completed by 1324)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 143 (AM 6827 = 1318/19)", "Tzavara, A. 2011, 'I trattati commerciali tra Venezia e l'impero di Trebisonda, 1319–1396', Thesaurismata 41–42: 41–87 (abstract)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 73 (the Sinopitan ruler, the former ally of the Genoese, \"attacked and burnt down part of the city of Trebizond in 1319\")", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 183 (Alexios II enclosed the Lower City down to the sea; two inscriptions on the west wall name him, one with his master of works Constantine, the other dated 1324; \"By 1324... Alexios II had completed the enclosure of the Lower City\")"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield give the walled area as growing from 86,400 to 220,900 square metres.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Venetian quarter; city burned"}, {"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "base of the raiders"}]}, {"id": "H4-030", "date": "1330-05-03", "date_end": "1332-09-23", "date_text": "1330-1332", "precision": "year", "title": "Death of Alexios II and dynastic murders", "summary": "Alexios II died on 3 May 1330. His son Andronikos III had two of his brothers killed on his accession and died in January 1332; the boy Manuel II was deposed in September 1332 by his uncle Basil, who had him killed with a sword on 21 February 1333, the Sunday of Orthodoxy. A long run of instability began.", "people": ["Alexios II Megas Komnenos", "Andronikos III Megas Komnenos", "Manuel II Megas Komnenos", "Basil Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1330-1332"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Karpov 2007", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 191 (3 May 1330; Andronikos III killed his brothers Michael Azachoutlou and George Achpouga; died Wednesday 8 January 1332; Basil arrived Tuesday 22 September 1332; Manuel II killed 21 February 1333)", "Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 37-38"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}]}, {"id": "H4-031", "date": "1335-11-30", "date_text": "30 November 1335 (13 Rabi' II 736: death of Abu Sa'id)", "precision": "day", "title": "The Ilkhanate breaks up: end of Mongol overlordship", "summary": "The death of the Ilkhan Abu Sa'id ended the unity of Mongol Iran. Trebizond was freed of its overlord, but the Tabriz trade became less safe and Turkmen confederations, above all the Aq Qoyunlu of Diyarbakir, began to press on the Pontic frontier.", "people": ["Abu Sa'id"], "sources_primary": [], "sources_modern": ["Shukurov 2001", "Karpov 2007", "Woods 1999, The Aqquyunlu", "Jackson, P. 1983, 'Abū Saʿīd Bahādor Khan', Encyclopaedia Iranica (iranicaonline.org/?p=4832): died at his headquarters in Qarabagh, 13 Rabīʿ II 736 / 30 November 1335; for a decade or more the Ilkhanate was 'racked by disputes among rival claimants'"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sultaniyya (Ilkhanid capital)", "lat": 36.434, "lon": 48.797, "role": "Ilkhanid court"}, {"name": "Amida (Diyarbakir), centre of the Aq Qoyunlu", "lat": 37.91, "lon": 40.24, "role": "rising Turkmen power"}]}, {"id": "C2b-04", "date": "1336-03-12", "date_end": "1337-03-03", "date_text": "Almanac for 12 March 1336 - 12 March 1337; eclipse and riot on Monday 3 March 1337", "precision": "day", "title": "An almanac for Trebizond, and the eclipse riot of 1337", "summary": "A Greek almanac drawn up at Trebizond for 12 March 1336 to 12 March 1337, with astrological predictions addressed to the protonotarios and protovestiarios Constantine Loukites, computed the positions of sun, moon and planets from the Persian Zij-i Ilkhani of the Maragha observatory. It survives in a manuscript copied by Andreas Libadenos, prototaboullarios and chartophylax of Trebizond; its editor ascribes it to the priest Manuel of Trebizond, the teacher of George Chrysokokkes. Persian tables are not known in Constantinople before about 1346. On Monday 3 March 1337, the first Monday of Lent, an annular eclipse, seen at Trebizond as partial, darkened the city from the fourth to the seventh hour, and 'the people rose in turmoil against the emperor' Basil and stoned the citadel (Panaretos).", "people": ["Constantine Loukites", "Manuel of Trebizond (priest, astronomer)", "Andreas Libadenos", "George Chrysokokkes", "Basil Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Almanac for 1336, Monacensis gr. 525 (copied in Libadenos' codex), ed. Mercier 1994", "Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 3 March AM 6845"], "sources_modern": ["Mercier 1994, An Almanac for Trebizond for the Year 1336 (Corpus des astronomes byzantins VII), pp. 14-17 (dedication to Loukites; Persian tables at Trebizond), Introduction (Libadenos' colophon), pp. 78-81 (the eclipse of 1337), pp. 92-97 (authorship: the priest Manuel)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 192 (the eclipse and the stoning of the citadel)", "Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 22-23 (Loukites, still in Basil's service in 1336 'as appears from the Trapezuntine Horoscope')"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The author was the priest Manuel of Trebizond (Mercier 1994), not Libadenos, who only copied it (Lambros), nor Chrysokokkes (Bryer 1986), nor Manuel Bryennios. Panaretos does not say why the people rose in 1337; Bryer (1986, as cited by Mercier) sees a protest at the emperor's immoral life, his bigamy with Irene of Trebizond. A 'school' or 'academy' of astronomy with an observatory in the tower of Hagia Sophia (Papadopoulos 1919, 1927) is rejected: the tower was built about 1426/7 (Mercier; Rosenqvist).", "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "where the almanac was computed; the citadel stoned in 1337"}, {"name": "Maragha (observatory)", "lat": 37.39, "lon": 46.24, "role": "source of the Zij-i Ilkhani tables"}]}, {"id": "H4-032", "date": "1340-04-06", "date_text": "6 April 1340", "precision": "day", "title": "Basil dies; Irene Palaiologina seizes power; the Scholarioi and the Amytzantarioi", "summary": "Basil died on Thursday 6 April 1340 and his widow Irene Palaiologina, an illegitimate daughter of the emperor Andronikos III Palaiologos, took power; Basil's other wife, Irene of Trebizond, was sent to Constantinople with her sons. The archons split: the megas stratopedarches Tzanichites with the Scholarioi, the Meizomatai, Constantine Doranites, the Kabazitai and Kamachenos, part of the people and some imperial units seized the monastery of St Eugenios, while the Amytzantarioi (the holders of court titles) held the citadel with the empress. On 2 July 1340 the megas doux John the Eunuch came from Limnia, stormed the monastery with siege engines and burned it; Tzanichites and the other archons were imprisoned at Limnia and executed there in June 1341. Nine years of civil war followed.", "people": ["Basil Megas Komnenos", "Irene Palaiologina", "Andronikos III Palaiologos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1340"], "sources_modern": ["Kursanskis 1979 (on the civil war)", "Karpov 2007", "Miller 1926", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 192 (6 April 1340; the two factions)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 193 (2 July 1340; executions at Limnia, June 1341)", "Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 37-39 and n. 69 (Basil's bigamy with Irene of Trebizond and the anger it caused; Bryer's emendation of 'all its beauties' to 'granaries' for what burned)", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 418-419", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 141", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 183, 223 (St Eugenios was fortified and needed siege engines to reduce in 1340; Panaretos says the church was burnt to the ground); p. 98 (Tzanichites and the nobles banished to Limnia in 1340 and executed there in 1341)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Panaretos does not say what the factions stood for. Older accounts (Miller 1926, British) saw a court party looking to Constantinople against local landed families, with the Laz as a third force; Bryer (1979, British) sees a struggle over land, since so much crown land had gone out in grants that the emperors could no longer buy loyalty, the coastal Scholarioi and the inland Mesochaldaioi holding different kinds of estates; he also notes (1975) that the factions had nothing to do with the Turkmen and mirrored the disturbances in Constantinople. Rosenqvist (1996, Swedish), after Kislinger and Bryer, stresses Basil's bigamy with a local woman, Irene of Trebizond, which humiliated the princess from Constantinople and angered Constantinople and 'wide circles in Trebizond'; a patriarchal letter of 1340 backed Irene Palaiologina.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}, {"name": "Monastery of St Eugenios (Yeni Cuma Mosque; approximate)", "lat": 41.004, "lon": 39.724, "role": "stronghold of the Scholarioi"}]}, {"id": "H4-033", "date": "1341-07-04", "date_text": "4-30 July 1341", "precision": "month", "title": "Aq Qoyunlu raid; Anna Anachoutlou takes the throne with the Laz", "summary": "On Wednesday 4 July 1341 the Amitiotai Turkmen (the early Aq Qoyunlu) returned; the Romans fell back without a fight, many Christians were killed and all of Trebizond, inside and outside the walls, was burned, followed by a sudden pestilence from the stench of the dead. Anna Anachoutlou, a daughter of Alexios II, escaped from her convent, was proclaimed among the Laz, and entered Trebizond on 17 July 1341. When Michael Komnenos arrived from Constantinople with three warships on 30 July to take power, the Laz and Anna's noble allies routed his men the next morning and imprisoned him at Oinaion, then Limnia. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 75-76, 231-246) adds the church side: St Eugenios burned in 1340 and the whole city in July 1341 ('Ἐκαύθη ἡ Τραπεζοῦς ὅλη'); Metropolitan Akakios received Michael on 30 July 1341 'with the Gospel', crowned John III in the Chrysokephalos at the ambo on 9 September 1342, rebuilt the Chrysokephalos 'from the ash-heaps' and crowned Alexios III in the rebuilt St Eugenios on 21 January 1350.", "people": ["Anna Anachoutlou", "Irene Palaiologina", "Michael Megas Komnenos", "Akakios, metropolitan of Trebizond"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1341"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 2007", "Miller 1926", "Bryer 1975", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 231–246; p. 75; pp. 380–381; p. 395", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 193 (4 July 1341: 'all of Trebizond was burned, both inside and outside'; Anna 17 July; Michael 30 July)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 194 (Akakios; Michael sent to Oinaion, then Limnia, 7 August; Irene sent off 10 August)", "Samouilidis, pp. 190-191", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 183 (houses inside and outside the walls burned in 1341; the walled city held against Turkish attack in 1223, 1297, 1336, 1341 and 1456); p. 98 (Michael imprisoned at Limnia 1341-1344)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 124 (Bryer withdraws his proposal that Omidie, at Bulancak, gave its name to the Amitiotai/early Akkoyunlu)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Panaretos dates the burning precisely to Wednesday 4 July 1341. The Amitiotai are generally identified as the early Aq Qoyunlu (Asp-Talwar). Bryer once derived their name from 'Omidia' west of Kerasous rather than from Amida, but later withdrew the proposal (Bryer & Winfield 1985, who place Omidie at Bulancak).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "raided, then taken by Anna"}, {"name": "Lazia (Greater Lazia, Athina/Pazar)", "lat": 41.18, "lon": 40.88, "role": "where Anna was proclaimed (approximate)"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Unye)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.29, "role": "Michael imprisoned"}, {"name": "Limnia (near the Iris delta, Carsamba; location approximate)", "lat": 41.23, "lon": 36.73, "role": "Michael held by the megas doux John the Eunuch"}]}, {"id": "H4-034", "date": "1342-09-04", "date_text": "Sailed from Constantinople 17 August; arrived Wednesday 4 September; crowned 9 September 1342", "precision": "day", "title": "John III arrives with Genoese galleys; Anna strangled", "summary": "The Scholarioi leaders who had fled to Constantinople on 10 September 1341 returned with Michael's son John, two galleys of their own and three hired Genoese galleys; they left on 17 August and reached Trebizond on Wednesday 4 September 1342. John III was crowned on 9 September at the ambo of the Chrysokephalos; the Amytzantarian archons were killed, and Anna Anachoutlou was strangled together with Sargale, after a reign Panaretos gives as one year, one month and eight days. The Genoese role shows the Italians as actors in Trapezuntine politics.", "people": ["John III Megas Komnenos", "Anna Anachoutlou", "Niketas Scholares", "Constantine Doranites", "Gregory Meizomates"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1342"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 2007", "Miller 1926", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 231–246; p. 75; pp. 380–381; p. 395", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 194 (10 September 1341; 17 August; Wednesday 4 September 6851 (1342); coronation 9 September)", "Samouilidis, pp. 190-191 (17 August, 4 September, 9 September 1342)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The day of Anna's death is not given; her 'one year, one month and eight days' from 17 July 1341 would end before the arrival, so the count is inexact.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "street fighting and coup"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "where the exiles found John"}]}, {"id": "H4-035", "date": "1344-05-03", "date_text": "3 May 1344", "precision": "day", "title": "Michael crowned; the Scholarioi oligarchy", "summary": "After the megas doux John the Eunuch, who held Michael prisoner at Limnia, was killed there in March 1344, Niketas Scholares fetched Michael, who took power on Monday 3 May and was crowned on 21 May 1344; his son John III was shut up in the cave of St Sabbas. The new court was shared out among the archons: Niketas Scholares megas doux, Gregory Meizomates megas stratopedarches, Leo Kabazites megas domestikos, Constantine Doranites protovestiarios, John Kabazites megas logariastes. In November 1345 Michael had Niketas Scholares and others of his party arrested. The western districts were meanwhile left to the Turkmen.", "people": ["Michael Megas Komnenos", "Niketas Scholares", "John III Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1343-1345"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 2007", "Miller 1926", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 194", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 195 (coronation 21 May 1344; offices; arrests November 1345)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 98 (the megas doux John the Eunuch, gaoler of Michael at Limnia, raised a \"large army\" there)"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}, {"name": "Limnia (near the Iris delta, Carsamba; location approximate)", "lat": 41.23, "lon": 36.73, "role": "where Michael was freed"}]}, {"id": "H4-036", "date": "1347", "date_end": "1348", "date_text": "AM 6855 (September 1346 - August 1347) and 29 June 1348", "precision": "year", "title": "Turkmen take Oinaion: the western frontier gives way", "summary": "In AM 6855 (1346/47) the Turkmen of Chalybia took Hagios Andreas, probably on Cape Jason, and Oinaion with its mountain road to Neokaisareia. On 29 June 1348 a coalition attacked Trebizond itself: Ahi Ayna Beg of Erzincan, Muhammad the Rikabdar of Bayburt, Turali Beg of the Amitiotai (Aq Qoyunlu), and Boz Doğan with the Chepni; they fought for three days and left in flight. The coast west of Kerasous, the old banda of Oinaion and Limnia, was slipping into the hands of Turkmen emirs.", "people": ["Michael Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1346-1348"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975", "Zachariadou 1979, Archeion Pontou 35", "Karpov 2007", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 195 ('In 6855 (1347), St. Andrew and Oinaion were captured'; 29 June 1348; n. 104: St Andrew probably on Cape Jason, after Bryer)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 130, 133-134 (the Chalybians took Oinaion and St Andrew in 1347; the coalition of 1348)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 101 (1347: Oinaion and \"St. Andreas (presumably Cape Jason)\" lost); p. 121 (the Jasonian promontory passed to the Turkmens of Limnia between 1357 and 1404)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 172 (in 1348 \"the ahi Ayna beg of Erzincan invaded Trebizond, having made an alliance with the rulers of Bayburt, the Akkoyunlular, and the cepni Turkmens\"; the Çepni allies probably from round Golacha)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "AM 6855 runs from September 1346 to August 1347; Bryer and Asp-Talwar write 1347, older accounts 1346. Hagios Andreas was 'probably on Cape Jason' (Bryer); it is not shown on the map.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Oinaion (Unye)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.29, "role": "lost to the Turkmen"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "attacked 1348"}, {"name": "Hagios Andreas (presumably Cape Jason, Yasun Burnu, Perşembe)", "lat": 41.13377, "lon": 37.6824, "role": "lost to the Turkmens in 1347; Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 101 'presumably Cape Jason'; approximate"}]}, {"id": "H4-037", "date": "1347-09", "date_text": "September 1347", "precision": "month", "title": "The Black Death reaches Trebizond", "summary": "The plague, carried by ship from Caffa, struck Trebizond in September 1347 and lasted about seven months. Panaretos says only that it took 'many children and wives, brothers, mothers and relatives'; other contemporaries say nothing of it, so its scale is unknown. It came back in 1362 and 1382.", "people": ["Michael Panaretos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 1347"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 2007", "Bryer 1980", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 195 ('In September, in the first indiction... It raged for seven months')", "Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 38, 71 (no healing miracles of St Eugenios recorded in the plague years of the 1340s-1350s)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, pp. 201, 207 (new outbreaks in 1362 and from 8 July 1382, when it reached Matzouka, Trikomia and Sourmaina)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 121 (after the Black Death Tafur's 4,000 for the city's population 'not too low')"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Andrew Libadenos does not mention it, and no figures exist.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Caffa (Feodosia)", "lat": 45.03, "lon": 35.38, "role": "where the plague entered the Black Sea trade"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "struck"}]}, {"id": "H4-038", "date": "1348", "date_end": "1349", "date_text": "1348-1349", "precision": "year", "title": "War with Genoa: Kerasous seized; the fleet destroyed", "summary": "After a massacre of Genoese in the capital, the Genoese attacked Kerasous, the empire's second city, in January 1348, and ransacked and burned the town. In May 1349 a Genoese fleet from Caffa destroyed the Trapezuntine fleet under the megas doux John Kabazites, and townspeople killed westerners in revenge. The peace left the Genoese in possession of the Leontokastron.", "people": ["Michael Megas Komnenos", "John Kabazites"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1348-1349", "Genoese documents (see Karpov 1981)"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 1981", "Miller 1926", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 195 (January 6856 (1348): Kerasous captured, enslaved and burned by the Genoese; Tuesday 5 May 1349 sea battle; John Kabazites and Michael Tzanichites killed)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 196 (15 June 1349: peace and the Leontokastron)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 129 (January 1348: a Genoese reprisal raid ransacked and burned Kerasous; \"there is no question of there ever being an Italian station in the place\"; the fortress perhaps unscathed)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Older accounts have the Genoese seize and keep Kerasous; Bryer & Winfield (1985) read Panaretos as describing a reprisal raid, and state that there was never an Italian station at Kerasous. By 1354–1355 the town was in the hands of the rebel Niketas Scholares.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "seized by the Genoese"}, {"name": "Leontokastron (Genoese castle, later Guzelhisar/Kalepark; approximate)", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 39.741, "role": "Genoese castle confirmed"}, {"name": "Caffa (Feodosia)", "lat": 45.03, "lon": 35.38, "role": "base of the Genoese fleet"}]}, {"id": "H4-039", "date": "1349-12-22", "date_text": "13-22 December 1349", "precision": "day", "title": "Alexios III arrives and takes the throne", "summary": "On 13 December 1349 Niketas Scholares and his allies deposed Michael in favour of John, a son of Basil, who had been sent from Constantinople with the support of John VI Kantakouzenos. He arrived on 22 December, took the name Alexios III and was crowned on 21 January 1350. His forty-year reign (1349-1390) ended the civil war.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "John VI Kantakouzenos", "Niketas Scholares", "Michael Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1349-1350", "John Kantakouzenos, Histories", "John Lazaropoulos, Synopsis of the miracles of St Eugenios, lines 1685-1717 (ed. Rosenqvist 1996): Kantakouzenos decided to replace Michael, 'blunt and frivolous as well as old and childless', with Basil's son"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 2007", "Miller 1926", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 231–246; p. 75; pp. 380–381; p. 395", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 196 (Sunday 13 December 1349; Tuesday 22 December; crowned in St Eugenios on 21 January, the saint's feast)", "Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 31, 38-39 (Lazaropoulos, Synopsis lines 1685-1717: Kantakouzenos sent the eleven-year-old John, escorted by Lazaropoulos, in autumn 1349; coronation in St Eugenios, wedding there in 1351)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 254 (Alexios III was crowned in St Eugenios on 21 January 1350, not at Soumela as guidebooks say)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Rosenqvist (p. 31) gives 12 December for the accession; Panaretos has Michael deposed on Sunday 13 December and John arriving on Tuesday 22 December (both weekdays fit 1349).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "where Alexios III was sent from"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}]}, {"id": "H4-040", "date": "1350", "date_text": "C. 1350", "precision": "decade", "title": "The empire about 1350", "summary": "After the losses of 1346-1348 the empire runs from Kerasous (contested 1348-1355) to Makriali, with Chaldia under its own dukes of the Kabazites family. Oinaion and the coast west of Kerasous are Turkmen; Limnia is a doubtful outpost.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985", "Bryer 1975", "Zachariadou 1979", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 129-130 (Limnia: Alexios III's winter visits 1351-1379)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "How far Chaldia obeyed Trebizond, and when Limnia passed to the Taceddinoğulları (before 1379), are uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Coast west of Kerasous", "lat": 40.93, "lon": 38.3, "role": "boundary vertex 1 (coast, western limit)"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "boundary vertex 2 (coast; contested 1348-1355)"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "boundary vertex 3 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kordyle (Akcakale)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 39.48, "role": "boundary vertex 4 (coast)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "boundary vertex 5 (coast)"}, {"name": "Sourmaina (Surmene)", "lat": 40.91, "lon": 40.12, "role": "boundary vertex 6 (coast)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "boundary vertex 7 (coast)"}, {"name": "Athina (Pazar)", "lat": 41.18, "lon": 40.88, "role": "boundary vertex 8 (coast)"}, {"name": "Archabis (Arhavi)", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 41.3, "role": "boundary vertex 9 (coast)"}, {"name": "Makriali (Kemalpasa)", "lat": 41.485, "lon": 41.52, "role": "boundary vertex 10 (coast, eastern limit; approximate)"}, {"name": "Lazian ridge", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 41.55, "role": "boundary vertex 11 (inland)"}, {"name": "Kackar ridge", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 41.1, "role": "boundary vertex 12 (inland)"}, {"name": "Pontic watershed south of Rize", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 40.55, "role": "boundary vertex 13 (inland)"}, {"name": "East of Chaldia, short of Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.4, "lon": 40.05, "role": "boundary vertex 14 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Keltzine (Kelkit)", "lat": 40.05, "lon": 39.45, "role": "boundary vertex 15 (inland)"}, {"name": "West of Cheriana (Siran)", "lat": 40.15, "lon": 38.95, "role": "boundary vertex 16 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Kerasous", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 38.4, "role": "boundary vertex 17 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills west of Kerasous", "lat": 40.8, "lon": 38.25, "role": "boundary vertex 18 (inland)"}, {"name": "Limnia (near the Iris delta, Carsamba; location approximate)", "lat": 41.23, "lon": 36.73, "role": "isolated outpost, doubtful (separate marker)"}, {"name": "Chalybia (emirate of Hacı Emir; approximate centre at Bolaman)", "lat": 41.04, "lon": 37.59, "role": "Turkmen neighbour (label)"}]}, {"id": "C2-025", "date": "1351-08", "date_text": "August 1351", "precision": "month", "title": "Niphon of Trapezous signs the Palamite tome of 1351", "summary": "Metropolitan Niphon Pterygionites signed the third (Palamite) tome of August 1351 as 'the humble metropolitan of Trapezous and hypertimos Niphon' ('ὁ ταπεινὸς μητροπολίτης Τραπεζοῦντος καὶ ὑπέρτιμος Νήφων').", "people": ["Niphon Pterygionites", "Gregory Palamas"], "sources_primary": ["Tome of the synod of August 1351"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 206–207"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "synod"}]}, {"id": "H4-041", "date": "1352", "date_text": "1352", "precision": "year", "title": "First Turkmen marriage: Maria to Qutlugh Beg of the Aq Qoyunlu", "summary": "In August 1352 Alexios III married his eldest sister Maria to Fahreddin Kutlug Beg, son of Turali, emir of the Amitiotai (the early Aq Qoyunlu), whose raids had burned the city in 1341. It was the first time a princess of the Roman tradition had been given to a Turkmen, and it began a policy of marrying Komnenian princesses to Turkmen rulers to turn raiders into in-laws and allies: state visits followed (Maria in 1358, Kutlug Beg in 1365, the emperor in 1367). The marriage left a trace in Ballad 6 of the Book of Dede Korkut, where Kan Turali wins the princess of Trebizond in the Meydan.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Maria Megale Komnene", "Qutlugh Beg (Aq Qoyunlu)"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 1352"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975, 'Greeks and Türkmens: The Pontic Exception', DOP 29", "Woods 1999", "Shukurov 2016", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 197 (August 1352)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 134-137", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 33 (Dede Korkut)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 50 (Angold's 'Greek emirate')", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. 153-154", "Malone-Lee 2023, Cardinal Bessarion, p. 11 (Chalkokondyles: 'Greek manners and the Greek language')", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 124 (Bryer withdraws his proposal that Omidie, at Bulancak, gave its name to the Amitiotai/early Akkoyunlu)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "On how 'Greek' the empire was, the question this marriage policy raises: the Genoese Belban, reported by the Egyptian compiler al-Umari (1342–1348), found it in every respect like the Turkish principalities round it; Angold (1999, British) calls it a 'Greek emirate'; Bryer (1975, British) holds that in the west the Grand Komnenos was at once emperor and 'melik of Canik', the lord of small Turkmen emirates he helped create, yet that Turkish and Laz influence on society and government was 'unexpectedly superficial' and the Trapezuntines 'even more Roman than the Romaioi' (of 192 known office-holders all but two had Pontic or Roman names); Laonikos Chalkokondyles (15th century) says the rulers kept 'Hellenic manners and the Hellenic speech' and married barbarians only to protect their lands; Eastmond (2004, British) sees a Roman empire in its own right that adapted to local audiences; and Bessarion's Encomium (about 1436) makes Trebizond the image of ancient Athens, its people of pure Hellenic stock.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "court"}, {"name": "Amida (Diyarbakir), Aq Qoyunlu centre", "lat": 37.91, "lon": 40.24, "role": "groom's people"}]}, {"id": "H4-042", "date": "1355", "date_text": "1355", "precision": "year", "title": "Alexios III retakes Kerasous; defeat by the Turkmen", "summary": "Niketas Scholares fled to Kerasous in June 1354 and on 25 March 1355 attacked the capital with one galley and eleven small boats before withdrawing. In May 1355 Alexios III sailed against Kerasous with his mother, his empress and the metropolitan, and after a battle the city submitted; Kechrina, where Niketas held out, surrendered soon after, and in October 1355 Niketas was seized and a settlement made. On 27 November 1355 an expedition against Cheriana ended in a rout: about 50 Christians died, John Kabazites, duke of Chaldia, was captured, and Panaretos escaped only because his horse was strong. Niketas, by then reconciled, died on 30 July 1361; the emperor mourned him in white.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Niketas Scholares", "Michael Panaretos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 1354-1355 and 1360"], "sources_modern": ["Karpov 2007", "Miller 1926", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 197 (June 1354; 25 March and May 1355)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 198 (Kechrina; October 1355; 27 November 1355)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 200 (death of Niketas Scholarios, 30 July 6869 = 1361)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 144 (translation of the Cheriana entry)", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 419 ('1361. Grand Duke Niketas Scholaris dies')", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 135 (Kenchrina, an imperial castle with a small town between Kerasous and Tripolis, mentioned only by Panaretos and Libadenos for 1349-55; tentatively placed at the coastal castle of Kalecik-Hisarüstü on the west side of Cape Zephyrion)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The weekday Panaretos gives (Wednesday) does not fit 30 July 1361 (a Friday) as printed, so the day may be corrupt; the month and year stand.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "retaken from the rebels"}, {"name": "Kechrina/Kenchrina (Bryer & Winfield: tentatively Kalecik-Hisarüstü castle, Keşap)", "lat": 40.9602, "lon": 38.6169, "role": "castle where Niketas Scholares held out in 1355; Bryer & Winfield proposal; mapped at Hisarüstü (Nişanyan, old names Kalecik 1916, Kenxrina 1390); approximate"}]}, {"id": "C2b-05", "date": "1355-08", "date_end": "1355-11-27", "date_text": "August - Friday 27 November 1355", "precision": "month", "title": "Cheriana won and lost", "summary": "In August 1355 John Kabazites, duke of Chaldia, took Cheriana (Şiran) and brought Sorogaina under imperial rule. On Friday 27 November an expedition led by Alexios III against Cheriana ravaged and besieged, then broke in panic when a few Turks gave chase: about 50 Christians and many horses were lost, the duke was captured, and Panaretos, who rode with the emperor, escaped to Trebizond in three days. Cheriana, the only rural district south of the mountains that the emperors normally could not protect, was raided again in 1373, when 140 men died in the snow.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "John Kabazites, duke of Chaldia", "Michael Panaretos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for August and 27 November AM 6864 and 13 January AM 6881"], "sources_modern": ["Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 198", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 204 (13 January 1373)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 125-126, 144 (Cheriana; translation of the 1355 entry)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 303 (August 1355: John I Kabazites attacked the Turks of Cheriana and liberated Sorogaina on the way); p. 308 (Sorogaina = Solochaina, Soruyana, now Yalınkavak, castle on the Soruyana Dere)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Panaretos blames the rout on 'diabolical connivance' (Asp-Talwar, on his bias). Bryer & Winfield (1985) identify Sorogaina with the castle at Soruyana, now Yalınkavak, south-west of Gümüşhane (Nişanyan gives the old names Soroyana and Solochaina).", "places": [{"name": "Cheriana (Şiran, upper Kelkit valley)", "lat": 40.188, "lon": 39.125, "role": "the district won and lost (town location)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "base of the expedition"}, {"name": "Sorogaina (Solochaina) castle, at Yalınkavak (Soruyana), Torul", "lat": 40.4426, "lon": 39.2605, "role": "castle liberated by John I Kabazites in August 1355 (Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 303, 308); mapped at Yalınkavak village (Nişanyan); approximate"}]}, {"id": "H4-043", "date": "1358", "date_text": "1358", "precision": "year", "title": "Theodora married to Hacı Emir of Chalybia", "summary": "On 29 August 1358 Alexios III sent his sister Theodora, escorted by Basil Choupakas Scholarios, to marry Hacı Emir Ibrahim, son of Bayram, the Turkmen lord of Chalybia, the hill country behind Oinaion and Kotyora, whose raiders had broken into Matzouka on 13 November 1357. Oinaion seems to have come back to the empire with the alliance, and in December 1361 the emperor paid a state visit to the emir's 'home castle'. The Hacıemiroğulları held the coast between Oinaion and Kerasous and were the empire's nearest western neighbour; a daughter of Alexios III later married Hacı Emir's son Suleyman.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Theodora Megale Komnene (sister of Alexios III)", "Hacı Emir Ibrahim", "Suleyman Beg"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 1358"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975", "Zachariadou 1979", "Shukurov 2016", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 199 (13 November 1357; 22 and 29 August 1358)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 200 (13 December 1361, the state visit)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 130-132", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 101-102 (November 1357 raid on Matzouka by \"Hacı Omar, son of Bayram\"; the marriage of August 1358; the emir's \"spitokastron\" of December 1361 perhaps Çaleoğlu Kale south of Oinaion, Kekirkalesi west of Akkuş, or Boloman Kale)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Where the emir's 'home castle' was is unknown: Bryer (1975) offers Çaleoğlu Kale, Gölköy Kilise Kale or Bolaman; Bryer & Winfield (1985) Çaleoğlu Kale at Kaleköy, 5 km south-west of Ünye, Kekirkalesi about 13 km west of Akkuş, or Bolaman. They name the emir Hacı Omar, son of Bayram.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Chalybia (approximate centre at Bolaman)", "lat": 41.04, "lon": 37.59, "role": "emirate of Hacı Emir"}, {"name": "Oinaion (Unye)", "lat": 41.13, "lon": 37.29, "role": "in the emirate"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "frontier town"}]}, {"id": "C2b-06", "date": "1361-07-23", "date_text": "Friday 23 July 1361", "precision": "day", "title": "The Matzoukans destroy Hodja Latif's raid", "summary": "Hodja Latif, ruler of Bayburt, against whom Alexios III had gone into Chaldia in April 1360 to build the fortress of 'the Cuckoo', crossed into Matzouka with about 400 picked men by Lacharane (Larhan yayla, Akarsu) and Chasdenicha. The Matzoukans, who had prepared the passes, killed about 200 of them, took many animals and weapons and beheaded Hodja Latif; next day the heads were paraded through Trebizond. It shows the valley bandon defending itself, and Lazaropoulos' tale of Alexios II killing a dragon on Mount Minthrion may echo such victories.", "people": ["Hodja Latif of Bayburt", "Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Metropolitan Niphon"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for April AM 6868 and 23 July AM 6869", "Andreas Libadenos (reflects the victory, via Rosenqvist 1996, p. 81)"], "sources_modern": ["Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 200 (and nn. 111-112: Lacharane = Akarsu, Chasdenicha = Yukarıköy, after Bryer)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 145", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 475 (the summer pasture of Larhan, across which Hodja Latif invaded)", "Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 80-81 (the dragon story and the victory of 1361)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 256 (Panaretos' Larachane = Larhan, now Akarsu, over 1,250 m, a minor staging post; the 1361 raid quoted); p. 257 (Chasdenicha = Upper Chortokopion, Hortokop bala, now Yukarıköy, the Gizenenica of the itineraries); pp. 303-304 (\"Tou Koukou\" = Koç Kale, founded by Alexios III in 1360)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield (1985) identify Lacharane with Larhan (now Akarsu, Maçka) and Chasdenicha with Upper Chortokopion (now Yukarıköy, Maçka), and the fortress of 'the Cuckoo' of 1360 with Koç Kale in Chaldia.", "places": [{"name": "Matzouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.613, "role": "the bandon that defended the passes (Lacharane and Chasdenicha not located exactly)"}, {"name": "Paipert (Bayburt)", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.227, "role": "Hodja Latif's base"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "heads paraded"}, {"name": "Lacharane (Larhan, now Akarsu, Maçka)", "lat": 40.7219, "lon": 39.5977, "role": "one of the two passes by which Hodja Latif entered Matzouka in 1361 (Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 256); village point"}, {"name": "Chasdenicha (Upper Chortokopion, now Yukarıköy, Maçka)", "lat": 40.7664, "lon": 39.5751, "role": "the other pass of 1361 (Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 257); village point"}]}, {"id": "C2-027", "date": "1363-10-27", "date_end": "1364-03-19", "date_text": "Plot 27 October 1363; death 19 March 1364", "precision": "day", "title": "Metropolitan Niphon in the archons' plot; confined at Soumela", "summary": "Niphon, former protosynkellos, served Alexios III (Kerasous, May 1356; the victory in Matzouka over Hoca Latif, 23 July 1361). He was implicated in the plot of the Kabasitai and the Scholares of 27 October 1363, confined at Soumela, and died on 19 March 1364; he was buried in the tomb of Barnabas in the Chrysokephalos.", "people": ["Niphon Pterygionites", "Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Kabasites family", "Scholares family"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 246–250; p. 393", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 202 (27 October 1363; Niphon 'confined in the monastery of Soumela in Trebizond'; died 19 March 1364, Holy Tuesday)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 202 n. 115 (Panaretos names two Soumela monasteries, in Matzouka and in Trebizond; the latter perhaps a city metochion)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 255 (Metropolitan Niphon died of pleurisy at Soumela on 18 March 1364)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Rosenqvist (1996, p. 31) gives Niphon's death as 18 March 1364; Panaretos' 19 March is the Holy Tuesday of 1364.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Soumela monastery (Mt Mela)", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.658, "role": "confinement (or a Soumela dependency in the city: see disputed)"}, {"name": "Chrysokephalos church (Ortahisar Fatih Mosque), Trabzon (approx.)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.719, "role": "burial"}, {"name": "Matzouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.81, "lon": 39.61, "role": "victory 1361"}]}, {"id": "H4-044", "date": "1364", "date_text": "1364 (chrysobull for Soumela dated December 1364; Kyriakidis 1898 prints 1365)", "precision": "year", "title": "Soumela refounded by Alexios III; Venetian depot", "summary": "Alexios III restored the monastery of the Virgin at Soumela in the Matzouka valley; a chrysobull (dated 1364 or 1365) confirms its lands and tax exemptions and names its dependent peasants. In the same year the Venetians had their privileges confirmed and received a depot in the city. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 468-476) adds an inscription of AM 6868 (1360) calling Alexios III 'new founder' ('Κτίτωρ... νέος'), the gifts of John II, Alexios II and Basil (40 paroikoi, 24 estates), and Manuel III's relic of the Cross (AM 6898, 1390). The chrysobull is dated December, indiction 3, AM 6873, i.e. December 1364 (Kyriakidis 1898, whose own text says 1365).", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysobull of Alexios III for Soumela (Miklosich & Muller, Acta et diplomata V)", "Venetian documents of 1364 (Diplomatarium Veneto-Levantinum II)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 254-255 (Alexios III rebuilt Soumela in 1360-65; the chrysobull of December 1364, granted in gratitude for his rescue from shipwreck, lists the kapalion of Doubera and holdings in Matzouka, Sourmaina and Gemora with forty paroikoi and the watchtower of Doubera)", "Bryer 1980", "Karpov 1981", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 468–476; p. 470", "Bryer 1980, VI ('The Late Byzantine Monastery in Town and Countryside', 1979) 229 ('In 1364 he refounded Soumela')", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 419 and n. 1 (1364 refoundation; editions of the Soumela bull: Fallmerayer 1843, pp. 92-100; Kausokalybites & Metaxopoulos 1775, pp. 45-52; Kyriakides 1898, pp. 66-74; Lampros 1907; Miklosich-Müller V 280 ff.)", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 170 (Alexios III gave the monastery of St Theodore Gabras to the Venetian quarter in 1364)", "Bryer 1980, VIII ('Shipping in the Empire of Trebizond', 1966) 12 (the Venetian treaty of 1364)", "Zakythinos 1932, Le chrysobulle d'Alexis III Comnène en faveur des Vénitiens (cited by Asp-Talwar 2016)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 200 (5 May 1361: the emperor and his mother at Soumela in Matzouka during the eclipse)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The founding of Soumela in 386 by the Athenian monks Barnabas and Sophronios is monastic tradition. Bryer doubts the authenticity of the chrysobulls for Vazelon and Choutoura, but uses this one without reservation.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Soumela monastery", "lat": 40.69, "lon": 39.658, "role": "monastery"}, {"name": "Matzouka (Macka)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.613, "role": "bandon of Matzouka"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Venetian depot"}]}, {"id": "C2-028", "date": "1364", "date_end": "1368-07-19", "date_text": "1364; returned 13 April 1365; left the throne 12 (or 19) November 1367; died before December 1369", "precision": "day", "title": "Joseph Lazaropoulos, metropolitan and hagiographer", "summary": "Joseph Lazaropoulos (as a layman John), author of the miracles of St Eugenios, had escorted the young Alexios III to Trebizond in 1349. He was elected metropolitan in 1364, was consecrated in Constantinople, returned to the city on Easter Sunday, 13 April 1365, and left the throne in November 1367 for the Eleousa monastery by the Daphnous harbour; he died before December 1369. Chrysanthos shows that the 'John' of 1166 in older lists is his lay name.", "people": ["Joseph Lazaropoulos", "Alexios III Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle", "Patriarchal acts 1364–1368 (Miklosich–Müller)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 207–211; pp. 250–254", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 202 (election 1364; Easter 13 April 1365)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 203 ('On 12 November 6876 (1367)... left the see'; 'Around 19 July... 1368, I went to Constantinople on account of the raids that the azapic paraskalmia carried out on the Araniotai', n. 118)", "Rosenqvist 1996, pp. 30-31 (life of Lazaropoulos; consecration by Patriarch Philotheos after 8 October 1364; resignation November 1367; died before December 1369)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 129-130 (Lazaropoulos resigned on 12 November 1367 and retired to the Eleousa monastery near the Genoese arsenal on the Daphnous beach east of St Sabbas, not to the island of Ares; the Ottoman raid on Ares in July 1368, the first appearance of the Ottomans in Trapezuntine history, prompted an embassy \"by Panaretos, it seems\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "He left the throne on 12 November 1367 (Panaretos) or 19 November (Chrysanthos). Why he resigned is unknown; Chrysanthos suspected a break with Alexios III. Bryer & Winfield (1985) place his Eleousa monastery by the Daphnous beach in Trebizond, not on the island of Ares.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "see"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "departure 1368"}]}, {"id": "C2b-07", "date": "1369-01-06", "date_text": "Epiphany (6 January) 1369; Golacha retaken briefly 16 April 1374", "precision": "day", "title": "Golacha falls and Chaldia is laid waste", "summary": "On Epiphany 1369 the Turks took the fortress of Golacha in Chaldia by a ruse, and 'Chaldia was obliterated': some died fighting, others in 'the treacherous cave there'. Golacha had survived a sixteen-day siege with a siege engine by Ahi Ayna Beg of Erzincan in October 1361, and in March 1368 the emir Kilij Arslan had blockaded Trapezuntine Chaldia; the Chaldians retook it on Sunday 16 April 1374, but it soon fell again. The losses south of the mountains, against the Turks of Erzincan and Bayburt rather than the Turkmen of the coast, were the empire's main frontier problem in Alexios III's later reign.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Ahi Ayna Beg of Erzincan", "Kilij Arslan (emir, 1368)"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for October AM 6870, March AM 6876, January AM 6877 and 16 April AM 6882"], "sources_modern": ["Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 201 (October 1361)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 204 (January 1369)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 205 (16 April 1374)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 137 (Alexios III turning to save Chaldia and Cheriana from the Turks of Erzincan and Bayburt)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 308 (Golacha (Colosana) castle, about 10 km west of modern Gümüşhane above the İkisu Dere; \"Golacha is now Bahçelik\")", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 302 (1361 siege, Epiphany 1369 capture, the cave on the Cheriana road, April 1374 recapture, retaken by 1404; Golacha = Clavijo's Dorileh, ducal seat of Chaldia)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 172 (Golacha \"guards the road from Cheriana into Chaldia\"; siege dated 1362 there)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield (1985) identify Golacha, with a reservation ('If we are also correct'), with the castle at Colosana, now Bahçelik (Torul), about 10 km west of Gümüşhane, high above the İkisu Dere on the road from Cheriana into Chaldia; they take it for Clavijo's 'Dorileh' of 1404, the seat of the Kabazites dukes, and a large cave on the Cheriana road just south of it for Panaretos' 'treacherous cave'. They attribute the siege of 1361 once to the emir of Bayburt and once to the ahi of Erzincan, and add that after 1374 the Turks retook it almost at once and it was recovered again at some date before 1404.", "places": [{"name": "Golacha castle (Colosana castle above Bahçelik, Torul, Gümüşhane)", "lat": 40.4727, "lon": 39.3284, "role": "the fortress taken by the Turks on Epiphany 1369; mapped at Bahçelik village (Nişanyan), the castle stands high above the west bank of the İkisu Dere (Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 308); approximate"}]}, {"id": "C2-029", "date": "1370-08-13", "date_end": "1391", "date_text": "Enthroned 13 August 1370; died c. 1391", "precision": "day", "title": "Metropolitan Theodosios, brother of St Dionysios", "summary": "Theodosios, a monk of Philotheou and of the Mangana, brother of St Dionysios (founder of Dionysiou on Athos), became 'hypertimos and exarch of all Lazike' (act of 31 January 1370) and was enthroned on 13 August 1370. He baptized the future Alexios IV (Basil) in June 1382 and died about 1391.", "people": ["Theodosios of Trapezous", "Dionysios of Athos", "Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Alexios IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle", "Patriarchal act of 31 January 1370 (Miklosich–Müller)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 211; pp. 254–256", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 204 (Tuesday 13 August 1370: Theodosios from Thessaloniki, twenty years on Athos, hegumen of the Mangana)"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "see"}, {"name": "Dionysiou monastery, Mount Athos", "lat": 40.172, "lon": 24.236, "role": "brother's foundation"}]}, {"id": "H4-045", "date": "1374", "date_text": "1374", "precision": "year", "title": "Alexios III founds Dionysiou on Mount Athos", "summary": "Alexios III's chrysobull founding the monastery of Dionysiou on Athos survives in the monastery with painted portraits of the emperor and his empress Theodora Kantakouzene. Its signature gives the full title 'faithful emperor and autocrat of all the East, the Iberians and the Perateia'. It is the best single image of a Trapezuntine ruler.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Theodora Kantakouzene", "Dionysios (founder monk)"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysobull of Alexios III for Dionysiou (Oikonomides 1968, Actes de Dionysiou, no. 4)"], "sources_modern": ["Oikonomides 1968", "Bryer 1980", "Bryer 1980, VI ('The Late Byzantine Monastery in Town and Countryside', 1979) 228-229 (foundation grant of about 19,000 Trebizond aspers and 1,000 a year)", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 420", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 32 ('in 1374 emperor Alexios III financed the building of the Dionysiou monastery')", "Eastmond 2004, Art and Identity in Thirteenth-Century Byzantium, p. n. 14 (Karakatsanis 1997, cat. 2.29; Oikonomides 1968, no. 4)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The month is not known.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Dionysiou monastery, Mount Athos", "lat": 40.172, "lon": 24.287, "role": "monastery founded"}]}, {"id": "H4-046", "date": "1376", "date_text": "14th century (Bryer: refounded 1376)", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Theoskepastos convent", "summary": "The convent of the Theoskepastos ('protected by God'), built into a cave on Mount Minthrion (Boztepe) east of the city, was founded or refounded by Irene, mother of Alexios III, who was painted there as founder with her son and his empress Theodora Kantakouzene; Alexios himself probably endowed it in 1376. That year his illegitimate son, the despot Andronikos, who died after a fall from the palace on 14 March 1376, was buried there, and in 1843 an abbess had Irene's portrait repainted as Andronikos. Manuel III was buried there in 1417. It remained a Greek convent until 1923.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Theodora Kantakouzene", "Irene (mother of Alexios III)"], "sources_primary": ["Donor portraits and inscriptions (known partly from 19th-century records)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985", "Bryer 1980", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 442", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 52 (Irene the founder; the 1843 repainting; Andronikos buried there in 1376)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 205 (14 March 1376: Andronikos buried in the Theoskepastos)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 207 (Manuel III buried in the Theoskepastos, 1417)", "Bryer 1980, VI ('The Late Byzantine Monastery in Town and Countryside', 1979) 229 ('probably in 1376 to refound the Theoskepastos')", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 419 ('1376. Alexios III may endow the Theoskepastos')", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 244-245 (the nunnery \"probably founded, refounded, or endowed by Eirene of Trebizond in the 1340s\"; portraits of Alexios III, Theodora and Eirene with a model of the church, replaced in 1843 by Theodora, Alexios and Andronikos; tombs of the despot Andronikos, d. 14 March 1376, Manuel III, d. 1417, and Alexios IV, d. 1429; the only known nunnery of the empire, women religious until 1922); p. 184 (in 1376 one could fall to one's death from the palace)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer's date of 1376, given as 'probably', seems tied to the burial of Andronikos that year; the founder was Irene, mother of Alexios III (Eastmond 2016; Chrysanthos), and Bryer & Winfield (1985) date her founding, refounding or endowment to the 1340s. They add that Alexios IV (died 1429) was buried there before his remains were moved to a tomb outside the Chrysokephalos, and that the visible paintings appear to be of the 14th century.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Theoskepastos convent (Kizlar Manastiri; approximate)", "lat": 41.0, "lon": 39.739, "role": "convent"}]}, {"id": "H4-047", "date": "1379-10-08", "date_text": "8 October 1379", "precision": "day", "title": "Eudokia married to Taceddin, emir of Limnia", "summary": "Taceddin Çelebi, the Turkmen who was taking over Limnia and the Iris delta, had asked for a Komnenian bride in June 1362 and been refused. After years of embassies Alexios III married his daughter Eudokia to him at Oinaion on 8 October 1379; 'at that time the emperor gained control over Limnia', probably as her dowry, though in fact it was lost. Other daughters went to Mutahharten, emir of Erzincan, to Osman Kara Yülük of the Aq Qoyunlu and to Suleyman of Chalybia, and one, Anna, to King Bagrat V of Georgia (1367). The princesses ringed the shrinking empire with in-laws.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Eudokia Megale Komnene", "Taceddin Beg", "Mutahharten", "Qara Osman", "Bagrat V of Georgia"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for 1379"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975", "Zachariadou 1979", "Shukurov 2016", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 79–88; p. 89", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 206 (14 August and 8 October 1379)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 203 (Anna and Bagrat V, 1367)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 129-131, 136 (Limnia; the 1362 request; dowry)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 149 nn. 142-143 (Osman Kara Yülük, Doukas; Mutahharten, the only one of three proposed Erzincan marriages certain enough to include)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 98, 102 (Alexios III betrothed Eudokia to Taceddin at Oinaion on 8 October 1379; Limnia \"perhaps\" her dowry; Taceddin \"emir of Limnia\" by 1386; Limnia lost 1380-87)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The dates of the other marriages are not all recorded. Whether Limnia was a dowry ceded in 1379 or a district the emperor really recovered ('gained control', Panaretos) is debated; Bryer argues that it was already lost to Taceddin. Mutahharten's wife was a daughter of Alexios III or, less likely, of Manuel III (Bryer).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Limnia (near the Iris delta, Carsamba; location approximate)", "lat": 41.23, "lon": 36.73, "role": "emirate of Taceddin"}, {"name": "Neokaisareia (Niksar)", "lat": 40.59, "lon": 36.95, "role": "Taceddinoğulları centre"}, {"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.49, "role": "emirate of Mutahharten"}]}, {"id": "C2b-08", "date": "1380-03-04", "date_text": "February - Sunday 4 March 1380", "precision": "day", "title": "Alexios III's campaign against the Chepni", "summary": "At the end of the winter grazing season Alexios III attacked the Chepni Turkmen by land and sea. On Sunday 4 March 1380 his army split: 600 foot went in from Petroma and raided Kotzauta, while the emperor with the cavalry went up the Philabonites (Harsit) river to the Chepni winter quarters, burned their tents and freed many of 'our captured simylika' (captives or loot: the word is disputed). Over 100 Turkish men, women and children and about 42 Romans died. The Chepni stayed; the Philabonites valley became the Chepni nahiye after 1461.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Michael Panaretos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entry for February - 4 March AM 6888"], "sources_modern": ["Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 206 (and nn. 123-124)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 133 (the campaign went about 75 km inland, to Simikle/Sümükli yayla at 2,919 m)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 147-148 (translation)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 141 (translation of the 1380 entry: the emperor \"crossed the country of those who live along the upper course of the Philabonites River up to their winter camps\"; Petroma castle; Kotzauta tentatively the valleys round Kürtün and Suma Kale; the pastures and ruined village of Sümüklü, 9 km SW of Kürtün, the goal; Sthlabopiastes proposed at or near Vakfıkebir)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Whether the army 'spurred' along the river or 'crossed' it, and what the freed 'simylika' were, captives, loot or a place, are disputed readings (Lampsides; Bryer & Winfield; Asp-Talwar). Bryer & Winfield (1985) translate 'crossed', take 'simylika' for the Sümüklü pastures, and put the Chepni winter camps on the upper Philabonites 'though not south of Torul'; they place Petroma at the castle above the Harşit near Tirebolu, and propose the valleys round Kürtün for Kotzauta and Vakfıkebir for Sthlabopiastes, where the foot were to meet the emperor.", "places": [{"name": "Philabonites (Harsit) mouth at Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "landing and start of the campaign"}, {"name": "Philabonites (Harsit) valley: Ardasa (Torul)", "lat": 40.556, "lon": 39.295, "role": "upper valley (the Chepni winter quarters lay on the lower and western reaches; not located exactly)"}, {"name": "Petroma castle (Örenkaya, old Kale-i Bedrama, Tirebolu)", "lat": 40.9618, "lon": 38.8698, "role": "starting point of the 600 foot in 1380 (Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 141); village point below the castle; approximate"}, {"name": "Sthlabopiastes (Bryer & Winfield: at or near Vakfıkebir, mouth of the Fol Dere)", "lat": 41.0458, "lon": 39.2764, "role": "beach where the emperor was to meet the foot (Bryer & Winfield proposal); approximate"}]}, {"id": "C2b-09", "date": "1386-10-24", "date_end": "1386-11-21", "date_text": "Wednesday 24 October and 21 November 1386", "precision": "day", "title": "Two Turkmen sons-in-law at war; Timur takes Tbilisi", "summary": "On 24 October 1386 Alexios III's son-in-law Taceddin of Limnia invaded Chalybia with 12,000 men against his other son-in-law, Suleyman Beg, son of Hacı Emir; Taceddin was killed and cut to pieces, about 3,000 of his men died and 7,000 horses were abandoned. A month later, on 21 November 1386, Timur sacked Tbilisi and captured King Bagrat V with his wife, Alexios' daughter Anna, and their son David. The emperor's in-laws now fought over coastal castles and lands rather than the summer pastures, while a new power appeared in the east.", "people": ["Taceddin of Limnia", "Suleyman Beg of Chalybia", "Timur", "Bagrat V of Georgia", "Anna Megale Komnene"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle, entries for 24 October and 21 November AM 6895"], "sources_modern": ["Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 207", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 131", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 148 (translation)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 102 (October 1386: Taceddin of Limnia invaded Chalybia with 12,000 men against Süleyman Beg and lost his life, 3,000 men and 7,000 horses)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Panaretos' 'over 800,000' for Timur's army is a rumour, not a count.", "places": [{"name": "Chalybia (approximate centre at Bolaman)", "lat": 41.04, "lon": 37.59, "role": "battle"}, {"name": "Limnia (near the Iris delta, Carsamba; location approximate)", "lat": 41.23, "lon": 36.73, "role": "Taceddin's emirate"}, {"name": "Tbilisi", "lat": 41.693, "lon": 44.802, "role": "sacked by Timur"}]}, {"id": "H4-048", "date": "1390-03-20", "date_text": "20 March 1390", "precision": "day", "title": "Death of Alexios III; Panaretos' chronicle", "summary": "Alexios III died after a reign of forty years and was succeeded by Manuel III. His secretary Michael Panaretos (c. 1320-after 1390) wrote the short chronicle 'On the Grand Komnenoi', which covers 1204-1390 (with notes added to 1426) and is first-hand from 1340. Almost every date in this chapter's narrative comes from it.", "people": ["Alexios III Megas Komnenos", "Manuel III Megas Komnenos", "Michael Panaretos"], "sources_primary": ["Michael Panaretos, Chronicle (Marc. gr. 608; ed. Lampsides 1958; trans. Kennedy 2019)"], "sources_modern": ["Kennedy 2019, Two Works on Trebizond (DOML 52)", "Kennedy 2019, Byzantinische Zeitschrift 112, pp. 899-933", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 207 (died Sunday 20 March 1390, of the fifth week of Lent, aged 51, after 40 years and 3 months)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 208 (later entries to 1426)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 126 (24 of Alexios III's 30 recorded expeditions were by sea)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 18 (24 of Alexios III's 30 recorded expeditions by sea; his six land journeys in the immediate hinterland of Trebizond)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Kennedy (2019) argues that Panaretos chose what to record in order to flatter Alexios III and the empire's standing, so his silences mislead.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}]}, {"id": "C2-030", "date": "1393", "date_text": "Founded 752; deserted 1203; refounded 1393", "precision": "year", "title": "Peristera (St George Peristereota) refounded", "summary": "From the notes of Elias Konstantinides, Chrysanthos dates the monastery of St George Peristereota: founded 752, deserted in 1203 because of raids, refounded in 1393 by Theophanes, a native of Lazike and former hegumen of Soumela, who rebuilt the cells and church. Later, through the mediation of Bessarion, then staying in Trebizond, he obtained a chrysobull of Alexios IV; it was lost in the fire of 1495.", "people": ["Theophanes of Peristera", "Bessarion", "Alexios IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Notebook of notes by Elias Konstantinides (monastery tradition)"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 499–501", "Bryer 1980, IX ('Some Trapezuntine Monastic Obits', 1976) 127 (Peristera 'supposedly founded in 752, ravaged by barbarians and abandoned in 1203, refounded in 1393 and endowed by... Alexios IV'; the Soumelan Theophanes of Lazia)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "752 and 1203 are monastic tradition.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Peristera monastery (Kuştul), Maçka (approx.)", "lat": 40.883, "lon": 39.817, "role": "refounded"}]}, {"id": "C2b-11", "date": "1395-04-03", "date_text": "Born 3 April 1395 (not 4 April 1395 or 1396); in Italy from c. April 1416", "precision": "day", "title": "George of Trebizond born in Crete to a Trapezuntine family", "summary": "The humanist George of Trebizond (George Trapezountios) was born at Candia in Venetian Crete on 3 April 1395; his father told him that his great-great-grandfather had emigrated to Crete 'from Trebizond, some city of Pontus'. He signed himself 'Georgius Trapezuntius Cretensis' all his life. Invited by the Venetian Francesco Barbaro, he came to Italy about April 1416, became a leading teacher of rhetoric, a translator and a papal secretary, and a fierce opponent of Plethon and, in the end, of Bessarion. His name shows how far the memory of Trebizond reached in the Greek world of the 15th century.", "people": ["George of Trebizond", "Francesco Barbaro"], "sources_primary": ["George of Trebizond, Commentary on ps.-Ptolemy's Centiloquium, aphor. 58 (his birthday, 3 April)", "George of Trebizond, Comparatio philosophorum Aristotelis et Platonis (Venice 1523), f. R1r: 'proavum suum ex Trapezunte... in Cretam migrasse'"], "sources_modern": ["Monfasani 1976, George of Trebizond: a Biography and a Study of his Rhetoric and Logic, pp. 3-8 and nn. 2-4", "Alexander 2013, Honor, Reputation and Conflict: George of Trebizond and Humanist Acts of Self-Presentation (PhD, Kentucky), p. 2 and p. 170 n. 51", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 186 ('born in Candia in 1396')", "Malone-Lee 2023, pp. 23-25 (George of Trebizond against Plethon and Bessarion)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Monfasani (1976) dates his birth to 3 April 1395 from George's own horoscope note; older works (Gauricus, Garcaeus) give 4 April 1395 or 1396, and Bryer 1396. He came to Italy about April 1416 (Monfasani; two other dates in George's own notes are less likely).", "places": [{"name": "Candia (Heraklion), Crete", "lat": 35.339, "lon": 25.144, "role": "birthplace"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "the family's city of origin"}, {"name": "Venice", "lat": 45.438, "lon": 12.335, "role": "arrival in Italy, 1416"}]}, {"id": "H4-049", "date": "1396", "date_end": "1419", "date_text": "1392-1419 (Ottoman conquest of Canik); Kerasous challenged 1396/97", "precision": "year", "title": "The Ottomans reach the frontier; Hacıemiroğulları hold Kerasous", "summary": "Between 1392 and 1419 the Ottomans conquered Canik, the Turkmen principalities of the central Black Sea coast, bringing them to the empire's western edge and pushing the Turkmen emirates east. Suleyman Beg of Chalybia, son-in-law of Alexios III, challenged Kerasous in 1396/97; Turkish sources say he took it. In 1404 Clavijo put the Trapezuntine frontier at Tripolis, east of Kerasous.", "people": ["Bayezid I", "Suleyman Beg (Hacıemiroğulları)", "Manuel III Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Mehmed Neşri, Kitab-ı Cihan-nüma, ed. Unat & Köymen 1949, I, 323 (via Bryer V 130 n. 58)", "'Aziz ibn Ardashir Astarabadi, Bazm u Razm, ed. Giesecke 1940, p. 110 (via Bryer V 131 n. 61)"], "sources_modern": ["Zachariadou 1979", "Bryer 1975", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 130-131 and nn. 58, 61-62 (Neşri, ed. Unat & Köymen, I, 323; Bazm u Razm, ed. Giesecke, p. 110; Sümer, Oğuzlar, p. 328; Clavijo)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 148 (notes to Appendix I: Suleyman, son of the hacı emir, attacked Giresun in 1396/97)"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "occupied 1398-1400"}, {"name": "Amisos (Samsun), Canik", "lat": 41.29, "lon": 36.33, "role": "annexed by Bayezid I"}], "disputed": "That Süleyman took Kerasous rests on Turkish sources (through Sümer) and is 'a matter of conjecture' (Bryer)."}, {"id": "H4-050", "date": "1400", "date_end": "1402-07-28", "date_text": "1400-1402", "precision": "year", "title": "Manuel III submits to Timur", "summary": "As Timur advanced into Anatolia, Manuel III accepted his overlordship; Clavijo, passing in 1404, wrote that 'this emperor pays tribute to Timour Beg, and to other Turks, who are his neighbours' (tr. Markham). Timur's victory over Bayezid I at Ankara (28 July 1402) set the Ottomans back by a generation and gave Trebizond a last respite.", "people": ["Manuel III Megas Komnenos", "Timur", "Bayezid I"], "sources_primary": ["Ruy González de Clavijo, Embajada a Tamorlán (1404), tr. Markham 1859, p. 140 ('This emperor pays tribute to Timour Beg, and to other Turks, who are his neighbours')"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Karpov 2007", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 347 n. 72 ('no Byzantine, Trapezuntine or Western source confirms Timur's presence in Trebizond'; no Trapezuntine contingent known at Ankara)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 130-132 (by 1404 the emir Altamur and the Greek lord of Oinaion were tributaries of Timur)", "Malone-Lee 2023, pp. 11-12 (Manuel III sided with Timur and was spared after Ankara)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Markham's translation calls the emperor 'Manuel II' (Manuel III in modern numbering). Bryer finds no source that puts Timur in Trebizond and no Trapezuntine troops at Ankara; the submission rests on Clavijo and the Timurid historians.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ankara battlefield (Cubuk plain; approximate)", "lat": 40.08, "lon": 32.95, "role": "battle of 28 July 1402"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "Timur's vassal"}]}, {"id": "H4-051", "date": "1400", "date_text": "C. 1400", "precision": "decade", "title": "The empire about 1400", "summary": "About 1400 the empire held the coast from Kerasous or Tripolis to Makriali, with the districts of Matzouka and Palaiomatzouka up to the Zigana pass. Chaldia beyond the mountains was a nearly independent lordship of the Kabazites (Clavijo, 1404), and Cheriana was lost.", "people": ["Manuel III Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Ruy Gonzalez de Clavijo, Embajada a Tamorlan (on Chaldia and its lord)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985", "Zachariadou 1979", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 125-126, 131 (Chaldia under the Kabazitai, Tzanicha under the Tzanichitai; frontier at Tripolis in 1404)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The southern line is drawn on the watershed by convention, and whether Chaldia was a vassal or independent is debated.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Coast west of Kerasous", "lat": 40.93, "lon": 38.3, "role": "boundary vertex 1 (coast, western limit)"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "boundary vertex 2 (coast)"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "boundary vertex 3 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kordyle (Akcakale)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 39.48, "role": "boundary vertex 4 (coast)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "boundary vertex 5 (coast)"}, {"name": "Sourmaina (Surmene)", "lat": 40.91, "lon": 40.12, "role": "boundary vertex 6 (coast)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "boundary vertex 7 (coast)"}, {"name": "Athina (Pazar)", "lat": 41.18, "lon": 40.88, "role": "boundary vertex 8 (coast)"}, {"name": "Archabis (Arhavi)", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 41.3, "role": "boundary vertex 9 (coast)"}, {"name": "Makriali (Kemalpasa)", "lat": 41.485, "lon": 41.52, "role": "boundary vertex 10 (coast, eastern limit; approximate)"}, {"name": "Lazian ridge", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 41.55, "role": "boundary vertex 11 (inland)"}, {"name": "Kackar ridge", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 41.1, "role": "boundary vertex 12 (inland)"}, {"name": "Pontic watershed south of Rize", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 40.55, "role": "boundary vertex 13 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Of", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 40.1, "role": "boundary vertex 14 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Soumela", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 39.7, "role": "boundary vertex 15 (inland)"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "boundary vertex 16 (inland)"}, {"name": "Upper Tonya valley", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 39.1, "role": "boundary vertex 17 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Tripolis", "lat": 40.75, "lon": 38.7, "role": "boundary vertex 18 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills west of Kerasous", "lat": 40.8, "lon": 38.25, "role": "boundary vertex 19 (inland)"}, {"name": "Ardasa (Torul), Chaldia", "lat": 40.556, "lon": 39.295, "role": "Chaldia lordship vertex A (dependent polygon)"}, {"name": "Tzanicha (Canca, near Gumushane)", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.48, "role": "Chaldia lordship vertex B"}, {"name": "Eastern Chaldia", "lat": 40.35, "lon": 39.9, "role": "Chaldia lordship vertex C"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Of", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 40.1, "role": "Chaldia lordship vertex D"}, {"name": "South of Soumela", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 39.7, "role": "Chaldia lordship vertex E"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "Chaldia lordship vertex F"}]}, {"id": "C2b-10", "date": "1403-01-02", "date_text": "Received date 2 January 1403; other dates 1400-1408", "precision": "year", "title": "Bessarion born at Trebizond", "summary": "Bessarion, baptised Basil, was born at Trebizond to parents of modest means, Theodore and Theodula; his mother had fifteen children, of whom only he lived to adulthood. Entrusted as a boy to Metropolitan Dositheos, who adopted him, he went with him to Constantinople at about thirteen, became a monk in 1423, studied with Plethon at Mistra, was made metropolitan of Nicaea (1437) and, after the Council of Florence, a cardinal of the Roman Church. His lifelong friend George Amiroutzes was born at Trebizond about the same time. Bessarion never forgot his city: he wrote its encomium (H4-056), had Dositheos prayed for to the end of his life, and after 1461 was asked by Amiroutzes to help ransom his son (H5-001).", "people": ["Bessarion", "Dositheos of Trebizond", "George Amiroutzes"], "sources_primary": ["Death notice appended to Capranica's funeral oration (died 19 November 1472, aged 68 years, 10 months, 16 days)", "Michael Apostolis, funeral oration (PG 161, cxxxii-cxxxiv)", "Andrea Barbazza, on the baptismal name Basil", "Bessarion's missal, Vat. Barb. lat. 562 (names Theodula and Theodore)"], "sources_modern": ["Malone-Lee 2023, Cardinal Bessarion (1403-1472), pp. 10-13 and pp. 27-29 nn. 5-14", "Cattaneo 2020, Le lettere greche del Cardinal Bessarione, Introduzione, p. VI ('Nato a Trebisonda nel 1403 o, più probabilmente, nel 1408 con il nome di Basilio'; monk 20 July 1423; priest 8 October 1430)", "Chrysanthos 1933, pp. 268-271 (2 January 1403, after Vast; baptised John)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "His birth is dated 2 January 1403 (Vast; Chrysanthos; Kennedy 2018; Malone-Lee 2023; from his death notice and Capranica's oration), 2 January 1408 (Monfasani 1986; Cattaneo 2020, 'more probably', because Traversari calls him tricenarius in 1438), 1401 (Joannou 1947, from the canonical ages for his ordinations in 1426 and 1431) or 1399. His baptismal name was Basil (Barbazza; Malone-Lee; Cattaneo) or John (an autograph receipt; Chrysanthos); Mioni and Bianca take 'John' for a scribal error. His family was humble (Apostolis), or a branch of the imperial house (Braccini 2006, after a 17th-century source, 'to be treated with reserve', Malone-Lee).", "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "birthplace"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "education from c. 1416"}]}, {"id": "H4-052", "date": "1404-04-11", "date_text": "11 April 1404", "precision": "day", "title": "Clavijo's embassy passes through Trebizond", "summary": "Ruy Gonzalez de Clavijo, ambassador of Henry III of Castile to Timur, left Constantinople on 20 March 1404, landed at Trebizond on 11 April and went on over the mountains to Erzincan, Erzurum, Tabriz and Samarkand, which he reached on 4 September 1404. He described the walled city, the emperor Manuel III and his son Alexios, the Genoese and Venetian quarters, and the Greek lord of Chaldia (of the Kabazites family) who made travellers pay for passage. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 79-89), from Clavijo, names the lord of Chaldia at Doryle (Torul) as 'the duke kyr Leon Kabasites' ('δοὺξ κὺρ Λέων Καβασίτης'), with Chaldia reaching Alantza near Kelkit; Limnia, Eudokia's dowry of 1379, was independent under Arzamir; Erzincan, under Taharten (son-in-law of Alexios III), had mainly Greek and Armenian people.", "people": ["Ruy Gonzalez de Clavijo", "Manuel III Megas Komnenos", "Alexios IV Megas Komnenos", "Mutahharten of Erzincan"], "sources_primary": ["Ruy Gonzalez de Clavijo, Embajada a Tamorlan (ed. Lopez Estrada; trans. Le Strange 1928), chapters on Trebizond"], "sources_modern": ["Le Strange 1928", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 52-53 (Clavijo's route out of Trebizond: Palaiomatzouka, Zigana, Ardasa, the tower at Dipotamos, Dorileh, then over the mountains to Alansa); pp. 302-303 (the Kabazites duke at Dorileh = Golacha; the duke \"kyr Leon Kabazites\", third of his family as duke since 1355; tolls at Ardasa, Dorileh and Bayana; Çepni no-man's-land before Alansa)", "Manz & Dunaway 1992, 'Clavijo', Encyclopaedia Iranica", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 79–88; p. 89", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 125 and n. 38 (Clavijo's 'Quilileo Cauasica' is 'kyr Leon Kabazites', not 'Cyril Cabasica')", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 119 and 122 n. 26 (Clavijo's return through Hemşin and Sourmaina in September 1405; on 17 September 1405 he sailed in a Genoese hazelnut ship from Platana to Pera)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 131-132 (Clavijo on the Melissenos lord of Oinaion, tributary to Timur)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Bryer names the duke of Chaldia in 1404 once as Leon II Kabazites and once as John II Kabazites.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 1 (by sea)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 2 (landed April 1404)"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Ardasa (Torul)", "lat": 40.556, "lon": 39.295, "role": "waypoint 4 (Chaldia; approximate)"}, {"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.49, "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.27, "role": "waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Tabriz", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.29, "role": "waypoint 7"}, {"name": "Samarkand", "lat": 39.655, "lon": 66.96, "role": "waypoint 8 (destination)"}]}, {"id": "H4-053", "date": "1417-03-05", "date_end": "1429-04-26", "date_text": "5 March 1417 - 26 April 1429", "precision": "day", "title": "Alexios IV and the Turkmen marriages of the 15th century", "summary": "Alexios IV, co-emperor since 1395, succeeded on 5 March 1417. One daughter married Jihan Shah of the Qara Qoyunlu, and his daughter Maria became the third wife of John VIII Palaiologos in Constantinople (1427), a match negotiated by an embassy from Constantinople in which the young Bessarion took part (1425-1427). A marriage of another daughter to Ali Beg of the Aq Qoyunlu is often repeated but doubtful. Marriage was now the empire's main defence.", "people": ["Alexios IV Megas Komnenos", "Maria of Trebizond", "John VIII Palaiologos", "Jahan Shah (Qara Qoyunlu)"], "sources_primary": ["Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories (trans. Kaldellis 2014)", "Short chronicles"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975", "Woods 1999", "Miller 1926", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 149 nn. 144-145 (Jihan Shah: Chalkokondyles, Bonn ed., p. 462; Ali Beg 'dubious')", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 50 (accepts both Jihan Shah and Ali Beg)", "Malone-Lee 2023, p. 26 (Bessarion in John VIII's embassy to Alexios IV, 1425-1427, for the marriage with Maria)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 207 (Manuel III died 5 March 1417)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The marriage to Ali Beg is accepted by Eastmond and doubted by Bryer.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "court"}, {"name": "Tabriz", "lat": 38.08, "lon": 46.29, "role": "Qara Qoyunlu capital"}, {"name": "Amida (Diyarbakir)", "lat": 37.91, "lon": 40.24, "role": "Aq Qoyunlu centre"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "Maria marries John VIII"}]}, {"id": "C2-033", "date": "1422-11-13", "date_text": "Successor elected 13 November 1422, indiction 1 (manuscript note)", "precision": "day", "title": "Metropolitan Dositheos forced out; Bessarion's defence", "summary": "Dositheos, metropolitan from after 1408 and Bessarion's teacher, was forced by the resident synod of Constantinople to resign; a manuscript note records that a new metropolitan was elected on 13 November 1422 ('μηνὶ νοεμβρίῳ ιγ΄, ἰνδικτιῶνος α΄'). Bessarion wrote a defence of him. Chrysanthos takes the successor to be probably Dorotheos, who held the see to the fall and beyond.", "people": ["Dositheos of Trapezous", "Bessarion", "Dorotheos of Trapezous"], "sources_primary": ["Manuscript note on the election of November 1422 (as cited by Chrysanthos)", "Bessarion, speech in defence of Dositheos"], "sources_modern": ["Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 259–261; p. 259", "Malone-Lee 2023, p. 13 and p. 29 n. 19 (Dositheos driven out by Alexios IV, who installed his own man; offered Monemvasia, refused; Bessarion's defence probably written in 1422, ed. Chrysanthos, Archeion Pontou 9, 1939, pp. 3-42)"], "confidence": "medium", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "see"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "patriarchal synod"}]}, {"id": "H4-054", "date": "1426", "date_end": "1427", "date_text": "C. 1426-1427", "precision": "decade", "title": "Johann Schiltberger passes Trebizond", "summary": "Johann Schiltberger, a Bavarian captured at Nicopolis (1396) who served the Ottomans and then Timur's successors, escaped home by way of the Black Sea coast around 1427. His book has a short description of 'Trabesanda', its emperor and its vineyards.", "people": ["Johann Schiltberger"], "sources_primary": ["Johann Schiltberger, Reisebuch (trans. Telfer 1879), chapter on Trabesanda"], "sources_modern": ["Telfer 1879", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 132 n. 67 (Schiltberger, trans. Telfer 1879, pp. 41-42)", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 377 and n. 2 ('Soon after 1402 Schiltberger almost confined his description of Trebizond to the observation that it was \"fruitful in vineyards\"', Telfer p. 41)", "Bryer 1980, IV ('The Fate of George Komnenos', 1973) 347 n. 72 (Schiltberger 'was in Trebizond at the time' of Timur's campaign)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Whether he visited the city or describes it from hearsay is unclear, and the date is approximate.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bathys (Batumi)", "lat": 41.64, "lon": 41.63, "role": "waypoint 1 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 3"}]}, {"id": "C2b-12", "date": "1426", "date_end": "1444", "date_text": "Tower dated AM 6935 (1426/7) by an inscription read by Millet; inscriptions of 1429-1444; chapel frescoes 1443-1444", "precision": "decade", "title": "The tower of Hagia Sophia and its frescoes", "summary": "A tall free-standing tower was built west of Hagia Sophia in the last decades of the empire, probably as a belfry or lighthouse; Millet read the date AM 6935 (1426/7) on its east face, and portraits of Alexios IV and John IV were painted on that face, towards the church. Its upper chapel was painted in 1443-1444, with a dated inscription of 1443 and commemorative portraits of two turbaned officials dated 1 January, seventh indiction (1444): the last great work of art of the empire, from a time of stability and of Armenian traders passing through the city.", "people": ["Alexios IV Megas Komnenos", "John IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Inscriptions on the tower and in its chapel (Millet 1895, pp. 431-433)"], "sources_modern": ["Rapti, 'The Tower Frescoes: Art at the End of the Empires', in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, pp. 147, 161, 164-165, 168", "Eastmond 2016, 'Hagia Sophia at Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 59 (portraits of John IV and Alexios IV on the east wall)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 52 (frescoes commissioned 'by Alexios IV between 1426-7 and 1444')", "Mercier 1994, p. 15 and p. 24 n. 13 (AM 6935 = 1426/7 inscribed at the foot of the east face; not an observatory)", "Rosenqvist 1996, p. 24 n. 16 ('probably a lighthouse or possibly a belfry')", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 233 (a graffito states that the building of the tower began in 1426/27; chapel paintings dated 1442/43 and an epitaph of January 1444; the south figure of the imperial panel is \"emperor son of an emperor Alexios\", i.e. John IV, the north figure proposed as Alexios IV, \"who began building the tower which his son would have finished\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The tower is dated 1426/7 from Millet's reading of its inscription (followed by Mercier 1994 and Eastmond 2016), or between 1429 and 1444, the limits set by the other inscriptions (Rapti 2016). It was probably a belfry or a lighthouse; the idea that it housed the observatory of a Trapezuntine 'academy' (Papadopoulos) is rejected.", "places": [{"name": "Hagia Sophia, Trebizond (tower)", "lat": 41.003, "lon": 39.697, "role": "the tower stands west of the church"}]}, {"id": "H4-055", "date": "1429-04-26", "date_text": "26 April 1429", "precision": "day", "title": "Alexios IV murdered; John IV takes power", "summary": "Alexios IV was killed in a conspiracy that put his son John IV on the throne, and contemporaries believed John was behind it. John IV reigned until 1460 and was the last effective emperor of Trebizond.", "people": ["Alexios IV Megas Komnenos", "John IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Pero Tafur, Andancas e viajes (on the rumour of parricide)", "Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories"], "sources_modern": ["Miller 1926", "Vasiliev 1932 (on Tafur)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 135 ('the parricidal John IV')", "Bryer 1980, IX ('Some Trapezuntine Monastic Obits', 1976) 129 (relics of Alexios IV, d. 1429, at St Gregory of Nyssa)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 163 (Ahanda, now Kavaklı, just west of Akçaabat on the coast road, proposed as Chalkokondyles' Achantos where John IV had his father killed in his tent by night)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "John IV's responsibility rests on reports, not proof.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}]}, {"id": "H4-056", "date": "1436", "date_end": "1437", "date_text": "C. 1436-1437", "precision": "year", "title": "Bessarion's Encomium of Trebizond", "summary": "Bessarion, born at Trebizond (see C2b-10) and later a cardinal, wrote a long encomium of his home city, describing its walls, palace, harbours, trade, churches and the surrounding valleys. It is rhetoric, but it is also the fullest picture of the city before its fall. Chrysanthos (1933), after Vast, dates Bessarion's birth to 2 January 1403 and has him baptised John, taken to Constantinople by Metropolitan Dositheos in 1415/16, made a monk in 1423, taught by Plethon at Mistra, and sent by John VIII to Trebizond to negotiate his marriage with Maria Komnene.", "people": ["Bessarion"], "sources_primary": ["Bessarion, Encomium of Trebizond (ed. Lampros 1916; trans. Kennedy 2019)"], "sources_modern": ["Kennedy 2019", "Monfasani, BMCR review of Kennedy 2019", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), pp. 268–271", "Malone-Lee 2023, pp. 10-11 (written 'possibly when he was a monk in Constantinople in about 1436'; 'our forefathers', 'our city')", "Cattaneo 2020, Le lettere greche del Cardinal Bessarione, p. 110 (in letters of 1450-1455 Bessarion asks for books to be sought in 'what was anciently our city', Trebizond)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Kennedy leaves the date open (an exercise written at Mistra or Constantinople); Monfasani argues for Constantinople 'in or near 1437', after Bessarion's return from Mistra; Malone-Lee (2023) says 'possibly... in about 1436'. On his name and birth see C2b-10.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "subject of the encomium"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "probable place of writing"}]}, {"id": "H4-057", "date": "1437", "date_end": "1438", "date_text": "1437-1438", "precision": "year", "title": "Pero Tafur visits Trebizond", "summary": "The Castilian traveller Pero Tafur came to Trebizond from Constantinople and met John IV; he repeated the story that John had killed his father. His account adds colour on the court and the city's dealings with the Turks.", "people": ["Pero Tafur", "John IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Pero Tafur, Andancas e viajes (trans. Letts 1926) (pages)"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1932, 'Pero Tafur, a Spanish traveler of the fifteenth century and his visit to Constantinople, Trebizond and Italy', Byzantion 7", "Bryer 1980, VII ('The Estates of the Empire of Trebizond', 1979) 377-378 (Tafur 'was there in 1438'; Letts p. 134)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 121 n. 22, 135 n. 79 (Tafur, trans. Letts 1926, p. 131: population of 4,000; John IV's claim to have married a Turk)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The order of Tafur's journeys on the Black Sea is uncertain.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "waypoint 1"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Caffa (Feodosia)", "lat": 45.03, "lon": 35.38, "role": "waypoint 3 (Tafur also visited Caffa)"}]}, {"id": "H4-058", "date": "1438", "date_end": "1439", "date_text": "1438-1439", "precision": "year", "title": "Trapezuntines at the Council of Ferrara-Florence", "summary": "At the council that tried to unite the Churches, Bessarion (by then metropolitan of Nicaea) led the unionists; Dorotheos, metropolitan of Trebizond, and George Amiroutzes, John IV's envoy, also took part. George of Trebizond, born at Candia in Crete in 1395 to a family whose ancestor had come from Trebizond (see C2b-11), was by then among the leading Greek scholars in Italy. Chrysanthos (1933, pp. 174, 212-216), after Syropoulos, has Metropolitan Dorotheos 'holding the place of Caesarea', first among the metropolitans, and makes him an opponent of the union who refused the patriarchate ('οὔτε καλή μοι φαίνεται ἡ ἕνωσις αὕτη').", "people": ["Bessarion", "Dorotheos of Trebizond", "George Amiroutzes", "George of Trebizond"], "sources_primary": ["Sylvester Syropoulos, Memoirs", "Acts of the Council of Florence"], "sources_modern": ["Gill 1959, The Council of Florence", "Monfasani 1976, George of Trebizond", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 174; pp. 212–216; pp. 271–273", "Malone-Lee 2023, pp. 12-13 and n. 15 (Amiroutzes born at Trebizond c. 1400, a lay philosopher in the emperor's train and an ally of Bessarion at the council; the 'recantation' attributed to him is a 16th-century document, Gill 1964)", "Cattaneo 2020, pp. 17-18 n. 38 (Amiroutzes among the supporters of the anti-unionist faction)", "Monfasani 1976, pp. 3-8"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Dorotheos is an opponent of the union in Chrysanthos (after Syropoulos), but some lists count him among the signers. Amiroutzes is an ally of Bessarion and the unionists in Malone-Lee (2023), a supporter of their opponents in Cattaneo (2020).", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ferrara", "lat": 44.838, "lon": 11.62, "role": "council 1438"}, {"name": "Florence", "lat": 43.77, "lon": 11.256, "role": "council 1439"}]}, {"id": "C2b-13", "date": "1450", "date_end": "1459", "date_text": "1450s (before Cüneyd's death in 1460)", "precision": "decade", "title": "Sheikh Cüneyd of Ardabil attacks Trebizond", "summary": "In the 1450s Sheikh Cüneyd (Junayd) of Ardabil, head of the Safavid order, attacked Trebizond. John IV found his capital almost empty: by Chalkokondyles' account some 4,000 citizens had 'simply melted away' into the valleys, leaving about fifty men, whom he called 'women, cowards and traitors'. Cüneyd's son Haydar later married Martha, daughter of Uzun Hasan and Theodora of Trebizond; their son Ismail founded Safavid Iran (H4-061).", "people": ["Sheikh Cüneyd (Junayd) of Ardabil", "John IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories, Bonn ed., pp. 462-466 (via Bryer V 120-121)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 120-121 and n. 20", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 148 n. 139 (the campaign 'fought over the same area' as Alexios III's of 1380; Hinz 1948, pp. 19-22)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 148-149 (genealogy: Cüneyd, invader of Trebizond in the 1450s, d. 1460; Haydar, Mar(t)a, the Safavids)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 63-64 (Cüneyd of Erdebil reached Kordyle by outflanking the central routes to the west through Turkmen territory, \"a few years\" before 1456 and \"a decade or more\" before 1461)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Bryer reads the empty city as the Pontic habit of taking to the forests, not cowardice.", "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "attacked"}, {"name": "Ardabil", "lat": 38.25, "lon": 48.293, "role": "seat of the Safavid order"}]}, {"id": "H4-059", "date": "1453-05-29", "date_text": "29 May 1453", "precision": "day", "title": "Constantinople falls; Trebizond the last Roman throne", "summary": "Mehmed II took Constantinople. Trebizond, with the despots of the Morea, was now the last Greek state of the Roman tradition, and John IV began to pay tribute to the Ottomans.", "people": ["Mehmed II", "Constantine XI Palaiologos", "John IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Doukas; Kritoboulos; Sphrantzes (on the fall of Constantinople)"], "sources_modern": ["Babinger 1978", "Nicol 1993"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "taken by Mehmed II"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "last Roman throne"}]}, {"id": "H4-060", "date": "1456", "date_end": "1458", "date_text": "1456-1458", "precision": "year", "title": "Ottoman raid and tribute", "summary": "Hizir Pasha, the Ottoman governor of Amasya, raided the countryside of Trebizond in 1456 and carried off about 2,000 people; John IV submitted and agreed to pay 2,000 gold pieces a year. In 1458 the tribute was raised to 3,000. These figures are Chalkokondyles'.", "people": ["Hizir Pasha", "John IV Megas Komnenos", "Mehmed II"], "sources_primary": ["Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories II.222 (trans. Kaldellis 2014, vol. II, p. 313)", "Doukas"], "sources_modern": ["Babinger 1978", "Miller 1926, pp. 85-87", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 63 (in 1456 Haydar Pasha of Amasya could attack Trebizond only by sea, the central passes being blocked, \"demonstrating that a marine siege was not enough to take it\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The captives and the tribute are Chalkokondyles' figures. Miller (1926) has the tribute raised to 3,000 when David ratified the agreement in 1458. Bryer & Winfield (1985) call the Ottoman commander Haydar Pasha of Amasya and say that he attacked by sea because the passes were blocked; they give no figures.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amaseia (Amasya)", "lat": 40.65, "lon": 35.83, "role": "base of Hizir Pasha"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "raided"}]}, {"id": "H4-061", "date": "1458", "date_text": "C. 1458", "precision": "year", "title": "Theodora (Despina Khatun) marries Uzun Hasan", "summary": "John IV betrothed his daughter Theodora to Uzun Hasan, ruler of the Aq Qoyunlu, in 1457 and married her to him in 1458, perhaps at Diyarbakir, with David (her uncle; Bryer writes 'brother') as her escort. The terms are known: a dowry of crown and monastic land in the villages of Halanik and Sesera in the banda of Matzouka and Sourmaina (not 'Cappadocia', as was claimed to Mehmed II); Uzun Hasan's undertaking to protect Trebizond, which he did not keep in 1461; and her right to remain Orthodox with her own chaplain and household. Known as Despina Khatun, she held court at Harput, was buried in the church of St George at Diyarbakir, and through her daughter Martha became the grandmother of Shah Ismail I, founder of Safavid Iran; two of her daughters still spoke Trebizond Greek in Damascus in 1512.", "people": ["Theodora Megale Komnene (Despina Khatun)", "Uzun Hasan", "John IV Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories (trans. Kaldellis 2014)", "Venetian reports on Uzun Hasan", "Accounts of Caterino Zeno, Iosafat Barbaro, Ambrogio Contarini and a 'Merchant in Persia' (Travels to Tana and Persia, Hakluyt Society 1873), on Despina Khatun (via Bryer V 149 n. 146)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer 1975", "Woods 1999", "Kursanskis (on Uzun Hasan and Trebizond)", "Babinger 1978, p. 185", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 136, 149 n. 146 (betrothal 1457, marriage 1458; dowry villages from Ottoman registers; Venetian and Italian witnesses: Caterino Zeno, Barbaro, Contarini, in Hakluyt Society ser. 1, 49)", "Bryer 1980, X ('Ludovico da Bologna and the Georgian and Anatolian Embassy', 1965) 197 (notes to the appendix) (her daughters in Damascus in 1512)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 148 (Koloneia passed to Uzun Hasan in 1459; \"perhaps\" he regarded it as part of the \"Cappadocian\" dowry of Theodora)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 323 (Halanik, now Zeytinli, just west of Hamurgan, a substantial village of Sourmaina, formed part of Theodora's dowry in 1458 and remained Christian until 1923); p. 253 (dowry properties at Sira/Sesera, now Günay, in Matzouka)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Dated 1458, with the betrothal in 1457 (Bryer; Babinger 1978); other accounts range from 1456 to 1460.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "bride's city"}, {"name": "Amida (Diyarbakir), Uzun Hasan's capital", "lat": 37.91, "lon": 40.24, "role": "groom's capital"}, {"name": "Halanik (now Zeytinli, Sürmene)", "lat": 40.9141, "lon": 40.088, "role": "dowry village of 1458 (Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 323)"}, {"name": "Sesera (now Günay, Maçka)", "lat": 40.89572, "lon": 39.70286, "role": "dowry properties of 1458 (Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 253, as Sira, now Günay)"}]}, {"id": "H4-062", "date": "1460-04", "date_text": "April 1460", "precision": "month", "title": "John IV dies; David seizes the throne", "summary": "John IV died in April 1460, before 22 April (Ganchou 2000), and his brother David pushed aside the young heir Alexios V with the backing of the archons. David sent envoys west for a crusade; the friar Ludovico da Bologna toured European courts in 1460-1461 with envoys claiming to speak for Trebizond, Georgia and Persia. Ludovico had left the Caucasus about January 1460 with two Georgian envoys; in Florence on 15 December 1460 the merchant Michael Alighieri, a descendant of Dante acting for David, signed a treaty granting Florence a consulate, warehouse and the Genoese and Venetian trading terms in Trebizond.", "people": ["John IV Megas Komnenos", "David Megas Komnenos", "Alexios V Megas Komnenos", "Ludovico da Bologna", "Michael Alighieri"], "sources_primary": ["Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories (trans. Kaldellis 2014)", "Western letters of the embassy"], "sources_modern": ["Ganchou 2000, Byzantinische Zeitschrift 93, pp. 113-124", "Bryer 1965, 'Ludovico da Bologna and the Georgian and Anatolian embassy of 1460-1461', Bedi Kartlisa 19-20", "Babinger 1978", "Bryer 1980, X ('Ludovico da Bologna and the Georgian and Anatolian Embassy', 1965) 178-198 (pp. 181-182: departure c. January 1460; p. 185: Alighieri and the Florentine treaty of 15 December 1460; letter in David's name dated 22 April 1459, perhaps Alighieri's own work)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 184 (Fallmerayer alone read the date of an inscription of John IV on the citadel tower as 1460; \"perhaps he was mistaken\"; John IV usually taken to have died in 1458)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "John IV's death is dated 1458, 1459 or 1460 in different works. Ganchou (2000) fixes it in April 1460, before 22 April, from a Genoese memorandum of 19 April with a postscript of 5 May reporting his death; the year 1459 in a letter of Ludovico da Bologna is a copying error. Bryer suspects that a letter in David's name dated 22 April 1459 was composed by Alighieri himself.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "capital"}, {"name": "Florence", "lat": 43.77, "lon": 11.256, "role": "treaty with David's envoy (1460)"}]}, {"id": "H4-063", "date": "1461-05", "date_end": "1461-08", "date_text": "Spring-summer 1461", "precision": "month", "title": "Mehmed II's land campaign to Trebizond", "summary": "Mehmed II crossed to Bursa, gathered the armies of Europe and Asia and marched by Ankara and Sivas, taking Koyulhisar from the Aq Qoyunlu. Near Erzincan he met Uzun Hasan's mother Sara Khatun and made peace with Uzun Hasan, but refused to include Trebizond in it; the army then crossed the Pontic Alps in hard summer rain. The size of the army is unknown: the '80,000 foot and 60,000 horse' often quoted is a modern figure (Freely 2009, p. 67), not a chronicle one.", "people": ["Mehmed II", "Mahmud Pasha Angelovic", "Sara Khatun", "Uzun Hasan"], "sources_primary": ["Kritoboulos, History of Mehmed the Conqueror IV (trans. Riggs 1954)", "Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories 9 (trans. Kaldellis 2014)", "Tursun Beg, Tarih-i Ebu'l-Feth (trans. Inalcik & Murphey 1978)", "Konstantin Mihailovic, Memoirs of a Janissary (trans. Stolz & Soucek 1975)"], "sources_modern": ["Babinger 1978, Mehmed the Conqueror and His Time", "Inalcik 1960, Speculum 35", "Topalidis 2024", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 132 and n. 68 (Mehmed's army 'suffered as much from rainstorms as from \"Wuna Greeks and Turks\"' in Chalybia; Aşıkpaşazade, trans. Kreutel, p. 226)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 60-65 (excursus by Bryer on Mehmed II's routes in 1461, from Tursun Beg: left Edirne after 23 March; Bursa, where Mahmud was on 21 April; Ankara, documents of 12-21 May; Kastamonu and Sinope before about 10 July; Tokat, Sivas, Koyulhisar taken from Uzun Hasan; camp on the Yassı Çimen; Sara Hatun pleaded there or on the Barkar/Bulgar Dağı; the armies divided about 28 July; an eighteen-day mountain crossing in rain)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 148 (Mehmed took Koyulhisar in 1461 but \"prudently skirted Koloneia\")"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The route between Sivas and the coast is vague in the sources (Kritoboulos, Doukas, Mihailović). Bryer & Winfield (1985) argue that neither army can have used the main road through the Torul (Ardasa) defile and the Zigana, which the Kabazites still held: Mahmud Pasha took 'the left-hand way' west through Kelkit and Cheriana, over the Balaban Dağı to Matsera (Alınyayla) and the Chepni pastures, entering Matzouka at Maçka; Mehmed took 'the right-hand way' east, probably up the Kara Dere valley to Araklı and along the coast, arriving a day after Mahmud. They call this a proposal from Tursun Beg's eyewitness account and the terrain. They give the fleet 100 to 300 ships with cannon, and doubt that the armies had 'the 140,000 cavalry and infantry claimed for them'.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bursa (Prousa)", "lat": 40.183, "lon": 29.061, "role": "waypoint 1 (muster)"}, {"name": "Ankara (Ankyra)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Sivas (Sebasteia)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.017, "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Koyulhisar", "lat": 40.3, "lon": 37.83, "role": "waypoint 4 (taken from the Aq Qoyunlu)"}, {"name": "Meeting with Sara Khatun, near Erzincan (approximate)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.3, "role": "waypoint 5 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Ardasa (Torul)", "lat": 40.556, "lon": 39.295, "role": "waypoint 6 (probable)"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "waypoint 7"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "waypoint 8 (goal)"}]}, {"id": "H4-064", "date": "1461-06", "date_text": "Summer 1461", "precision": "month", "title": "Sinope surrenders to Mehmed II", "summary": "Mehmed demanded Sinope from its Turkish lord Ismail Bey of the Candaroğulları ('Tell your father that I want Sinope', says Doukas). Ismail gave up the city without a fight and was compensated with lands in Thrace; the admiral Kasim Pasha took charge of it and the fleet sailed on east.", "people": ["Mehmed II", "Ismail Bey (Candaroğulları)", "Kasim Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Doukas, Historia Turcobyzantina 45 (trans. Magoulias 1975)", "Kritoboulos IV (trans. Riggs 1954)"], "sources_modern": ["Babinger 1978", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 61 (Mehmed and Mahmud reached Sinope from Kastamonu after Kasım's fleet had invested it and before about 10 July 1461; İsmail, the Sultan's brother-in-law and David's ally, surrendered); p. 73"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The exact date of the surrender is not known; Bryer & Winfield (1985) put it before about 10 July 1461, after Kasım's fleet had invested the city.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sinope (Sinop)", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "surrendered"}, {"name": "Kastamonu, Candaroğulları seat", "lat": 41.376, "lon": 33.776, "role": "Ismail Bey's lands"}]}, {"id": "H4-065", "date": "1461-07", "date_end": "1461-08-14", "date_text": "July - mid-August 1461", "precision": "month", "title": "The Ottoman fleet blockades Trebizond", "summary": "Kasim Pasha's fleet reached Trebizond, burned the suburbs and blockaded the city for about 32 days (Chalkokondyles 9.74); its assaults made no headway (Doukas 45.19). Mahmud Pasha's vanguard then arrived over the mountains and opened talks through the protovestiarios George Amiroutzes, a cousin of his according to Chalkokondyles. Before the siege David sent his wife Helena to Mamia, lord of Guria, for safety; in the mountains of Chaldia the Kabazites lord was still fighting.", "people": ["Kasim Pasha", "Mahmud Pasha Angelovic", "George Amiroutzes", "David Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Doukas 45.19 (the assaults make no headway)", "Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories 9.74 (the 32-day blockade; trans. Kaldellis 2014)", "Kritoboulos IV (names Thomas son of Katabolenos as the go-between)"], "sources_modern": ["Babinger 1978", "Topalidis 2024", "Bryer 1980, X ('Ludovico da Bologna and the Georgian and Anatolian Embassy', 1965) 183 (David sent his wife Helena Kantakouzene to Mamia, lord of Guria, for safety before the siege; Chalkokondyles, Bonn ed., pp. 467, 495)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 130 n. 60 (the Kabazites chief fought as pansebastos in the Chaldian mountains in 1461, Chalkokondyles p. 496; the last grand mesazon was 'Altamourios', Ecthesis chronica p. 26)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 61, 63 (Kasım's fleet invested Trebizond from after about 13 July; Mahmud arrived about 13 August, Mehmed about 14 August; Kritoboulos and Amoiroutzes give the sea siege as 32-40 days)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, pp. 64, 303 (the pansebastos Kabazites held Mesochaldia in 1461; after the fall Haydar Pasha of Amasya was sent against him and his son, Chalkokondyles, Bonn ed., 494-496; the principality of Torul held out until 1479-80)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Kritoboulos and Chalkokondyles disagree on who carried the talks; modern historians prefer Chalkokondyles' account of Amiroutzes.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "blockaded"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "route of Mahmud Pasha's vanguard"}]}, {"id": "H4-069", "date": "1461-08", "date_end": "1462", "date_text": "1461-1462", "precision": "year", "title": "Kerasous holds out", "summary": "Kerasous, the second city of the empire, is said by tradition to have held out for months after the capital fell before surrendering on terms that let its Greeks stay and keep their arms; another tradition has the fortified monastery on the island of Ares, opposite the city, hold out until 1468.", "sources_primary": ["Not identified (reported in secondary works)"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 130 (Bordier, 1609, p. 115, was told that the islanders of Ares held out against the Ottomans for seven years after 1461, which Bryer & Winfield think \"perfectly feasible\"; the 19th-century Greek tradition that Kerasous held out for many months and surrendered on condition that the Christians stay and bear arms in return for ferry service over a local river, after Miller, Trebizond, p. 107, \"presumably following\" Triantaphyllides, Phygades, p. 32)", "Samouilidis, p. 324 ('according to the traditions' Kerasous held out for many months and surrendered when the Turks let the Greeks stay and keep their arms; after Miller 1926, p. 143)", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 131 and n. 63 (the fortified monastery of the Eleousa on the island of Ares, opposite Giresun, 'traditionally held out against the Ottomans until 1468', after Bordier, ed. Chrysanthos)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Bryer & Winfield (1985) trace the tradition of Kerasous holding out to Miller (1926), 'presumably following' Triantaphyllides; its terms included ferry service over a local river. That the Christians stayed is borne out by the register of 1525, with 31 Muslim and 221 Christian households. For the island of Ares, Bordier (1609) was told that the islanders held out for seven years after 1461.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "held out"}]}, {"id": "H4-066", "date": "1461-08-15", "date_text": "15 August 1461 (the conventional date, 'of local tradition': Bryer); the documents prove only summer 1461, before 21 September", "precision": "day", "title": "Trebizond surrenders to Mehmed II", "summary": "David Komnenos surrendered the city on terms: his family, household and wealth were to be spared and he was to receive estates in Thrace. Mehmed entered Trebizond, and the Empire of Trebizond, the last state of the Roman emperors' tradition, came to an end after 257 years. The city became the seat of an Ottoman sanjak under Kasim Pasha, with a garrison of 400 janissaries in the citadel (Kritoboulos IV.50). Kritoboulos says Mehmed entered and toured the city and citadel, shipped David with his family and the leading men to Constantinople by sea, and himself went home by land 'after spending not many days there'.", "people": ["David Megas Komnenos", "Mehmed II", "Mahmud Pasha Angelovic", "George Amiroutzes", "Kasim Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Kritoboulos IV (trans. Riggs 1954)", "Laonikos Chalkokondyles, Histories 9 (trans. Kaldellis 2014)", "Tursun Beg (trans. Inalcik & Murphey 1978)", "Colophon in a copy of Petrarch's Africa saying the sultan's fleet was back by October 1461 (Wilkins, Harvard Library Bulletin 12, 1958, pp. 321f)"], "sources_modern": ["Babinger 1949, 'La date de la prise de Trebizonde par les Turcs (1461)', REB 7, pp. 205-207", "Babinger 1978, pp. 195f", "Lowry 2009", "Bryer 1980, study X ('Ludovico da Bologna and the Georgian and Anatolian Embassy of 1460-1461', 1965), pp. 192-193 n. 63", "Lambros 1905, 'Ἡ ἅλωσις τῆς Τραπεζοῦντος καὶ ἡ Βενετία', Νέος Ἑλληνομνήμων 2, pp. 324-333 (Sagundino's letter, pp. 331-333; cited by Bryer and by Chrysanthos)", "Chrysanthos 1933 (Chrysanthos Philippides, Ἡ Ἐκκλησία Τραπεζοῦντος, Ἀρχεῖον Πόντου 4–5, Athens 1933), p. 319; p. 516; p. 519", "Samouilidis (Greek popular history of the Empire of Trebizond)", "Lowry 2009, pp. 5 ('the summer of 1461') and 14-15 (Kritoboulos)", "Eastmond 2016, 'The Empire of Trebizond', in Eastmond (ed.), Byzantium's Other Empire: Trebizond, p. 31", "Cattaneo 2020, p. 110 n. 19 ('15 agosto 1461')", "Malone-Lee 2023, pp. 12-13 (Amiroutzes in the city at the fall)", "Bryer & Winfield 1985, p. 61 (\"David surrendered his city on 15 August\", Mahmud and Mehmed having arrived about 13 and 14 August; Sagundino's report posted 21 September, in Venice before 20 October, Rome before 26 October; Mehmed back along the coast, at Niksar 28 August-6 September, Gerede 17-26 September, Edirne before 4 December); p. 64 (David negotiated terms only for his family and household, Tursun; nine of twelve pronoiaroi changed sides and kept their lands)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "No document gives the day. 15 August 1461, the Dormition, is 'the date... of local tradition' (Bryer 1980), used by most modern writers; Bryer & Winfield (1985) give it without reservation, and Mehmed's dated movements (at Niksar from 28 August) fit a fall in mid-August. The documents set only limits: the Venetian Niccolò Sagundino, an eyewitness of the fall, wrote to the Senate from Asia Minor on 21 September 1461 (Lambros 1905, cited by Bryer and Chrysanthos), and the news was in Venice before 20 October and in Rome by 26 October (Bryer, after Pastor). Chrysanthos gives the range 'between 2 March and 21 September 1461'. A colophon in a Petrarch manuscript at Harvard reports the fleet, about 150 ships, home by October (Bryer & Winfield). Fallmerayer's summer 1462 was refuted by Miller from a Venetian document of 26 October 1461. A Turkish tradition, following Parmaksızoğlu, dated the conquest to 21 Muharram 866, 26 October 1461, and Trabzon celebrated it on that day from 1960; in 2017 the city moved the celebration to 15 August (BirGün, 26 October 2017). Sagundino's letter excludes the October date.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "surrendered"}]}, {"id": "H4-067", "date": "1461-08-15", "date_text": "As of 15 August 1461 (on the eve of the fall)", "precision": "day", "title": "The empire in 1461", "summary": "On the eve of the surrender the empire was a coastal strip from Kerasous to Makriali with the Matzouka valleys up to the Zigana; Chaldia's Greek lords were by then of uncertain allegiance. The Ottomans hold the coast to the west, and the Aq Qoyunlu the interior.", "people": ["David Megas Komnenos"], "sources_primary": ["Kritoboulos; Chalkokondyles"], "sources_modern": ["Bryer & Winfield 1985", "Babinger 1978"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The inland limits are drawn on the watershed; Chaldia is shown apart because its allegiance in 1461 is unclear.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Coast west of Kerasous", "lat": 40.93, "lon": 38.3, "role": "boundary vertex 1 (coast, western limit)"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.388, "role": "boundary vertex 2 (coast; held out after the capital fell)"}, {"name": "Tripolis (Tirebolu)", "lat": 41.007, "lon": 38.815, "role": "boundary vertex 3 (coast)"}, {"name": "Kordyle (Akcakale)", "lat": 41.05, "lon": 39.48, "role": "boundary vertex 4 (coast)"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trebizond, Trabzon)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.73, "role": "boundary vertex 5 (coast)"}, {"name": "Sourmaina (Surmene)", "lat": 40.91, "lon": 40.12, "role": "boundary vertex 6 (coast)"}, {"name": "Rhizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.52, "role": "boundary vertex 7 (coast)"}, {"name": "Athina (Pazar)", "lat": 41.18, "lon": 40.88, "role": "boundary vertex 8 (coast)"}, {"name": "Archabis (Arhavi)", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 41.3, "role": "boundary vertex 9 (coast)"}, {"name": "Makriali (Kemalpasa)", "lat": 41.485, "lon": 41.52, "role": "boundary vertex 10 (coast, eastern limit; approximate)"}, {"name": "Lazian ridge", "lat": 41.2, "lon": 41.55, "role": "boundary vertex 11 (inland)"}, {"name": "Kackar ridge", "lat": 40.85, "lon": 41.1, "role": "boundary vertex 12 (inland)"}, {"name": "Pontic watershed south of Rize", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 40.55, "role": "boundary vertex 13 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Of", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 40.1, "role": "boundary vertex 14 (inland)"}, {"name": "South of Soumela", "lat": 40.62, "lon": 39.7, "role": "boundary vertex 15 (inland)"}, {"name": "Zigana pass", "lat": 40.63, "lon": 39.4, "role": "boundary vertex 16 (inland)"}, {"name": "Upper Tonya valley", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 39.1, "role": "boundary vertex 17 (inland)"}, {"name": "Watershed south of Tripolis", "lat": 40.75, "lon": 38.7, "role": "boundary vertex 18 (inland)"}, {"name": "Hills west of Kerasous", "lat": 40.8, "lon": 38.25, "role": "boundary vertex 19 (inland)"}]}, {"id": "H4-070", "date": "1463-11-01", "date_text": "1 November 1463", "precision": "day", "title": "David Komnenos and his sons executed", "summary": "David was settled with estates in Thrace, but in 1463 letters from his niece Theodora (Despina Khatun) asking that a son or nephew be sent to her at Uzun Hasan's court reached the sultan, it is said through George Amiroutzes. David, his sons and his nephew Alexios were arrested (26 March 1463) and beheaded in Constantinople on 1 November 1463; his wife Helena and a young son and daughter were spared. The Orthodox Church canonised David and his sons in 2013.", "people": ["David Megas Komnenos", "Helena Kantakouzene", "Basil Komnenos", "Manuel Komnenos", "George Komnenos", "Alexios V Megas Komnenos", "Theodora (Despina Khatun)", "George Amiroutzes", "Mehmed II"], "sources_primary": ["Marginal note in a gospel manuscript (imprisonment 26 March 1463; execution 1 November 1463)", "Pseudo-Chalkokondyles (Amiroutzes and the letters)", "Theodore Spandounes, On the Origins of the Ottoman Emperors (claims the letters were forged) (trans. Nicol 1997)"], "sources_modern": ["Babinger 1978", "Nicol 1994, The Byzantine Lady (chapter on Helena)", "Samouilidis, p. 308 (transcribes the two notes in a gospel lectionary of the Halki monastery, fol. 294a: arrested at Adrianople on Saturday 26 March, indiction 11; beheaded with his three sons and his nephew in Constantinople on 'Sunday' 1 November, indiction 12)", "Bryer 1980, X ('Ludovico da Bologna and the Georgian and Anatolian Embassy', 1965) 197 (notes to the appendix) ('the death of David (in 1463)')", "Bryer 1980, V ('Greeks and Türkmens: the Pontic Exception', 1975) 149 n. 146 (Theodora's attempt of 1463 to bring a son or nephew of David to her court)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Whether the letters were genuine is doubted: Spandounes says they were forged on the sultan's orders. How many sons died is uncertain (Treadgold says six of seven). A note gives the arrest as Saturday 26 March 1463, which fits, and the execution as Sunday 1 November 1463, though that day was a Tuesday, so the weekday or the day is wrong.", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Adrianople (Edirne)", "lat": 41.677, "lon": 26.556, "role": "David's place of exile"}, {"name": "Struma valley (Serres area)", "lat": 41.09, "lon": 23.55, "role": "David's estates (approximate)"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "execution"}]}, {"id": "H4-071", "date": "1475-12", "date_text": "June-December 1475", "precision": "month", "title": "Caffa and Theodoro fall: the end of the old Perateia", "summary": "Gedik Ahmed Pasha took Genoese Caffa (June 1475) and the Greek principality of Theodoro (Gothia) at Mangup (December 1475). Theodoro had been closely tied to Trebizond (David's first wife was Maria of Gothia) and was the last Greek state in the lands of the old Perateia.", "people": ["Gedik Ahmed Pasha", "Alexander of Theodoro"], "sources_primary": ["Genoese and Ottoman sources"], "sources_modern": ["Vasiliev 1936b, The Goths in the Crimea", "Babinger 1978", "Bryer 1980, IIIa ('A Byzantine Family: the Gabrades', 1970) 172-173 and n. 38 (the princes of Theodoro-Mangup bore the name Khovra = Gabras from the 1390s; Alexander, last prince of Gotthia, lost Theodoro-Mangup to the sultan in 1475, after Vasiliev, Goths, pp. 244-268; Babinger wrongly made it the last refuge of the Grand Komnenoi)", "Asp-Talwar, 'The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos' (translation), in Eastmond (ed.) 2016, p. 208 (November 1426: Maria of Gothia, daughter of Alexios of Theodoro, married David)", "Reindl-Kiel, H. 1996, 'Gedik Ahmed Paşa', TDV İslâm Ansiklopedisi (islamansiklopedisi.org.tr/gedik-ahmed-pasa): Kefe taken in June 1475, then Sudak and Azak, and Mangup besieged; bibliography lists Aşıkpaşazade, İbn Kemal, Oruç b. Âdil, Tursun Bey and Neşrî [international scholar (German), in a Turkish encyclopedia]"], "confidence": "high", "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Caffa (Feodosia)", "lat": 45.03, "lon": 35.38, "role": "taken June 1475"}, {"name": "Gothia: Doros/Theodoro (Mangup)", "lat": 44.59, "lon": 33.81, "role": "taken December 1475"}]}], "turkish": [], "synthesis": "## The founding and the first losses, 1204–1214\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch06_frontiers.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch06_frontiers.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1040\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map from Herakleia to Georgia showing the frontiers of the empire of Trebizond in 1204–1206, 1214 to the 1340s, about 1350 and 1400–1461, with Mehmed II's march and fleet of 1461 and the battles of Köse Dağ and Ankara.\"></a><figcaption>The frontiers of the empire of Trebizond, from Herakleia and Sinope in 1204 to the narrow coast of 1400–1461, with Chaldia as a dependent lordship, Mehmed II's march and fleet of 1461, and the battles of Köse Dağ (1243) and Ankara (1402). The lines follow the frontier descriptions in this chapter and are approximate. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nIn April 1204, the month in which the crusaders of the Fourth Crusade stormed Constantinople, two young princes came along the coast from Georgia and took Trebizond; which came first is not known. Alexios and David Komnenos were grandsons of the emperor Andronikos I, who had been lynched in 1185. They had grown up at the court of Queen Tamar, their kinswoman, and came with Georgian backing. Three independent traditions attest that help: the Georgian royal chronicle; Panaretos, the empire's own chronicler, who says that Alexios set out \"from Iberia with the zeal and labour of his paternal aunt Tamar\"; and Niketas Choniates, who has David hiring a company of \"hungry Iberians\" from the Phasis. How large the Georgian share was, and what Tamar intended, are disputed. Alexios, about 22, was proclaimed emperor. His descendants, later called the Grand Komnenoi, ruled until 1461.\n\nDavid at once marched west, and by 1205 he held Sinope, Amastris and Paphlagonia as far as Herakleia Pontike. For a moment the brothers held almost the whole Anatolian coast of the Black Sea. But Theodore Laskaris was building a rival Greek state at Nicaea. He defeated David's general Synadenos near Nikomedeia, and David had to look to the Latin emperor Henry for help; by 1214 Laskaris held Herakleia and Amastris. Then, on 1 November 1214, the Seljuk sultan Kaykaus I took Sinope after capturing Alexios outside its walls, and released him as his tributary. From then on the empire was what it would remain: a coastal state from the Iris delta to Lazia, about 400 km long and seldom more than 50–80 km deep, walled in by the Pontic Alps.\n\n## Vassal of Seljuks and Mongols, 1214–1335\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch06_world.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch06_world.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1200\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Black Sea and Anatolia showing Trebizond, the caravan road from Tabriz, the sea routes to Caffa and Constantinople, and the powers around the empire.\"></a><figcaption>Trebizond in its world: the caravan road from Tabriz through Erzurum and Bayburt, the sea routes to Caffa and Constantinople, the neighbouring powers, and the battles of Köse Dağ and Ankara. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nThe 13th century was one of overlords. A Seljuk army besieged Trebizond in 1222/3 and failed, and the city thanked its patron, St Eugenios. Its commander, the Melik, was captured and released (Lazaropoulos). Bessarion, who names no enemy, tells of an army destroyed to the last man whose commander was led away a prisoner, and his editor Lampros took this to be the same siege. In 1230 Trebizond backed the Khwarazmshah against the Seljuks and lost. After the Mongols crushed the Seljuks at Köse Dağ on 26 June 1243, Trebizond paid tribute to the Mongols, from 1256 to the Ilkhans of Iran; William of Rubruck, crossing the Black Sea in 1253, noted that its lord was \"subject to the Tartars\". Manuel I (1238–1263) won Sinope back, in 1253 by Eastmond's reading of the Seljuk historian Ibn Bibi or in the late 1250s by Bryer's, and held it until 1265. He struck the first silver aspers and built Hagia Sophia. At his request a synod of 1260 allowed the metropolitan of Trebizond to be elected and consecrated at home, in the presence of a patriarchal envoy; the church of Trebizond never became independent of Constantinople.\n\nMongol rule made the empire rich. After Hülegü sacked Baghdad in 1258, Tabriz became the great market of western Asia. Its silk and spices went by caravan over Erzurum, Bayburt and the Zigana pass to Trebizond, and from there by ship to Caffa, Constantinople and Italy. Marco Polo came home this way in 1295, and an English embassy sent by Edward I to the Ilkhan bought its supplies at Trebizond in 1292. The Genoese settled from the 1280s, quarrelled with Alexios II (in June 1304 their men burned the suburbs), and made peace by a treaty of 26 October 1314. They held the fortified headland of Leontokastron, east of the walls, as their base until 1316, and it was formally ceded to them in 1349 (Bryer & Winfield 1985). The Venetians received their first privileges in 1319. In the same year Turkish raiders from Sinope set fire to the city, and in 1324 Alexios II completed the walls of the lower city. Turkmen raids on the western districts were now regular; in 1301 Alexios II overwhelmed a Turkmen leader at Kerasous and rebuilt its walls.\n\nIn 1282 John II married a daughter of Michael VIII and gave up the claim to be \"emperor of the Romans\". The Grand Komnenoi then took the regional style \"faithful emperor and autocrat of all the East, the Iberians and the Perateia\". The Perateia, \"the land across the sea\", was the Crimean dependency of Cherson and Gothia. It paid tribute in the 1220s but was lost in practice when the Mongols overran the Crimea in 1238–1242.\n\n## The civil war, 1340–1349\n\nThe Ilkhanate fell apart in 1335. Its fall freed Trebizond but let loose the Turkmen of the Aq Qoyunlu, and the dynasty broke down at the same moment. Basil died on 6 April 1340, and his widow Irene Palaiologina seized power. The archons split into two factions: the Scholarioi fortified the monastery of St Eugenios against her, and the Amytzantarioi backed her, with western mercenaries. On 4 July 1341 the Aq Qoyunlu burned the city, inside and outside the walls. Anna Anachoutlou, a daughter of Alexios II, left her convent, was proclaimed among the Laz and entered the city on 17 July. On 30 July Michael Komnenos arrived from Constantinople to take the throne; within a day the Laz had scattered his men, and he was imprisoned at Oinaion and Limnia.\n\nThe Scholarioi came back with Michael's son John and three hired Genoese galleys, arriving on 4 September 1342, and John was crowned on 9 September. Anna was strangled, and many Laz and Amytzantarioi were killed. In 1344 the Scholarioi replaced John III with his father Michael, and their leader Niketas Scholares became megas doux with almost dictatorial powers. The state meanwhile lost its western edge. Turkmen took Oinaion in 1346/7; the Black Death arrived from Caffa in September 1347; and the Genoese raided and burned Kerasous in 1348 and destroyed the fleet in May 1349. In December 1349 Niketas deposed Michael and brought in Basil's son from Constantinople, with the backing of John VI Kantakouzenos, as Alexios III.\n\n## Alexios III and the marriage policy, 1349–1390\n\nAlexios III reigned for forty years. He recovered Kerasous from Niketas Scholares in 1355; that August the duke of Chaldia, John Kabazites, took Cheriana (Şiran), but on 27 November an expedition led by the emperor against it broke in panic, and Panaretos, who rode with him, escaped to Trebizond in three days. In 1360 Alexios built the fortress of Koukos on the Chaldian frontier, and in July 1361 the men of Matzouka ambushed Hodja Latif, ruler of Bayburt, at the passes, killed about 200 of his men and paraded the heads through Trebizond. South of the mountains the losses mounted: the fortress of Golacha in Chaldia fell on Epiphany 1369, and Panaretos wrote that \"Chaldia was obliterated\". Alexios survived a plot of the Kabazites and the Scholarioi in 1363. He refounded Soumela (December 1364), founded the monastery of Dionysiou on Mount Athos (1374), and had his secretary Michael Panaretos keep the chronicle.\n\nHis great tool was marriage. His sister Maria married Qutlugh Beg of the Aq Qoyunlu in 1352, and his sister Theodora married Hacı Emir of Chalybia, whose Turkmen held the coast west of Kerasous, in 1358. His daughter Eudokia married Taceddin, emir of Limnia, in 1379. Other daughters went to Mutahharten of Erzincan, to Qara Osman of the Aq Qoyunlu, to Süleyman of Chalybia and, in Georgia, to King Bagrat V. Raiders became in-laws. Bryer called the result \"the Pontic exception\": a Greek Christian state that survived among Turkmen emirates by intermarriage, trade and a shared interest in the summer pastures and the roads. The marriages did not end the fighting: in March 1380 Alexios attacked the Chepni Turkmen in their winter quarters in the Philabonites (Harşit) valley by land and sea, and over a hundred Turks and about 42 Romans died.\n\n## Between Timur, the Turkmen and the Ottomans, 1390–1461\n\nManuel III (1390–1417) submitted to Timur, whose victory at Ankara in 1402 checked the Ottomans as they advanced on the empire's western frontier; their conquest of Canik, the central Black Sea coast, ran from 1392 to 1419. Süleyman of Chalybia challenged Kerasous in 1396/97; Turkish sources say he took it, but Bryer thought that a matter of conjecture. Clavijo, passing in 1404, put the frontier at Tripolis, east of Kerasous, and found Chaldia beyond the mountains run by a Greek lord of the Kabazites family who charged travellers for passage. Alexios IV (1417–1429) married one daughter to Jihan Shah of the Qara Qoyunlu and another to John VIII in Constantinople; a marriage of a third to Ali Beg of the Aq Qoyunlu is accepted by Eastmond and doubted by Bryer. His son John IV, who was believed to have had him killed in 1429, faced the Ottomans alone after 1453. In the 1450s Sheikh Cüneyd of Ardabil attacked the city, and John found it almost empty: by Chalkokondyles' account some 4,000 citizens had melted away into the valleys, a habit Bryer reads as the Pontic custom of taking to the forests rather than cowardice. An Ottoman raid in 1456, which carried off about 2,000 people, forced a tribute of 2,000 gold pieces a year, raised to 3,000 in 1458 (all Chalkokondyles' figures). John's answer was an alliance with Uzun Hasan of the Aq Qoyunlu, sealed about 1458 by the marriage of his daughter Theodora, \"Despina Khatun\", and an embassy to the West in 1460–1461.\n\n## The fall, 1461, and after\n\nIn 1461 Mehmed II took Sinope from Ismail Bey without a fight, marched east by Ankara and Sivas, and made a separate peace with Uzun Hasan through his mother, Sara Khatun. He then crossed the Pontic Alps in the rain, while his fleet blockaded the city for about 32 days (Chalkokondyles). David, John IV's brother, who had pushed the boy Alexios V aside in 1460, surrendered on terms in the summer of 1461. The documents prove only that it was before 21 September, when the Venetian Niccolò Sagundino wrote to the Senate; 15 August, the feast of the Dormition, is the conventional day of local tradition, followed by most modern writers, but no document gives it. A Turkish tradition, following an article by Parmaksızoğlu, dated the conquest to 21 Muharram 866, which is 26 October 1461, and Trabzon celebrated it on that day from 1960; in 2017 the city moved the celebration to 15 August, citing Kenan İnan of Karadeniz Technical University (*BirGün*, 26 October 2017). The imperial family and the notables were shipped to Constantinople. Kritoboulos says that 1,500 children were taken as slaves; the continuator of Chalkokondyles that 800 boys went to the janissaries; and a garrison of 400 janissaries held the citadel. A tradition reported by Miller (1926) has Kerasous hold out for months and surrender on terms. In 1463 letters from Despina Khatun summoning a son or nephew to Uzun Hasan's court reached the sultan, and on 1 November 1463 David, his sons and his nephew were beheaded in Constantinople. In 1475 the Ottomans took Caffa and the Greek principality of Theodoro at Mangup, the last Greek state in the lands of the old Perateia.\n\n## The rulers\n\n| # | Ruler | Reign | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Alexios I | April 1204 – 20 Feb 1222 | with his brother David (d. 1212) |\n| 2 | Andronikos I Gidos | 1222 – 1235 | son-in-law of Alexios I |\n| 3 | John I Axouchos | 1235 – 1237/38 | killed playing polo |\n| 4 | Manuel I | 1237/38 – March 1263 | Hagia Sophia; first aspers |\n| 5 | Andronikos II | 1263 – 1266 | |\n| 6 | George | 1266 – June 1280 | betrayed to the Mongols |\n| 7 | John II | June 1280 – 16 Aug 1297 | new title after 1282 |\n| — | Theodora (usurper) | 1284 – 1285 | daughter of Manuel I |\n| 8 | Alexios II | 16 Aug 1297 – 3 May 1330 | |\n| 9 | Andronikos III | 1330 – 8 Jan 1332 | |\n| 10 | Manuel II | 8 Jan – 23 Sep 1332 | deposed and killed |\n| 11 | Basil | 1332 – 6 Apr 1340 | |\n| 12 | Irene Palaiologina | 1340 – 17 Jul 1341 | |\n| 13 | Anna Anachoutlou | 17 Jul 1341 – Sept 1342 | strangled |\n| 14 | John III | 4 Sep 1342 – 3 May 1344 | |\n| 15 | Michael | 3 May 1344 – 13 Dec 1349 | |\n| 16 | Alexios III | 13/22 Dec 1349 – 20 Mar 1390 | the longest reign |\n| 17 | Manuel III | 1390 – 5 Mar 1417 | |\n| 18 | Alexios IV | 1417 – 26 Apr 1429 | co-emperor from 1395 |\n| 19 | John IV | 1429 – April 1460 | co-ruler Alexander, about 1451–1459 |\n| 20 | Alexios V | April 1460, briefly | executed 1463 |\n| 21 | David | 1460 – summer 1461 | executed 1 Nov 1463 |\n\nCounting Theodora, there were 22 rulers in 257 years, three of them women; lists that count the co-emperor Alexander give 23. John IV died in April 1460 (Ganchou 2000, from a Genoese memorandum); the date 1459 found in some books goes back to an error in transcribing a letter of Ludovico da Bologna.\n\n## The banda and the frontier\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch06_banda.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch06_banda.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1040\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the coast from Kerasous to Makriali showing the banda of the empire, its inland limit in 1400–1461, the lordship of Chaldia, the monasteries of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristera, and the fighting of 1361 and 1369.\"></a><figcaption>The core of the empire, 1400–1461: the banda (military districts) in small capitals, the inland limit, the lordship of Chaldia, the great monasteries of Matzouka, the ambush of Hodja Latif in 1361 and the fall of Golacha in 1369. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\nThe empire was divided into *banda*, military districts inherited from the theme of Chaldia, each under a *doux* or *kephale*. Bryer counted seven valley banda at its heart, from west to east the Philabonites (the Harşit valley), Trikomia, Trebizond itself, Matzouka, Gemora, Sourmaina and Rhizaion; only the Philabonites was lost entirely to the Turkmen, and the other six kept their structure until 1461 and became, in turn, Ottoman districts. The table follows Bryer & Winfield (1985); the limits of each district are approximate.\n\n| District | Where | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trapezous | the city and its suburbs | a walled city in three parts, with the palace in the upper citadel; the Genoese and Venetian castles to the east |\n| Matzouka and Palaiomatzouka | the Pyxites (Değirmendere) valley south of the city, Maçka, and the upper valleys towards the passes | the road to the Zigana pass; the monasteries of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristera |\n| Trikomia | the hinterland of Platana (Akçaabat), west of the city | |\n| Kordyle | the Akçakale headland, west of Platana | |\n| Gemora | east of the city | |\n| Sourmaina, with Ophis | Araklı to Sürmene and Of | |\n| Rhizaion | Rize | a walled town |\n| Greater Lazia | Athina (Pazar), Ardeşen, Archabis (Arhavi) to Makriali | Laz country, half self-governing; Batumi belonged to Georgian Guria |\n| Chaldia | south of the Alps: Gümüşhane, Torul (Ardasa), Kelkit | hereditary dukes of the Kabazites family, nearly independent by 1404 (Clavijo) |\n| Cheriana | Şiran, on the frontier towards Erzincan | lost to the Turkmen by about 1400 |\n| Kerasous, with Tripolis | Giresun, Tirebolu | the second city; raided by the Genoese in 1348 |\n| Oinaion | Ünye and its hinterland | lost to the Turkmen in 1346/7 |\n| Limnia | the Iris (Yeşilırmak) delta near Çarşamba | a Turkmen emirate under Taceddin by 1379 |\n| Perateia | Cherson and Gothia in the Crimea | tributary until about 1240, afterwards only part of the title |\n\nThe striking fact is how little the core changed. In 1204–1206 the empire ran from Herakleia Pontike to Makriali; from 1214 from the Iris delta to Makriali, with Chaldia; by about 1350 from Kerasous to Makriali, with Oinaion lost; and by about 1400 Chaldia was a dependent lordship and Cheriana was gone. But from 1214 to 1461 the line from Trebizond to Rize and up the Pyxites to the Zigana held. The losses fell at the two ends, in the west and beyond the mountains.\n\n## The city in Bessarion's time\n\nBessarion's *Encomium of Trebizond* is the fullest description of the city under the Grand Komnenoi. He wrote it in the 1430s, as a native who had left Trebizond as a boy, and he counts 1,500 years since the city passed to Rome. It is a speech of praise and its boasts are a patriot's, but its picture of the city is exact in detail. It survives in Bessarion's own manuscript, edited by Lampros in 1916.\n\nThe first city, he writes, was the low hill between two ravines, \"once the city, now the citadel\". Later generations carried its walls down to the sea, with a gate on each side and a third towards the shore. At the gates the ravines were walled up to the level of the road, with towers on their banks and wooden bridges that could be taken up in war. Later still a third wall was begun beyond the western ravine, carried down into the sea and brought round to the east side of the city, as much larger than the second as the second was than the first. Even so the people overflowed the walls. Suburbs spread east and west among meadows, gardens, fruit trees and olive groves, and the whole market lay outside the walls to the east: a long, winding street of workshops with stalls projecting into it, where craftsmen of every trade sat to buy and sell, and where foreign merchants stayed for most of the year. Houses rose to two and three storeys, built of timber from the mountains, which also gave the wood for warships and merchant ships. In the market, he says, one could buy Persian and Egyptian cloth, silk thread, Chinese wares (porcelain, in Lampros' view) and Cilician fabrics, and the goods of the Phasis and the Don; and grain was brought in by sea and by land, so many people lived there.\n\nThe palace stood in the upper citadel. It shared the citadel's west wall, which rose a storey higher above it, and a cross-wall gave it more than half of the citadel. A single entrance up a flight of steps led to porches and halls ringed with balconies, and to a long hall floored with white marble, its ceiling gilded and painted with stars. On its walls were painted the emperors who had ruled Trebizond, with their ancestors, and the dangers the city had come through. At its upper end stood a royal dais under a pyramid roof on four columns, faced with white stone, where the emperor received his officials and ambassadors. Beyond were a wider colonnaded dais and a painted hall where he feasted his officials and subjects, a square room painted with the creation of the world and the story of mankind, many rooms on two floors, and a church.\n\nOf the army he writes that the men set apart to fight did nothing else, but that every citizen, besides his trade, practised arms on fixed days, with the spear, the bow and the weapons of close combat, and that the city raided enemy country by land and, from its ships, by sea. Of its history he boasts that the Turks had attacked it many times and never taken it, and that Trebizond, which had once led Colchis and Lazica, now \"rules it outright\"; Bryer, as the table above shows, describes Greater Lazia as half self-governing.\n\n## Churches, learning and visitors\n\nThe empire's chief churches were Hagia Sophia, built under Manuel I, whose Genesis frieze, Komnenian eagle and Seljuk-style niches make it a statement of a mixed eastern identity (Eastmond 2004); the Chrysokephalos, the cathedral of the middle city, where emperors were crowned and buried, and which became a mosque after 1461; St Eugenios, the shrine of the patron saint; the Theoskepastos convent, cut into the slope of Boztepe, with portraits of Alexios III's family; and the monasteries of Soumela, Vazelon and Peristera in Matzouka. A tall tower beside Hagia Sophia, dated 1426/7 by Millet's reading of its inscription, has a chapel painted in 1443–1444, the last great work of art of the empire.\n\nTrebizond was also a channel of science. Gregory Chioniades brought Persian astronomy from Tabriz about 1295–1302 and translated it into Greek; an almanac computed at Trebizond for 1336–1337 from the Persian tables of the Maragha observatory survives, and George Chrysokokkes' *Persian Syntaxis* (1347) came out of the same school. Persian tables are not known in Constantinople before about 1346. The writers of the empire include Michael Panaretos, whose chronicle runs from 1204 to 1390 with notes to 1426; John Lazaropoulos, metropolitan and author of the miracles of St Eugenios; Andrew Libadenos, a traveller; Bessarion, born at Trebizond about 1403, later a cardinal, whose *Encomium of Trebizond* (in or near 1437, by Monfasani's dating) is the fullest description of the city; George Amiroutzes, philosopher and the last protovestiarios; and John Eugenikos, author of a description of the city. George of Trebizond was born in Crete, in 1395, to a family that came from Trebizond.\n\nThe visitors left short but precious notices. William of Rubruck saw Trebizond in passing in 1253; Marco Polo came through in 1295; Pegolotti, about 1340, listed the tolls on the road to Tabriz; Ruy González de Clavijo, at Trebizond on 11 April 1404, left the best eyewitness account, of the walls, the court of Manuel III, the Italian quarters and the lord of Chaldia; Johann Schiltberger left only a short notice; and Pero Tafur, in 1437–1438, met John IV and repeated the story that he had killed his father.\n\n## The numbers, and who gives them\n\n| What | Figure | Who gives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rulers | 22 in 257 years; Alexios III reigned 40 years and 3 months | Panaretos and the standard list |\n| Population of the empire | 200,000–250,000 | Bryer's estimate; no census survives |\n| Turkmen marriages | about ten, 1352 – about 1458 | Panaretos; Chalkokondyles |\n| Citizens who fled before Cüneyd, 1450s | about 4,000 | Chalkokondyles |\n| Captives of the Ottoman raid of 1456 | about 2,000 | Chalkokondyles |\n| Tribute to the Ottomans | 2,000 gold pieces a year in 1456, 3,000 in 1458 | Chalkokondyles |\n| Blockade of 1461 | about 32 days | Chalkokondyles |\n| After the fall | 1,500 children enslaved; 800 boys to the janissaries; 400 janissaries in the citadel | Kritoboulos; the continuator of Chalkokondyles; Kritoboulos |\n| The city about 1486 | about 6,700 people, 19 percent of them Muslim: 258 Muslim, 749 Greek Orthodox, 130 Armenian and 34 Latin households | the first Ottoman register (Lowry) |\n\nThe size of Mehmed's army and fleet in 1461 is not known. The \"80,000 foot and 60,000 horse\" found in some modern books is a modern figure (Freely 2009), and the \"200 galleys\" often quoted have no source.\n\n## Where historians disagree\n\nOn the founding, Vasiliev (1936) held that the princes took Trebizond before the news of Constantinople's fall arrived, and Karpov (2012), from a seal, suggests about 23 April. Tamar's aim has been read as a buffer state (Vasiliev), a client or tributary state (the Georgian chronicle; Sheard), a reprisal raid that became an empire only because Constantinople fell (Eastmond), or a bid for the imperial throne (Kuršanskis 1977). Panaretos calls Tamar Alexios' \"paternal aunt\", which Bryer reads as a loose term for a cousin on the father's side; the Georgian chronicle says \"kinsman\"; Toumanoff (1940) argues for the mother's side.\n\nWhether to call the state an \"empire\" is a matter of convention, but an old one: the Grand Komnenoi called themselves *basileis*, and westerners spoke of the \"emperor of Trebizond\", though Constantinople did not treat them as equals. Panaretos gives the factions of the 1340s names, not programmes, and they have been read as a court party against local landed clans or as rival families, with the Laz as a third force. On the Turkmen, Bryer's \"Pontic exception\" (1975) stresses coexistence and intermarriage; Shukurov (2001, 2016) shows Turks living inside Trapezuntine society, as converts, soldiers and in-laws; Zachariadou (1979) stresses Trebizond's practical subordination to the stronger emirs; and Kennedy (2019) warns that Panaretos chose what to record in order to show the empire as a respected power.\n\nThe scale of the deportation after 1461 is disputed: the chroniclers' figures conflict, and Kritoboulos, writing for Mehmed, plays it down, while the register of about 1486 shows that the elite were removed rather than the population (Lowry). Whether Despina Khatun's letters of 1463 were genuine or forged on the sultan's orders, as Spandounes says, and whether Amiroutzes informed against David, are open questions. Bessarion's birth is put in 1399, 1403 or 1408.\n\n## Legend and evidence\n\nThe founding of Soumela in 386 by two Athenian monks with an icon painted by St Luke is monastic tradition; the monastery's documented history begins in the 14th century. The interventions of St Eugenios in the siege of 1222/3 were written up a century later by Lazaropoulos, though the siege itself is real. That Amiroutzes betrayed the city is a later Greek reading: Chalkokondyles makes him the negotiator, and Kritoboulos does not name him. The rich, exotic Trebizond of Western romance is literature, but it shows how famous the name was: Don Quixote dreams of being crowned \"at least emperor of Trebizond\".\n\n## The Greeks of Pontus and their neighbours\n\nThe empire gave the Greeks of Pontus a capital, a dynasty, a court and a church of their own for two and a half centuries, with their own saints, coinage and a chronicle written in Trebizond about Trebizond. The Greek-speaking Orthodox were the majority in the coastal towns, the Matzouka valleys and much of Chaldia, and their spoken Greek was already becoming the Pontic dialect. The monasteries of Matzouka and the privileged villages around them carried this world into the Ottoman centuries and, in the end, to 1923.\n\nThe empire was never Greek alone. The Laz of Greater Lazia, speakers of a Kartvelian language, were a military force that made and unmade rulers in 1341–1342. Armenians lived in the city as merchants and craftsmen, with their own churches. Georgia was in turn protector, rival and partner in marriage. Turkmen pastoralists, above all the Chepni, who were causing trouble at Samsun in 1277 and in the 14th century took over the Philabonites valley, filled the frontier hills from the late 13th century, and some served the emperors; the Turkmen dynasties of the Aq Qoyunlu, of Chalybia, of Limnia and of Erzincan were enemies and in-laws at once. The Mongols were the overlords who made the empire rich; the Genoese and Venetians were indispensable and often at war with their hosts; the emperors in Constantinople were rivals until 1282 and patrons in 1349; and the Ottomans ended the state. This mixed frontier society is the background to the conversions of Of, Sürmene and Tonya, and to the Greek-speaking Muslims of the next chapter.\n\n## The sources\n\nMichael Panaretos' chronicle (edited by Lampsides, 1958; translated by Kennedy, 2019) is the empire's own chronicle, year by year from 1204 to 1390, first-hand from 1340, with notes to 1426. Bessarion's *Encomium of Trebizond*, edited by Lampros (1916) from Bessarion's own manuscript, describes the city, its site, walls, palace, market, trade and history as they were in the 1430s. Lazaropoulos' miracles of St Eugenios (edited by Rosenqvist, 1996) tell the sieges and dangers of the city in the 13th century as the saint's miracles. Niketas Choniates and George Akropolites, writing in Constantinople and Nicaea, give 1204–1214. The Georgian royal chronicle gives Queen Tamar's part in 1204. Ibn Bibi, the Seljuk court historian, gives Sinope in 1214 and the Seljuk campaigns. Of the travellers, Rubruck mentions the city in 1253, Marco Polo the route home in 1295, Pegolotti's merchant's handbook the road and its tolls, Clavijo the city, the court and Chaldia in 1404, and Tafur the court of John IV in 1437–1438. For 1461, Kritoboulos, a Greek in Mehmed's service, Chalkokondyles and his continuator, Doukas, Tursun Beg, an Ottoman participant who describes the march, and Konstantin Mihailović, a janissary on the campaign, tell the conquest; Aşıkpaşazade and Neşri give the Ottoman conquest of Canik, and the Ottoman register of about 1486 the city after it. The chrysobulls and the Genoese and Venetian treaties are the documents.\n\nAmong modern works, Bryer & Winfield (1985) are the standard guide to the banda, castles, churches and roads; Bryer's \"Greeks and Türkmens: The Pontic Exception\" (1975) studies the marriages and coexistence; Karpov (1981, 2007) is the fullest modern history, especially of the trade; Shukurov (2001, 2016) covers the empire's eastern relations; Zachariadou (1979) Trebizond and the Turks, 1352–1402; Vasiliev (1936), Toumanoff (1940) and Kuršanskis (1977) the founding; Eastmond (2004) and Talbot Rice (1968) Hagia Sophia; Pingree (1964, 1985) Chioniades and Persian astronomy; Kennedy (2019) translates Panaretos and Bessarion; Babinger (1949, 1978) and İnalcık (1960) treat Mehmed's campaign; Lowry (1981) the Ottoman registers; and Miller (1926) gives a narrative of the whole empire. Faruk Sümer's studies of the Chepni have not been consulted.\n"};
